/* Machine translations of Baumgarten, Metaphysica (1757), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['baum/kemp'] = {"baum:1":["Metaphysics (*metaphysica*) is the science of the first principles (*principia*) in human knowledge (*cognitio*).",[]],"baum:2":["Ontology (*ontologia*), cosmology (*cosmologia*), psychology (*psychologia*) and natural theology (*theologia naturalis*) belong to metaphysics (*metaphysica*).",[]],"baum:3":["Natural metaphysics (*metaphysica naturalis*) is knowledge of the things occurring in metaphysics, acquired by use alone. It is useful to add to this the artificial metaphysics defined in §. 1, (1) for the development of concepts, (2) for the determination and conception of the first propositions, (3) for the continuity and certainty of proofs, and so on.",[]],"baum:4":["Ontology@@1@@ (*ontologia*) (ontosophia, metaphysics, cf. §. 1, universal metaphysics, architectonic, first philosophy) is the science of the more general predicates of a being (*praedicata entis generaliora*).",[]],"baum:5":["The more general predicates of a being (*praedicata entis generaliora*) are the first principles of human knowledge. Ontology is therefore, in virtue of §. 2, referred to metaphysics, §. 1, 4.",[]],"baum:6":["Ontology contains the predicates of a being (*praedicata entis*), §. 4, which are I) internal, either 1) universal, which are in each single being, or 2) disjunctive, one or other of which is in each single being; or II) relative.",[]],"baum:7":["The negative nothing (*nihil negativum*), cf. §. 54, is the unrepresentable, the impossible, the repugnant (the absurd, cf. §. 13), that which involves or implies a contradiction, the contradictory. It is A and not-A; that is, none of the contradictory predicates has a subject; that is, it is nothing, and is not. 0 = A + not-A. This proposition is entitled the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*), and is absolutely first.",[]],"baum:8":["The not-nothing (*nonnihil*) is something (*aliquid*)@@1@@, the representable. Whatever does not involve a contradiction, whatever is not A and not-A, is possible (*possibile*)@@2@@, §. 7.",[]],"baum:9":["A and not-A is not something, §. 8; hence it is nothing and the contradictory, §. 7; that is, a self-contradictory subject has no predicates; or, whatever is and is not, is nothing. A + not-A = 0.",[]],"baum:10":["Everything possible (*possibile*) is either A, or not-A, or neither, §. 8. Now the neither is nothing, because it would be both, §. 9. Therefore everything possible either is A or is not-A; that is, to every subject one or other of any pair of contradictory predicates belongs. This proposition is entitled the principle of excluded third (*principium exclusi tertii*), or of the middle between two contradictories.",[]],"baum:11":["Everything possible (*possibile*) A is A; that is, whatever is, is; that is, every subject is its own predicate. If you deny it, some possible A is not-A, §. 10; hence A and not-A, that is, nothing, §. 7, which is impossible, §. 9. This proposition is entitled the principle of positing (*principium positionis*), or of identity.",[]],"baum:12":["If the impossible (*impossibile*) is posited, a contradiction arises@@1@@. What not only appears but is, is called true (*verum*)@@2@@; what only appears, is not, and is called apparent (*apparens*)@@3@@. Hence a contradiction that arises is either true or apparent.",[]],"baum:13":["If A and not-A are posited, a contradiction arises, §. 9, 12. If A and B are posited, and by their positing not-A is posited, the impossible is posited, §. 9; hence a contradiction arises, §. 12. The former is called manifest (*patens*)@@1@@ (direct, immediate and explicit), the latter latent (*latens*)@@2@@ (indirect, hidden, mediate and implicit). That in which a true contradiction is manifest is absurd (*absurdum*)@@3@@ (out of tune).",[]],"baum:14":["A ground (*ratio*)@@1@@, cf. §. 640 (condition, hypothesis), is that from which it can be known why something is. What has a ground, that is, that of which something is the ground, is called its grounded (*rationatum*)@@2@@, and dependent (*dependens*)@@3@@ upon it. The predicate by which something is a ground, or a grounded, or both, is a nexus (*nexus*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:15":["What is considered, but not in a nexus with what is posited outside it, is considered in itself (*in se*)@@1@@. What is not representable even when considered in itself is impossible in itself (*impossibile in se*)@@2@@ (intrinsically, simply, absolutely, through itself). What when considered in itself is possible is possible in itself (*possibile in se*)@@3@@ (intrinsically, absolutely, through itself, simply).",[]],"baum:16":["What is possible even in a nexus with some of the things posited outside it is hypothetically possible (*possibile hypothetice*)@@1@@ (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and in a certain respect, *secundum quid*).",[]],"baum:17":["What is impossible only in some nexus with the things posited outside it is hypothetically impossible (*impossibile hypothetice*)@@1@@ (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and in a certain respect, *secundum quid*).",[]],"baum:18":["Nothing absolutely impossible is hypothetically possible, §. 15, 16. Therefore nothing hypothetically possible is absolutely impossible. Everything hypothetically impossible and possible is possible in itself, §. 17, 15. Therefore things absolutely impossible are neither hypothetically possible nor hypothetically impossible. Something absolutely possible is hypothetically impossible.",[]],"baum:19":["The possible in a nexus, that is, that in which there is a nexus, to which a nexus belongs, is connected (*connexum*)@@1@@ (rational, *rationale*); the impossible in a nexus is irrational (*irrationale*)@@2@@ (unconnected, incoherent). Hence irrational things are either impossible in themselves or hypothetically impossible, §. 15, 17.",[]],"baum:20":["Everything possible (*possibile*) either has a ground or does not, §. 10. If it has a ground, something is its ground, §. 8. If it has none, nothing is its ground, §. 7. Therefore the ground of every possible is either nothing or something, §. 10. If nothing were the ground of some possible, it would be knowable from nothing why that possible is, §. 14; hence nothing itself would be representable and something, §. 8; nothing would be something, §. 14, 8. Hence some possible would be impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Therefore something is the ground of every possible; that is, everything possible is grounded (*rationatum*); that is, nothing is without ground (*sine ratione*); that is, if something is posited, something is posited as its ground. This proposition is entitled the principle of ground (*principium rationis*), which you may also gather from §. 265, 279, partly by abstraction, partly by avoiding the circle.",[]],"baum:21":["The ground of every single thing in something is its sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*)@@1@@ (complete, total); the ground of only some of the things in it is its insufficient ground (*ratio insufficiens*)@@2@@ (incomplete, partial).",[]],"baum:22":["Nothing is without a sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*); that is, if something is posited, something is posited as its sufficient ground. Every single thing in everything possible has a ground, §. 20; hence everything possible has a sufficient ground, §. 21. This proposition is entitled the principle of sufficient ground (*principium rationis sufficientis*) (of agreement).",[]],"baum:23":["Everything possible (*possibile*) is a ground (*ratio*); that is, nothing is without a grounded (*rationatum*), nothing without a corollary and a pledge, nothing at all is sterile, idle and unfruitful; that is, if something is posited, something grounded by it is posited. For everything possible either has a grounded or does not, §. 10. If it has, something is its grounded, §. 8; if it has none, nothing is its grounded, §. 7. Therefore the grounded of every possible is either nothing or something, §. 10. If nothing were the grounded of some possible, it could be known from this, §. 14; hence it would be something, §. 8, and so some possible would be impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Let this proposition be entitled the principle of the grounded (*principium rationati*).",[]],"baum:24":["Everything possible (*possibile*) is both a ground and a grounded, §. 20, 23; hence, by a twofold nexus, §. 14, it is connected and rational, §. 19, and knowable both *a priori* and *a posteriori*. Let this proposition be entitled the principle of things connected on both sides (*principium utrimque connexorum*) (*a parte ante* and *a parte post*).",[]],"baum:25":["The ground (*ratio*) A of a ground B is the ground of the grounded (*rationatum*) C. From the ground B it can be known why C is, §. 23; hence A is the ground of C, §. 14.",[]],"baum:26":["The grounded (*rationatum*) C of a grounded B is the grounded of the ground A, §. 25, 14.",[]],"baum:27":["The ground (*ratio*) A of something B, on which C depends, is the mediate ground (*ratio mediata*)@@1@@ of C (further, remote); a ground that is not mediate is immediate (*immediata*)@@2@@ (proximate).",[]],"baum:28":["A ground (*ratio*) is called a ground in a certain respect (*ratio secundum quid*)@@1@@ (intermediate) if it still has a further ground; one that has none is a ground simply (*simpliciter talis*)@@2@@ (ultimate). The grounds and groundeds (*rationata*) of something are either considered also as grounds and groundeds among themselves, and are subordinate (*subordinata*)@@3@@, or not, and are coordinate (*coordinata*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:29":["If the grounded (*rationatum*) is posited, some ground of it is posited, §. 20, 14, and a sufficient one, §. 22; that is, from the grounded to the ground, and the sufficient ground, the inference is valid.",[]],"baum:30":["If the ground (*ratio*) is posited, and hence the sufficient ground, §. 21, the grounded is posited, §. 23; that is, from the ground, and the sufficient ground, to the grounded, the inference is valid.",[]],"baum:31":["If the ground (*ratio*), and the sufficient ground, is removed, something grounded is removed, because if this were posited, that would be posited too, §. 29.",[]],"baum:32":["If the grounded (*rationatum*) is removed, its ground, and the sufficient ground, is removed, because if this were posited, that would be posited too, §. 30.",[]],"baum:33":["If A and B are connected (*connexa*) with a third C, they are connected with each other. A is connected with C, which is connected with B; there is therefore in A something from which it can be known from B why it is; hence A and B are connected, §. 19.",[]],"baum:34":["What is either posited to be A or posited not to be A is determined (*determinatur*)@@1@@. But what is only posited to be either A or not-A is undetermined (*indeterminatum*)@@2@@. That is, if nothing is posited of a subject with respect to contradictory predicates except that one or other of them belongs to it, that subject is undetermined with respect to these predicates; but it is determined if one or other of them is posited in the subject. What can be determined is determinable (*determinabile*)@@3@@. That of which it can be posited either that it is A or that it is not-A is therefore determinable.",[]],"baum:35":["The ground of determining (*ratio determinandi*) is the determinant (*determinans*)@@1@@. Therefore every ground determines, a sufficient one sufficiently, an insufficient one insufficiently, §. 34, 21. Hence if the determinant is posited, the determined is posited, §. 30, and conversely, §. 29. If the determined is removed, the determinant is removed, §. 32, and conversely, §. 31.",[]],"baum:36":["What is posited in something by determining it (marks and predicates) are determinations (*determinationes*)@@1@@. One is positive and affirmative, §. 34, 10, which if it is true is a reality (*realitas*); the other is negative, §. 34, 10, which if it is true is a negation (*negatio*)@@2@@. An apparent negation is a hidden reality; an apparent reality is a vanity (*vanitas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:37":["The determinations of a possible (*possibile*) are either representable in it even when it is not yet considered in a nexus, and are absolute (*absolutae*)@@1@@, or only when it is considered in a nexus, §. 10, and are respective (*respectivae*)@@2@@ (assumptive). The respective determinations of possibles are respects (*respectus*)@@3@@ (relations, τα προσ τι, relationes in the wider sense, either outward or inward). Respects of possibles that are not representable in them when considered in themselves are relations (*relationes*)@@4@@ (in the stricter sense, outward). The relations of possibles are their external determinations (*determinationes externae*)@@5@@ (relative, outward, extrinsic); all the rest are internal (*internae*)@@6@@.",[]],"baum:38":["If what is in A is in B, A and B are the same (*eadem*)@@1@@. Things not the same are diverse (*diversa*)@@2@@ (different).",[]],"baum:39":["The internal determinations of a possible (*possibile*) either are grounds of the remaining internal determinations in what is internal simply as such, or are not, §. 10. The former are first (principal), or essentials (*essentialia*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:40":["The complex of essentials in a possible (*possibile*), or its internal possibility, is the essence (*essentia*)@@1@@ (the being of a thing, formal ground, nature, cf. §. 430, quiddity, form, the formal element of the whole, ουσια τινοτισ, substance, cf. §. 191, the first concept of a being).",[]],"baum:41":["The internal determinations of a possible (*possibile*), the groundeds of the essence, are affections (*affectiones*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:42":["An internal determination (*determinatio interna*) which is not essential (*essentiale*) is a consequent of the essence (*rationatum essentiae*), §. 39, 40, and hence an affection (*affectio*), §. 41.",[]],"baum:43":["When an essence (*essentia*) is posited in the possible (*possibile*), the affections are posited, §. 41, 30.",[]],"baum:44":["When the affections are posited in the possible, some essence is posited, §. 41, 29.",[]],"baum:45":["When the essence is removed, some affections are removed, §. 41, 31.",[]],"baum:46":["When the affections are removed, the essence is removed, §. 41, 32.",[]],"baum:47":["All the internal determinations of a possible are connected with one another, each with each. For the affections are connected, each singly, with the essentials (*essentialia*), §. 39, and these with the essence, §. 40, 14. Hence each determination is connected with each, §. 33.",[]],"baum:48":["The universal@@1@@ connection (*nexus*) (harmony, *harmonia*) is that which is in each single thing.",[]],"baum:49":["In the internal determinations of a possible there is a universal connection, §. 47, 48.",[]],"baum:50":["The affections have their ground in the essence, §. 41, and hence either a sufficient ground or a less than sufficient one, §. 21, 10. The former are attributes (*attributa*)@@1@@, the latter modes (*modi*)@@2@@ (predicable accidents, or logical ones, cf. §. 191; adjuncts; secondary predicates).",[]],"baum:51":["Attributes have a sufficient ground either in all the essentials or only in some of them, §. 50, 10. The former are proper (*propria*)@@1@@, the latter common (*communia*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:52":["Every determination of the possibles is either an essential, §. 39, or an attribute, or a mode, §. 42, or a relation, §. 37.",[]],"baum:53":["Every possible is determinate in respect of its possibility, §. 34, 8, and hence is possible in itself in respect of its internal possibility, §. 15; and since this is the essence, §. 40, every possible has an essence and is determinate in respect of its essence. Therefore that which is wholly indeterminate is nothing, §. 7.",[]],"baum:54":["A possible, besides its essence, §. 53, is either determinate in respect of all the affections that are also compossible in it, or it is not, §. 34, 10. The former is called actual (*actuale*)@@1@@; the latter is called a privative non-being (nothing, cf. §. 7) (the merely possible)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:55":["Existence (*exsistentia*)@@1@@ (act, cf. §. 210; actuality) is the complex of the affections in something of the compossibles, i.e. the complement of the essence or internal possibility, in so far as the latter is regarded only as a complex of determinations, §. 40.",[]],"baum:56":["Every internal possible determination belongs either to its essence or to its existence, §. 55, 42.",[]],"baum:57":["Everything actual is internally possible, §. 54; or, when existence is posited, internal possibility is posited, §. 55, 40: from being to being possible, a valid inference (V. C.).",[]],"baum:58":["Nothing internally impossible is actual, §. 57; when internal possibility is removed, actuality is removed, §. 55, 40: from not being possible to not being, a valid inference (V. C.).",[]],"baum:59":["Something possible is not actual, §. 54; that is, although a certain possibility is posited, actuality can nevertheless be removed: from a certain being possible to being, not a valid inference (N. V. C.).",[]],"baum:60":["Something not actual is possible, §. 59; when actuality is removed, not all possibility is removed: from not being to not being possible at all, not a valid inference (N. V. C.).",[]],"baum:61":["The possible, in respect of existence, if determinable is a being (*ens*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:62":["A non-being (*non ens*)@@1@@ (negative, cf. §. 54) would be a possible which, in respect of existence, is not determinable, §. 61. But this is impossible, §. 10; and if it seems to be a being, it is a fictitious being (*ens fictum*)@@2@@ (of the ratiocinating reason, *rationis ratiocinantis*, cf. §. 647).",[]],"baum:63":["Every being is possible, §. 61; it has a sufficient ground (*ratio*), §. 20, 22, and a consequent (*rationatum*), §. 23, and hence is connected in two ways, §. 24; it has an essence, §. 53, and essentials, §. 40, and hence affections, §. 43, in a universal connection, §. 49. All the determinations of a being are either essentials, or attributes, or modes, or relations, §. 52. When a being is posited, its essence is posited, and hence all its essentials. When the essence is removed, the being is removed. When an essential is removed, the essence is removed, and hence the being itself, §. 53, 40. Finally, every internal determination of a being belongs either to its essence or to its existence, §. 56.",[]],"baum:64":["Through the essence the attributes of a being are sufficiently determined, §. 35, 50; hence when the essence is posited, the attributes are posited, §. 35. Therefore when a being is posited, its attributes are posited, §. 63; when an attribute is removed, the essence and the being are removed, §. 35, 63.",[]],"baum:65":["The modes of a being are not sufficiently determined through the essence, §. 50, 35, and hence are not determined in respect of existence, §. 55. A being, therefore, in respect of its actual modes, is indeterminate through its essence, §. 34, 54; i.e. the modes can be absent or present while the essence of the thing remains intact.",[]],"baum:66":["Existence is not repugnant to the essence, but is a reality, §. 36, compossible with it, §. 50, 55.",[]],"baum:67":["Knowledge of diversity is distinction (*distinctio*)@@1@@, and the ground of the distinction, in distinguishing, is the difference (*discrimen*) (difference, character, distinctive character in the wider sense, cf. §. 350, note; characteristic mark)@@2@@. Now every determination of a being is that from which it can be known that this being is neither indeterminate nor determined in another way, §. 36, 34. Therefore every determination is a difference of the being, §. 38, 14.",[]],"baum:68":["The differences of a being are either external or relative, or internal, §. 67, 37; and the latter are either essential, which belong to the essence, or accidental, which belong to existence, §. 56; or they are absolute or respective, §. 37.",[]],"baum:69":["Internal differences can be represented in a being regarded in itself, §. 68, 37, and hence can be known in any way whatever, or given (*dari*)@@1@@. Once given, we either can, even without assuming another (without co-presence), without relation to another, conceive (*concipere*)@@2@@ and understand them, i.e. know them distinctly, or we cannot. The former are qualities (*qualitates*)@@3@@, the latter quantities (*quantitates*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:70":["Things that are the same in respect of quality are similar (*similia*) @@1@@; in respect of quantity, equal (*aequalia*) = @@2@@; in respect of both, congruent (*congruentia*) _ @@3@@. Things that are diverse in respect of quality are dissimilar (*dissimilia*) @@4@@; in respect of quantity, unequal (*inaequalia*) @@5@@; in respect of both, diverse, incongruent (*discongruentia*) @@6@@.",[]],"baum:71":["Things merely similar are not congruent, and hence differ in quantity, §. 70; hence quantity is the internal difference of things merely similar.",[]],"baum:72":["The determinations of a being are separated (*separantur*)@@1@@ if, of those posited together, some are removed. Hence those are inseparable (*inseparabiles*)@@2@@ of which, when posited together, none can be removed.",[]],"baum:73":["When a being is posited, its essence is posited, §. 63, and so the complex of essentials, §. 40; hence when a being is posited, all the essentials are posited at the same time, and in such a way that none can be removed, §. 63, 40. Therefore the essentials of a being are in themselves inseparable, §. 72, 15. That is one (*unum*)@@1@@ whose determinations are inseparable; and it is one transcendentally (*transcendentaliter*)@@2@@ whose determinations are in themselves inseparable. Therefore every being is transcendentally one.",[]],"baum:74":["A one and B one, etc., partly the same, partly diverse, are many (*multa*)@@1@@. Whatever we think, it is either many or not many, §. 10. The former determination is multitude (plurality, *multitudo*)@@2@@, the latter categorical unity (*unitas categorica*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:75":["The single determinations of a being are one, §. 73; partly the same, in that they are determinations of the same being, partly diverse, §. 38, and hence many, §. 74. Therefore multitude can be given in a being regarded in itself, §. 69, and it is its internal difference, §. 37; yet we cannot understand this unless one other is assumed, §. 74, 38, and hence multitude is a quantity, §. 69.",[]],"baum:76":["Since the inseparability of determinations is the impossibility of their separation, §. 72, it is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. Hence unity is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 73.",[]],"baum:77":["Whatever is such that it is not many is unique (*unicum*) (one in the exclusive sense)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:78":["If many are posited alongside one another or after one another, they are conjoined (*coniunguntur*)@@1@@. The conjunction of several is either the same or diverse, §. 10, 38. If the former, it is coordination (*coordinatio*)@@2@@, and its identity is order (*ordo*)@@3@@. The science of order was formerly called music, in the wider sense.",[]],"baum:79":["Diversity in the conjunction of several is confusion (disorder, *confusio*)@@1@@. The conjunction of inseparables is union (*unitio*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:80":["Every determination has a ground, §. 20, 36, and whatever can be known from some determinate ground is said to be conformable to the ground (suitable, consenting, *conformis*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:81":["If, when A is posited, B is removed, A and B are opposed (*opposita*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:82":["A determination opposed to one that is conformable to the ground is contrary to the ground (nonconformable, unsuitable, *contraria*), or a defect (*defectus*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:83":["A proposition (*propositis* [sic]) enunciating a determination conformable to the ground is a norm (rule@@1@@, law, *norma*); or, more broadly, the representation of a determination conformable to the ground.",[]],"baum:84":["Wherever there are determinations, there are laws, §. 83, 80.",[]],"baum:85":["The relation of a being, as determined from its conjunction with others, is its position (*positus*)@@1@@. Where there is position, therefore, there are laws, §. 84, 37.",[]],"baum:86":["Position is determined by conjunction, and hence there are laws in conjunction, §. 85; and in the same conjunction, the same laws, §. 38. Therefore in order several things are conjoined conformably to the same ground, §. 83, 78. What is in A and B is common (*commune*)@@1@@ to them. What is in A and not in B is, with respect to B, proper (*proprium*)@@2@@ to A. Therefore in order there are common rules.",[]],"baum:87":["In diverse conjunctions there are diverse laws, §. 86, 38. Therefore in confusion there are no common laws, §. 79, 86.",[]],"baum:88":["If the rule of an order is single, the order is called simple (*simplex*)@@1@@; if there are several, a compound order (*ordo compositus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:89":["Metaphysical truth (*veritas metaphysica*)@@1@@ (real, objective, material) is the order of several things in one; truth in the essentials and attributes of a being is transcendental (*transcendentalis*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:90":["Since all the determinations of every being are conjoined (the essentials and accidentals according to the principle of contradiction, §. 40, 7; the attributes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 64, 7, and of reason, §. 20, and of sufficient reason, §. 22, 50; the modes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 65, 7, and of reason, §. 42, 20; the essentials and affections according to the principle of the consequent, §. 23, 41), and hence under common rules, §. 83, 86, every being is transcendentally true, §. 89.",[]],"baum:91":["Confusion, as opposed to transcendental truth, would be a dream taken objectively (*somnium obiective sumptum*)@@1@@ (cf. §. 593). An aggregate of dreams would be a fabulous world (*mundus fabulosus*)@@2@@ (cf. §. 354).",[]],"baum:92":["Catholic (*catholica*)@@1@@ (universal) principles (cf. §. 307, 311) are common to single beings. Things metaphysically true are determined conformably to catholic principles, §. 7, 20, 22, 23, §. 90, 80; and whatever is determined conformably to these principles is metaphysically true, §. 89. Hence metaphysical truth can be defined by the agreement of a being with the catholic principles.",[]],"baum:93":["Objective certainty (*certitudo obiectiva*)@@1@@ (cf. §. 531) is the apperceptibility of the truth in a being. Now the truth of every being is clearly knowable, §. 90, 8. Therefore every being is objectively certain.",[]],"baum:94":["If several things taken together constitute the sufficient ground of one, they consent (*consentiunt*)@@1@@. The consent itself is perfection (*perfectio*)@@2@@, and the one thing in which they consent is the determining ground of the perfection (the focus of the perfection)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:95":["In a perfection several things are determined conformably to the same ground, §. 94, 80; therefore there is order in a perfection, §. 78, and common rules of the perfection, §. 86.",[]],"baum:96":["If the determining ground of perfection (*ratio perfectionis determinans*) is unique, the perfection is then simple (*simplex*)@@1@@; if there are several, it is composite (*composita*)@@2@@, §. 88, 95.",[]],"baum:97":["Opposed rules are said to collide (*collidi*)@@1@@, and the defect arising from colliding rules of perfection is an exception (*exceptio*)@@2@@, according as the norms collide through an opposition that is true or only apparent, §. 81, and so the exception is either true or apparent, §. 12.",[]],"baum:98":["The agreement of the essentials (*essentialia*) is (essential) transcendental perfection (*perfectio transcendentalis*)@@1@@; that of the accidental affections is accidental perfection@@2@@; each is internal@@3@@. The agreement of relations is external perfection@@4@@.",[]],"baum:99":["The essentials (*essentialia*) of every being agree toward its essence, §. 63, 40, and its attributes (*attributa*), §. 50, 94. Therefore every being is transcendentally perfect.",[]],"baum:100":["The good (*bonum*)@@1@@ is that by whose positing perfection is posited. Therefore every being is transcendentally good, §. 99.",[]],"baum:101":["The necessary (*necessarium*)@@1@@ is that whose opposite is impossible; the not necessary is the contingent (*contingens*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:102":["That whose opposite is in itself impossible is necessary in itself (*necessarium in se*)@@1@@ (metaphysically, intrinsically, absolutely, geometrically, logically). That whose opposite is impossible only extrinsically is hypothetically necessary (*necessarium hypothetice*)@@2@@ (in a certain respect, *secundum quid*). The determination of a being by which it is necessary is its necessity (*necessitas*)@@3@@. Therefore necessity is either absolute@@4@@ (of the consequent), or hypothetical@@5@@ (of the consequence): the former is that by which something is in and through itself, the latter that by which something is only hypothetically necessary.",[]],"baum:103":["Possibility involves the opposite, §. 81, 7; hence the possibility of a being is its necessary determination, §. 101, intrinsic possibility being intrinsically necessary, extrinsic possibility extrinsically, §. 102.",[]],"baum:104":["That whose opposite is absolutely possible is contingent in itself (*contingens in se*)@@1@@ (through itself, intrinsically); that whose opposite is also hypothetically possible is also extrinsically contingent (*extrinsecus contingens*)@@2@@ (hypothetically). The determination of a being by which it is contingent is its contingency (*contingentia*)@@3@@. Therefore contingency is either absolute@@4@@, by which something is contingent at least in and through itself, or hypothetical@@5@@, by which something is hypothetically contingent.",[]],"baum:105":["Nothing absolutely necessary is in any way contingent, §. 102, 104. Therefore whatever is in any way contingent is not absolutely necessary. Everything hypothetically necessary is contingent in and through itself, §. 18. Therefore something contingent in and through itself is hypothetically necessary. Everything hypothetically contingent is also contingent in itself, §. 104.",[]],"baum:106":["The essences of things are absolutely necessary in them, §. 40, 103.",[]],"baum:107":["When the essentials and attributes are removed, the essence is removed, §. 63, 64; hence the internal impossibility of the being is posited, §. 81, 40. Therefore both the essentials and the attributes are absolutely necessary determinations of a being, §. 103.",[]],"baum:108":["The opposite of the modes is absolutely possible in a being, §. 65, 81; hence the modes are determinations of a being that are contingent in themselves, §. 104. Therefore they are not absolutely necessary, §. 105. The opposite of the absolutely necessary determinations in a being is absolutely impossible in the same, §. 102. Therefore they themselves are absolutely possible in the being, §. 81, and hence can be represented in it when it is considered in itself, §. 15. But relations cannot be represented in a being considered in itself, §. 37. Therefore no relations of a being are absolutely necessary; all are contingent, §. 101.",[]],"baum:109":["Since the opposite of existence in something is possible, §. 54, 55, existence will be either absolutely necessary or contingent in itself, §. 102, 10. A being whose existence is absolutely necessary is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*)@@1@@; one whose existence is intrinsically contingent is a contingent being (*ens contingens*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:110":["All internal determinations of a necessary being are absolutely necessary. For they pertain either to essence or to existence, §. 56. Both the latter and the former are absolutely necessary in a necessary being, §. 109, 106.",[]],"baum:111":["In a necessary being there are no modes, §. 110, 108. Therefore that in which modes inhere is a contingent being, §. 109.",[]],"baum:112":["Every contingent being has modes (*modi*). For suppose that no modes inhere in some contingent being; then all its affections are absolutely necessary, §. 52, 107, and hence its existence also is absolutely necessary, §. 55, which is contrary to the assumption, §. 109.",[]],"baum:113":["The determinations of one thing are inseparable, §. 73, hence the opposite of any of them is impossible, §. 72, 81; therefore the determinations of one thing are necessary, §. 101: absolutely so when they are determinations of what is absolutely one, hypothetically so when of what is hypothetically one, §. 102, 76. Things united are necessarily connected, §. 79, either in themselves or hypothetically, §. 102.",[]],"baum:114":["Necessary things are determinable in only a single mode and respect. For they are either A or not-A. A third mode, a third respect of determinability, is impossible, §. 10. Now suppose that the necessary is determinable through A; then not-A is the opposite of this determination, §. 81, and hence impossible, §. 101. Therefore there are not several modes and respects of determinability besides the single one, A, §. 77. Conversely, let things be determinable in only a single mode and respect, A; their opposite would be not-A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. Let them be not-A; their opposite would be A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. There is no third alternative, §. 10. Therefore the opposite of things determinable in only a single mode and respect is impossible, and so they themselves are necessary, §. 102. Contingent things are not necessary, hence they are not determinable in only a single mode and respect, nor in more than two, §. 10. Therefore they are determinable in a twofold mode. Things determinable in a twofold mode and respect are not necessary, hence contingent, §. 102. Accordingly there can be defined: 1) the necessary, as that which is determinable in only a single mode; 2) the contingent, as that which is determinable in a twofold mode; 3) necessity, as the singleness of determinability; 4) contingency, as the twofoldness of determinability.",[]],"baum:115":["What is one only hypothetically has determinations that are separable in themselves, §. 76, 18; hence its unity is intrinsically contingent, §. 104.",[]],"baum:116":["Transcendental unity is absolutely necessary, §. 73, 102; hence it has no opposite, §. 102, 15.",[]],"baum:117":["Since the opposites of order are confusion, §. 79, 78, and the failure of connection to occur, §. 78, 81, absolutely necessary order is found in things connected necessarily through themselves, and indeed in the same way, §. 102. Wherever, therefore, neither the connection nor the sameness of the connection is absolutely necessary, there the order is contingent in itself, §. 104.",[]],"baum:118":["Transcendental truth is order in the essentials and attributes, §. 89, hence in things necessarily connected through themselves, §. 78, 107, and indeed in the same way, §. 7, 22; hence transcendental truth is absolutely necessary, §. 117, and has no opposite, §. 102, 15.",[]],"baum:119":["The order of several things in one, whose non-connection or confusion is internally possible in the being, is truth contingent in itself, §. 117, 89.",[]],"baum:120":["A dream taken objectively and a fabulous world are non-beings, §. 118, 91, and if they appear to be beings, they are fictitious beings, §. 62.",[]],"baum:121":["The opposite of perfection is imperfection (*imperfectio*)@@1@@, and indeed 1) non-agreement (*non consensus*), simple, if among several things taken together some are not grounds of one thing: this is imperfection in the privative sense; 2) disagreement (*dissensus*), if among several things taken together some agree toward one thing, others toward its opposite: this is imperfection in the contrary sense, §. 81, 94.",[]],"baum:122":["The perfection of things whose non-agreement, and hence also disagreement, is impossible in itself is absolutely necessary, §. 121, 102. But the perfection in things that can in themselves fail to agree or even disagree is contingent in itself, §. 104, 121.",[]],"baum:123":["Transcendental perfection is absolutely necessary, §. 122, 94; hence it has no opposite imperfection, §. 121, 102.",[]],"baum:124":["Things succeed one another (*succedunt*)@@1@@ (are successive to one another) when one exists after another. The determination of beings by which they are successive to one another is their succession (*successio*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:125":["That whose determinations succeed one another is changed (*mutatur*)@@1@@; hence the mutable (*mutabile*)@@2@@ (variable) is that whose determinations can succeed one another; that whose determinations cannot succeed one another is immutable (*immutabile*)@@3@@ (fixed, invariable, constant). But the very succession of determinations in a being is its change (*mutatio*)@@4@@, and at the same time the change of its determinations.",[]],"baum:126":["The change of a being is either internal (*interna*)@@1@@, of internal determinations, or external (*externa*)@@2@@, that is, relative, of relations, §. 125, 37.",[]],"baum:127":["The mutability (*mutabilitas*)@@1@@ of a being, that is, the possibility of changes in it, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 16. The immutability (*immutabilitas*)@@2@@ of a being, that is, the impossibility of changes in it, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 17.",[]],"baum:128":["Nothing immutable through itself is hypothetically mutable. Therefore nothing hypothetically mutable is absolutely immutable. Everything hypothetically immutable is mutable in itself. Everything hypothetically mutable is mutable in itself. Therefore things absolutely immutable are neither hypothetically mutable nor [hypothetically] immutable. Something absolutely mutable is hypothetically immutable, §. 127, 18.",[]],"baum:129":["The determinations of mutable things can succeed one another, §. 125; hence mutable things are determinable in several modes, §. 74, 34. Necessary things are not determinable in several modes, §. 114. Therefore necessary things are immutable, §. 125.",[]],"baum:130":["Absolutely necessary things are absolutely immutable, hypothetically necessary things are hypothetically immutable, §. 129, 127.",[]],"baum:131":["Nothing mutable is necessary, §. 129; hence everything mutable is contingent, §. 101; and what is mutable in itself is contingent in itself, what is hypothetically mutable is hypothetically contingent, §. 104, 127.",[]],"baum:132":["The essences of things, §. 106, the essentials and attributes, §. 107, the existence of a necessary being, §. 109, all its internal determinations, §. 110, and transcendental unity, §. 116, truth, §. 118, and perfection, §. 123, are absolutely and internally immutable, §. 130, 126.",[]],"baum:133":["The modes of a being can succeed one another in it when it is considered in itself, §. 124, 65; hence both they and the being in which they inhere are absolutely mutable, §. 125, 127. Now every contingent being has modes, §. 112. Therefore every contingent being is absolutely and internally mutable, §. 126. The relations of a being are intrinsically contingent, §. 108. Therefore they can succeed one another in it when it is considered in itself, §. 124, 104. Hence they are absolutely mutable in every being, §. 127. Every being, in respect of all its relations, is mutable, §. 125.",[]],"baum:134":["Essence (*essentia*) is not mutable, §. 132; hence every contingent being (*ens contingens*) is mutable as regards existence (*exsistentia*), §. 133, 56. Hence the existence of a contingent being is mutable, §. 125, and is therefore neither an essential (*essentiale*) nor an attribute (*attributum*), §. 132; yet it is an internal determination (*determinatio*), §. 55, and therefore a mode (*modus*), §. 52. Whatever has existence as a mode, its existence is absolutely mutable, §. 133, and hence also intrinsically contingent, §. 131. Therefore whatever has existence as a mode is a contingent being, §. 109. Hence a contingent being can be defined as a being whose existence is a mode.",[]],"baum:135":["With negation (*negatio*) posited, reality (*realitas*) is removed, §. 36, 10. Hence negations and realities are mutually opposed (*oppositae*), §. 81. Both the realities themselves and the beings in which they inhere are called real beings (*entia realia*)@@1@@ or positive. Negations, however, [are called] negative beings (*entia negativa*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:136":["A merely negative being (*ens mere negativum*) would be one in which no reality inheres, and hence neither possibility (*possibilitas*), §. 8, nor rationality (*rationalitas*), §. 19, nor actuality (*actualitas*), §. 55, nor unity (*unitas*), §. 73, nor truth (*veritas*), §. 89, nor perfection (*perfectio*), §. 94, [all of which are] realities, §. 36. Therefore a merely negative being is a non-being, and, if it seems to be a being, is a fictitious being (*ens fictum*), §. 62. Since, therefore, some reality inheres in every being, every being is real (*reale*), §. 135.",[]],"baum:137":["Since every being is real, §. 136, either no negation will inhere in it, or some negation will inhere in it together with its realities, §. 10; and this [negation] will be either absolutely necessary or in itself contingent, §. 102, 104. The former is negation strictly so called (*negatio stricte dicta*)@@1@@ (negative being strictly so called). The latter is privation (*privatio*)@@2@@ or privative being (*ens privativum*). Negative essentials and attributes are negations strictly so called, §. 106, 107; negative modes are privations, §. 108.",[]],"baum:138":["No privations in a being are absolutely necessary, §. 137, 105; hence internal privations are neither essentials (*essentialia*) nor attributes, §. 107. Therefore they are modes (*modi*), §. 52. A necessary being (*ens necessarium*), accordingly, has no internal privations, §. 111, and that in which they inhere is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"baum:139":["A negative being (*ens negativum*), as such, is not positive, §. 135; but if it agreed (*consentiret*), as negation, towards one reality, it would be a reality, §. 94, 36. Hence a negative being, as such, does not agree towards one reality in the real being in which it inheres, §. 137.",[]],"baum:140":["Realities (*realitates*), as such, agree (*consentiunt*) only towards realities. For the being of what is grounded (*rationatum*) by mere realities is a reality, §. 36, 14.",[]],"baum:141":["Every being is perfect (*perfectum*), §. 99, and real, §. 137; hence its perfection (*perfectio*), as such, is the agreement (*consensus*) of realities towards one, §. 94, 140.",[]],"baum:142":["With negations posited in a being, non-agreement is posited, §. 141, 139, and hence imperfection (*imperfectio*), §. 121; hence, with negations strictly so called posited in a being, an absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 137.",[]],"baum:143":["The absolutely necessary imperfection of a being is either its attribute or an essential, §. 107, 108. But since in realities there is no sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of negations, §. 139, there is no imperfection in a being's attributes which is not also in its essentials, §. 50; nor is there in a being's essentials an imperfection which would not also be in its affections (*affectiones*), §. 23.",[]],"baum:144":["With privations posited in a being, imperfection is posited, §. 142, but an imperfection in itself contingent, §. 138.",[]],"baum:145":["There is no essential perfection without accidental perfection, §. 98, 140, nor accidental without essential, §. 98; there is no essential imperfection without accidental, nor accidental without essential, §. 143, 98; nor are essential perfection and imperfection opposed (*oppositae*), §. 81, 142.",[]],"baum:146":["That with the positing of which imperfection is posited is evil (*malum*)@@1@@; hence negations are evil, §. 142. These are either [negations] strictly so called, [and then] metaphysical evil (*malum metaphysicum*)@@2@@, with the positing of which an absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 142; or privations, [and then] contingent evil (*malum contingens*)@@3@@ (physical in the wide sense, cf. §. 788), with the positing of which an imperfection in itself contingent is posited, §. 144.",[]],"baum:147":["With the realities of a being posited, its perfection is posited, §. 141; hence realities are goods (*bona*), §. 100, and indeed, if absolutely necessary, metaphysical good (*bonum metaphysicum*)@@1@@, if in themselves contingent, contingent good (*bonum contingens*)@@2@@ (physical in the wide sense, cf. §. 787).",[]],"baum:148":["The complex of all the determinations compossible in a being is its thoroughgoing determination (*omnimoda determinatio*)@@1@@. Hence a being is either thoroughly determined or less so, §. 10. The former is singular (*singulare*)@@2@@ (individual, *individuum*), the latter universal (*universale*)@@3@@. Each, in respect of all the less determined [beings] which it contains in itself, is called inferior (*inferius*)@@4@@, and these [less determined beings], in respect of it, superior (*superiora*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:149":["A universal being regarded in its inferior, and a singular being regarded in so far as its other predicates also, besides a certain universal, are regarded, is regarded in the concrete (*in concreto*)@@1@@ and is then called a concrete (*concretum*)@@2@@. A universal being which is indeed attended to, but not in its inferior, and a singular being in which only a certain superior of it is attended to, is regarded in the abstract (*in abstracto*)@@3@@ and is then called an abstract (*abstractum*)@@4@@. The universal in the concrete is the physical universal (*universale physicum*)@@5@@ (in many, in the thing); the universal in the abstract is the logical universal (*universale logicum*)@@6@@ (after many, after the thing).",[]],"baum:150":["A universal which can be represented in the concrete in individuals alone, or which contains under itself only individuals, is a species (*species*)@@1@@; one which can be represented in the concrete also in universals, or which also contains universals under itself, is a genus (*genus*)@@2@@. Of these, the lowest (*infimum*)@@3@@ is that which is in no genus, or which contains no genus under itself; the highest (*summum*)@@4@@ that in which there is no genus, or which is contained under no genus; and, finally, those which are not highest are called subaltern (*subalterna*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:151":["The determinations of an inferior being which are undetermined in its superior are its difference (*differentia*)@@1@@. Hence the generic difference (*differentia generica*)@@2@@ is the complex of the determinations determined in the genus and undetermined in its superior. The specific difference (*differentia specifica*)@@3@@ is the complex of the determinations of a species which are undetermined in its lowest genus. The numerical difference (*differentia numerica*)@@4@@ (haecceity, *haecceitas*; principle of individuation, *principium individuationis*) is the complex of the determinations of an individual which are undetermined in the species, §. 148.",[]],"baum:152":["Singulars (*singularia*) are wholly determined internally, §. 148, and hence actual (*actualia*), §. 54.",[]],"baum:153":["Since the superior is in the inferior, §. 149, 148, there are in the individual the species, the lowest genus, the subalterns and the highest; in the species, the genera; in the lowest and the subaltern genus, the superiors; and in all the inferiors, the highest, §. 150.",[]],"baum:154":["The determinations of the highest genus are in its inferiors, §. 148, that is, in the subaltern genera, the lowest [genus], the species and the individuals; the determinations of a subaltern genus are in its inferior genera, species and individuals; the determinations of the lowest genus are in the species and individuals; the determinations of a species are in the individuals, §. 153, 35; that is, the determinations of a superior are in its inferior, whether they be positive or negative, §. 36. This proposition is called the *dictum de O. et N.* [i.e. *de omni et nullo*].",[]],"baum:155":["One [thing] wholly the same with many taken together is a whole (*totum*)@@1@@, and the many taken together which are wholly the same with the whole are its parts (*partes*)@@2@@. Those [parts] which must be taken together with a given part so that [the sum] be wholly the same with the whole are its co-parts, or complements to the whole (*complementa ad totum*)@@3@@ (supplements, *supplementa*).",[]],"baum:156":["Any part taken together with its complements to the whole is an actual part (*pars actualis*)@@1@@; taken together with others [than its complements] it is a potential part (*pars potentialis*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:157":["The whole is wholly the same with its actual parts, §. 155, 156, and hence also equal (*aequale*), similar (*simile*) and congruent (*congruens*) [with them], §. 70.",[]],"baum:158":["A being whose essence is a part of another [being's essence] is an incomplete being (*ens incompletum*)@@1@@; a being whose essence is not a part of another [being's essence] is a complete being (*ens completum*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:159":["A multitude (*multitudo*) of parts is continuous magnitude (*magnitudo*)@@1@@ (absolute, cf. §. 161) or quantity (*quantitas*), §. 75. A multitude of wholes is number (*numerus*)@@2@@ (absolute, cf. §. 161) or discrete quantity, §. 75. If the wholes, of which there is a number, are again regarded as parts, the number is fractional (*fractus*)@@3@@ (fraction, *fractio*; *minutia*); if not, the number is integral (*integer*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:160":["That whose part is equal to a whole is greater (*maius*) >@@1@@; the whole equal to a part is less (*minus*) <@@2@@.",[]],"baum:161":["The minimum (*minimum*)@@1@@ is that which is greater than nothing alone, or than which a lesser is impossible; the maximum (*maximum*)@@2@@ is that which is less than nothing alone, [sic: less only than nothing, i.e. not less than anything but nothing,] or than which a greater is impossible. A greater multitude is a comparative multitude (*multitudo comparativa*)@@3@@, cf. §. 74; a smaller, fewness (*paucitas*)@@4@@. A greater magnitude is a comparative magnitude (*magnitudo comparativa*)@@5@@, cf. §. 159; a smaller, smallness (*parvitas*)@@6@@. A greater number is comparative, cf. §. 159; a smaller, rarity (*raritas*)°°). °°) Seltenheit.",[]],"baum:162":["To be changed into less is to be diminished (*minui*)@@1@@; to be changed into greater is to be increased (*augeri*)@@2@@. Whatever, therefore, can be increased or diminished is mutable (*mutabile*), §. 125, and indeed as regards quantity, §. 160, 70, and hence internally, §. 126, 69.",[]],"baum:163":["The essences of things, the essentials and attributes, all the internal determinations of a necessary being, unity in beings, truth and perfection, the transcendentals, cannot be increased or diminished intrinsically, §. 162, 132.",[]],"baum:164":["An internal determination of a being which can be increased or diminished is a mode, §. 163, 52; and that in which there occurs such an internal determination, which can be increased or diminished in it, is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"baum:165":["The minimal possibility (*possibilitas*) is the non-repugnance of the fewest minima, §. 161, 8. The more things, therefore, and the greater things, are compossible, the greater is the possibility, §. 160, until it is maximal, where the most maxima are compossible, §. 161. Hence every hypothetical possibility of a being is greater than its intrinsic possibility, §. 16, 15.",[]],"baum:166":["The minimal ground (*ratio*) is that which has a single minimal consequent (*rationatum*), §. 161. The more, therefore, and the greater the consequents it has, the greater it is, §. 160, until it becomes maximal, having the most maximal consequents, §. 161. The magnitude of a ground from the number of its consequents is fecundity (*fecunditas*)@@1@@; from the magnitude of them, weight (*pondus*)@@2@@ (gravity, *gravitas*; worth, *dignitas*; nobility, *nobilitas*).",[]],"baum:167":["The minimal connection (*nexus*) is that of a single minimal ground, §. 166, 14. The more, therefore, and the greater the grounds, the greater the connection, §. 160, until it becomes maximal, [that] of the most maximal grounds, §. 161, that is, of the most fecund and the weightiest, §. 166. A greater connection is harmony (*harmonia*).",[]],"baum:168":["The minimal hypothetical possibility (*possibilitas hypothetica*) is that in which a single minimal being enjoys the minimal connection, §. 16, 161. The more things, therefore, and the greater, and in the greater connection, are possible, the greater is the hypothetical possibility, §. 160, until it is maximal, where the most maxima are possible in the maximal connection, §. 161; that is, [where there is] the most fecund and weightiest consequent of the most fecund and weightiest grounds, §. 167.",[]],"baum:169":["A sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) is the most fecund of grounds (*rationum fecundissima*), §. 166, 21. Yet among sufficient grounds the least is that which suffices for a single least consequence (*rationatum*), §. 166, 161. The more consequences, therefore, and the greater the consequences for which a ground suffices, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is greatest, sufficient for the most and greatest consequences, §. 161. The same ground is then the most fecund and the most noble, §. 166.",[]],"baum:170":["A remoter ground (*ratio ulterior*) is greater than a nearer one (*propior*), §. 166, 27. Hence a ground sufficient absolutely (*simpliciter*) is greater than all grounds sufficient only in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), §. 169, 28.",[]],"baum:171":["An essence (*essentia*) is least when it is the sum (*complexus*) of the fewest and least essentials (*essentialia*), §. 40, 161. The more numerous and the greater the essentials, the greater the essence, §. 160, until it is greatest, the sum of the most and greatest essentials, §. 161.",[]],"baum:172":["A universal nexus (*nexus universalis*) is least where the individual items are either least grounds or their consequences, §. 48, 167. The more, then, the individual items become either greater grounds, or consequences of greater grounds, or both, the greater is the universal nexus, §. 160, until it is greatest, where the individual items are greatest grounds, §. 161, i.e. the most fecund and weightiest (*gravissimae*), §. 166.",[]],"baum:173":["Unity (*unitas*) is least if the fewest and least determinations (*determinationes*) are inseparable (*inseparabiles*) from a single least thing, §. 73, 161. The more numerous and the greater the determinations, and the more numerous and the greater the things from which they are inseparable, the greater the unity, §. 160, until it is greatest, where the most and greatest determinations are inseparable from the most and greatest things, §. 161. Uniqueness (*unicitas*) is least when a single least thing differs from the fewest and least things by a single least difference, §. 77, 161. The greater, therefore, the thing, and the more numerous and the greater the things from which it is distinguished, and the more numerous and the greater the differences by which it is distinguished, the greater the uniqueness, §. 160, until it is greatest, in which the greatest thing is distinguished from all others, even from the greatest in its own kind, by the most and greatest differences, §. 161.",[]],"baum:174":["Identity (*identitas*) is least if a single least determination is common to the fewest and least things, §. 38, 161. Hence the more numerous and the greater the determinations, and the more numerous and the greater the things to which they are common, the greater the identity, §. 160, until it is greatest, the community of the most and greatest determinations in the most and greatest things, §. 161.",[]],"baum:175":["Order (*ordo*) is least where the identity in the connection is least, §. 78, 161. Therefore the greater the identity of the connection, the greater the order becomes, §. 160, 174, until it is greatest, where the identity of the connection is greatest, §. 161, i.e. where the most and greatest things are connected in the same manner as often and as far as they can be, §. 174. Union (*unitio*) is least when it is the least necessary connection of the fewest and least things, §. 79, 113, 161. The more things, therefore, that are connected, the greater the union extensively; the greater the things connected, and the greater the necessity with which they are connected, the greater the union intensively. Union is greatest extensively when the connection of the most things is necessary; it is greatest intensively when [the connection is] of only two or three things, but of the greatest, and with the greatest necessity that can obtain among them.",[]],"baum:176":["Since the knowability (*cognoscibilitas*) of a determination from a ground is its conformity (*conformitas*) with the ground@@1@@, §. 80, this conformity will be least if the determination is knowable only from the least ground, §. 161, 166, with the least possibility of knowledge, §. 165. The greater the ground from which the determination is to be known, and the more possible this knowledge, the greater the conformity of the determination with the ground, §. 160, until it is greatest, [when the determination is] most knowable from the greatest ground, §. 161.",[]],"baum:177":["A determination conformable to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier ground, §. 166, 169, and hence to a remoter ground and one [sufficient] absolutely, §. 170, has greater conformity than one conformable only to an insufficient, less fecund and less weighty ground, §. 166, 169, or to a nearer ground or one sufficient only in a certain respect, §. 170, §. 176.",[]],"baum:178":["A defect (*defectus*) is least when it is opposed to a determination of least conformity with the ground, §. 82, 161. It is therefore the greater, the greater the conformity of its opposite, §. 160, 176, until it is greatest, opposed to a determination in which the conformity is greatest, §. 176, 161.",[]],"baum:179":["A defect contrary to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier, remoter ground, and to one [sufficient] absolutely, is greater than one contrary to an insufficient, less fecund, less weighty, nearer ground, or one sufficient only in a certain respect, §. 178, 177.",[]],"baum:180":["The magnitude of the conformity with the ground in the determination which a law enunciates is the strength of the law (*robur legis*)@@1@@. A law that enunciates a determination of comparatively great conformity with the ground is strong (*fortis*)@@2@@; one that enunciates a determination of small conformity is weak (*debilis*)@@3@@. Hence a law is weakest, or of least strength, which enunciates a determination of least conformity with the ground, §. 161, 176. The greater the conformity with the ground of the determination enunciated by the law, the stronger the law, §. 160, until it is strongest, enunciating a determination in which the conformity is greatest, §. 161.",[]],"baum:181":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier ground, to a remoter ground (of which the nearer ground to be compared with it would be a consequence, or to which it would be subordinated), and to a ground [sufficient] absolutely, is stronger than a weaker law enunciating a determination conformable only to an insufficient, less fecund, less weighty ground, to a nearer ground (which would be subordinated to the remoter ground with which it is compared), and to a ground sufficient only in a certain respect, §. 180, 177.",[]],"baum:182":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a remoter sufficient ground is called higher (*superior*)@@1@@; one enunciating a determination conformable to a nearer ground is called lower (*inferior*)@@2@@. Therefore higher laws are stronger than lower, and the highest law is the strongest, §. 181.",[]],"baum:183":["The greatest order (*ordo maximus*) has the most common rules, §. 175, 86, and is hence the most composite, §. 88; and so a simple order, however great, is nevertheless not the greatest, §. 175, 88.",[]],"baum:184":["Metaphysical truth (*veritas metaphysica*) is least when it is the least order of many things in one, §. 175, 89, or the least conformity with the universal principles (*principia catholica*), §. 176, 92. The more numerous, therefore, and the greater the things in a being, and the more numerous and the stronger the rules according to which they are connected, the greater the truth in it, §. 175, 180, until it is greatest, where the most and greatest things are found most in accord with the strongest rules (of the universal principles, §. 182), §. 160, 161.",[]],"baum:185":["Perfection (*perfectio*) is least when it is the single least agreement (*consensus*) of the fewest and least things toward one least thing, §. 94, 161. Hence the more numerous and the greater the things, the more numerous and the greater the things in which they agree, and the more often and the more fully they agree, the greater the perfection, §. 160, until it is greatest, the greatest agreement of the most and greatest things toward one, §. 161, 169. But since the highest perfection is thus most composite, §. 183, 96, a simple perfection, however great, is nevertheless not the greatest, §. 96.",[]],"baum:186":["An exception (*exceptio*) is least when it is made from a most weak rule of perfection (*regula perfectionis*) on account of a strongest, §. 178, 97. Therefore the stronger the rule of perfection from which an exception is made, and the weaker the rule on account of which it is made, the greater the exception, §. 160, until it is greatest, an exception made from the strongest rule, hence from the highest rule of perfection, §. 182, on account of a most weak law, §. 161. If a sufficient and an insufficient ground of perfection collide, the exception from the sufficient is greater, from the insufficient lesser. If a more fecund and a less fecund collide, the exception from the more fecund is greater, from the less fecund lesser. If a weightier and a less weighty ground of perfection collide, the exception from the weightier is greater, from the less weighty lesser. If a remoter ground and a nearer ground to be subordinated to it collide, the exception from the remoter is greater, from the nearer lesser. If a ground sufficient absolutely and one sufficient only in a certain respect collide, the exception from the former is greater, from the latter lesser, §. 181. If a higher and a lower rule of perfection collide, the exception from the higher is greater, from the lower lesser, §. 182. Hence an exception contrary to the law of the highest perfection, i.e. one made from the highest rule of perfection on account of the least, i.e. one conformable to the least, is the greatest, §. 187, 182.",[]],"baum:187":["A good (*bonum*) is least when, if it is posited, the least perfection is posited, §. 100, 161. The greater the perfection to be posited with the good, the greater the good, §. 160, until it is the best (*optimum*)@@1@@, when, if it is posited, the highest perfection is posited, §. 161.",[]],"baum:188":["That is least contingent (*contingens*) whose opposite is least possible, §. 104, 161. The greater, therefore, the possibility of one of two opposites, the greater the contingency of the other, §. 160. The contingency is greatest of that whose opposite has the greatest possibility, §. 161.",[]],"baum:189":["The greater the separability of the determinations of one thing hypothetically, the greater the contingency of this unity, §. 115, 188. The more both non-connection and confusion are possible, the more contingent the opposite order, §. 117, 188. The more the non-connection or confusion of some of the manifold of a being is possible, the more contingent the truth opposed to it, §. 119, 188. The more imperfection is possible, the more contingent the opposite perfection, §. 122, 188.",[]],"baum:190":["A change (*mutatio*) is least when it is the succession of a single least thing in a single least thing, §. 161, 125. Therefore the more numerous and the greater the things that succeed, and the more numerous and the greater the things in which they succeed, the greater the change, §. 160, until it is greatest, the succession of the most and greatest things in the most and greatest things. The least possibility of the least change in a being is its least mutability (*mutabilitas*), §. 161, 127. Therefore the more possible, and the greater the change in a being, the greater its mutability, until it is greatest, the greatest possibility of the greatest change, §. 161. Reality (*realitas*) in a being is least when there is the greatest paucity and smallness in it of truly positive determinations, §. 135, 161. The more numerous and the greater the realities it has, the more real it is. The most real (*realissimum*), therefore, is that in which the greatest and most numerous realities are found, §. 161, 36. These, if absolutely necessary, are the highest good, or the metaphysical best (*optimum metaphysicum*)@@1@@, §. 137, 147; if contingent in themselves, the highest contingent good@@2@@ (the physical good, broadly speaking).",[]],"baum:191":["A being either cannot exist except as a determination of another (in another), or it can, §. 10. The former is an accident (*accidens*)@@1@@ (the predicamental or physical, cf. §. 50, whose being is inherence, συμβεβηκοσ); the latter is a substance (*substantia*)@@2@@ (a being subsisting through itself, form, ἐντελεχεια οὐσια ὑποστασισ ἐνεργεια), which can exist even though it is not in another, even though it is not a determination of another.",[]],"baum:192":["The existence of an accident, as such, is inherence (*inhaerentia*)@@1@@; the existence of a substance, as such, is subsistence (*subsistentia*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:193":["Accidents, if they seem to subsist through themselves, are substantiated phenomena (*phaenomena substantiata*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:194":["Accidents (*accidentia*) [the text reads *Accidentianon*, i.e. *Accidentia non*] cannot exist except in others. Now there is nothing different from accidents except substances, §. 191. Therefore accidents cannot exist except in substances, that is, they do not exist outside their substances, §. 58.",[]],"baum:195":["The essentials (*essentialia*), attributes, modes and relations, being accidents, §. 191, 52, cannot exist except in substances, §. 194.",[]],"baum:196":["That in a substance in which accidents can inhere, or the substance in so far as it is the subject (cf. §. 344) in which accidents can inhere, is called the substantial (*substantiale*); nor do accidents exist outside the substantial, §. 194.",[]],"baum:197":["If accidents inhere in a substance, there is some ground (*ratio*) of the inherence, §. 20, namely power (*vis*) taken in the wider sense@@1@@ (efficacy, energy, activity, cf. §. 216), and a sufficient ground, §. 22. This is power (taken in the stricter sense@@2@@, and for brevity's sake sometimes simply so called).",[]],"baum:198":["Power in the stricter sense is either a substance or an accident, §. 191. Now it is not an accident, since it is the sufficient ground of all of them, §. 197. Therefore it is a substance; and in so far as accidents can inhere in it as in a subject, it is the substantial, §. 196.",[]],"baum:199":["Every substance is [*est substantiali*; perhaps read *substantiale*] a substantial, §. 191, 196, and hence endowed with power, §. 198; it is a substantial, §. 196, 191, and hence is power, in the wider as well as in the stricter sense, §. 198, 197.",[]],"baum:200":["If substances appear to be accidents, let them be [called] predicated substances; singular ones are supposits (*supposita*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:201":["Power is attributed to substantiated phenomena (*phaenomena substantiata*), §. 199, 193, and they are also powers in the wider sense, or are endowed with powers in the wider sense, §. 197, 23. But if power in the stricter sense is attributed to accidents, these are substantiated phenomena, §. 198, 193.",[]],"baum:202":["Every substance has essentials and attributes with absolute necessity, §. 107; hence every substance has accidents, §. 191, 195. But it either has modes or does not have them, §. 10. A substance which has modes is a contingent being (*ens contingens*); one which has none is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 111. Therefore a substance is either necessary or contingent. The subsistence of a contingent substance is a mode, §. 134, 192.",[]],"baum:203":["The least power is the ground of only a single, least, inhering accident, §. 197, 166. Therefore the more numerous and the greater the inhering accidents of which it is the ground, the greater it is, until it is greatest, as the ground of the most numerous and greatest inhering accidents, §. 197, 166.",[]],"baum:204":["The science of powers is dynamics (*dynamica*), both philosophical and mathematical, [the latter being] dynamometry (*dynameometria*).",[]],"baum:205":["A contingent supposit (*suppositum contingens*) is determined as regards modes and relations, §. 184, 200. Hence there coexist in it fixed things, that is, things intrinsically immutable, §. 107, 132, together with mutable things, §. 133. Such coexistence is a state (*status*)@@1@@. Therefore a contingent supposit has a state. The state of things united is union (*unio*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:206":["The coexistence of modes with the fixed is the internal state (*status internus*)@@1@@; hence a contingent supposit has an internal state, §. 205.",[]],"baum:207":["The relations of a substance are intrinsically mutable in it, §. 133. Therefore, coexisting with the fixed, they give a state, §. 205, which is called external (*externus*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:208":["When a mode is changed the internal state is changed, §. 125, 200; when a relation is changed the external state is changed, §. 125, 207. Now modes and relations are mutable, §. 133, 207. Therefore changes of state are possible in a contingent substance, §. 206, 207.",[]],"baum:209":["The change of a mode is modification (*modificatio*)@@1@@. Therefore modification is a change of the internal state, §. 208, and a contingent substance is modifiable (*modificabilis*), §. 128. The change of a relation is variation (*variatio*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:210":["Changes of state are accidents, §. 191; hence they cannot exist except in substances, §. 194, and indeed only when power, even in the stricter sense, is posited, §. 197, 22. That power, the sufficient ground of the change, or in general of the inhering accident, §. 197, is either the substantial which is changed, or in general that in which the accident inheres, or a power different from it, §. 10, 38. If the former, the substance whose state is changed, or in general that in which the accident inheres, acts (*agit*)@@1@@; if the latter, the substance whose state is changed, or in general that in which the accident inheres, is passive (*patitur*). Hence action (*actio*)@@2@@ (act, operation) is a change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance through the substance's own power; passion (*passio*)@@3@@ is a change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance through a foreign power.",[]],"baum:211":["A substance acting upon a substance outside itself flows into it (*influit*); hence influx (*influxus*)@@1@@ (transeunt action) is the action of a substance upon a substance outside itself. An action which is not influent is immanent (*immanens*).",[]],"baum:212":["If the passion of that substance into which another flows is at the same time an action of the patient itself, the passion and the influx are called ideal (*ideales*). But if the passion is not an action of the patient, the passion and the influx are called real (*reales*).",[]],"baum:213":["The action of the patient upon the agent is reaction (*reactio*)@@1@@, and the mutual action and reaction of substances is conflict (*conflictus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:214":["The least action, passion and reaction are those through which only a single least accident is actuated, §. 161, 210. Therefore the more numerous and the greater the accidents actuated, the greater the action, passion and reaction, §. 160, 213, until they are greatest, where the most numerous and greatest accidents are actuated, §. 161, 210.",[]],"baum:215":["An action and passion which is not a whole made up of others is called simple (*simplex*)@@1@@; one which is, is composite (*composita*)@@2@@; and the more partial [actions] it consists of, the more composite it is, §. 160. Hence the greatest action and passion is the most composite, §. 214, 161.",[]],"baum:216":["Every existing substance acts, §. 210, 199; hence it has the possibility of acting, or faculty (*facultas*)@@1@@ (active potency, power, cf. §. 197), §. 57; if it is passive, it has the possibility of being acted upon, that is, (passive potency, capacity) receptivity (*receptivitas*)@@2@@, §. 57.",[]],"baum:217":["The faculty and receptivity for real influxes are real, for ideal influxes ideal, for simple actions simple@@1@@, for composite actions composite@@2@@.",[]],"baum:218":["Faculties and receptivities are either absolute (*absolutae*), §. 216, 15, or hypothetical (*hypotheticae*), §. 16. The latter are indeed always greater than the former, §. 105, 216.",[]],"baum:219":["The least hypothetical faculty would be that by which only a single action would be possible in the least connection (*nexus*) of substances, §. 216, 161. The more numerous and the greater the actions that are possible, and the greater the connection in which the substances are, the greater is the hypothetical faculty, §. 168, 160. A greater hypothetical faculty is habit (*habitus*)@@1@@ (promptness, dexterity).",[]],"baum:220":["Since, when faculty and receptivity are posited, action or passion is not posited, §. 216, 259, yet is posited when power in the stricter sense is posited, §. 210, 30, this power will be the complement of the faculty to the act (*complementum facultatis ad actum*), that is, what is added to the faculty in order that the action may exist. Hence, given a certain power in the stricter sense, it either suffices for a given certain action or it does not, §. 21, 210; the former is called living (*viva*)@@1@@, the latter dead (*mortua*)@@2@@ solicitation (*sollicitatio*).",[]],"baum:221":["An impediment (*impedimentum*)@@1@@ (obstacle) is the opposite of the inherence of an accident; hence what is opposed to changes is also an impediment, §. 210.",[]],"baum:222":["Resistance (*resistentia*)@@1@@ is an impediment of action. And since the opposite of the inherence of accidents and of changes is an accident, §. 191, 81, impediments and resistances have their sufficient ground in power, §. 197, 27. When an impediment is posited, an impeding power is posited; when a resistance is posited, a resisting power is posited, §. 22.",[]],"baum:223":["A substance flowing more closely into a substance is present (*praesens*)@@1@@ to it; and substances most closely present to one another touch each other (*se contingunt*) and are contiguous (*contigua*)@@2@@. Thus presence (*praesentia*)@@3@@ is closer influx, and immediate mutual presence, or immediate conflict, is contact (*contactus*)@@4@@. In so far as something neither flows into another more closely nor is acted upon by it, it is said to be absent (*absens*)@@5@@ from it.",[]],"baum:224":["A composite being (*ens compositum*) (strictly and simply so called)@@1@@ is a whole of parts outside parts; a being which is not composite is simple (*simplex*) (simply and rigorously so called)@@2@@. A composite being in the wider sense@@3@@ is whatever has parts; and the simple, comparatively speaking@@4@@, is what is less composite.",[]],"baum:225":["The parts of composites are either, singly and taken together, accidents, or some parts of the composites are substances, §. 10, 191. If the former, the composite being is an accident, §. 224, 155. The latter is the composite being in the stricter sense (real)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:226":["Suppose that things posited outside one another can in no way constitute a whole, that is, be composed (*componi*)@@1@@: the composite will then be intrinsically impossible, §. 224, 15. The mode of composition is therefore the opposite of internal impossibility in composites, §. 81, that is, the internal possibility and the essence of composites, §. 40.",[]],"baum:227":["Origin (*ortus*)@@1@@ is change from the non-existent into the existent. Change from the existent into the non-existent is perishing (*interitus*)@@2@@. Hence the origin and perishing of a necessary being and of a necessary substance is absolutely impossible, §. 132, 202.",[]],"baum:228":["Origin from nothing (*ortus ex nihilo*)@@1@@ is the origin of that no part of which pre-exists it; and annihilation (*annihilatio*)@@2@@ is the perishing of that no part of which survives. The origin from nothing and the annihilation of a necessary being and substance is absolutely impossible, §. 227.",[]],"baum:229":["Origin and origin from nothing, perishing and annihilation cannot exist except in substances, §. 227, 228, because they are accidents, §. 210, but not in a necessary substance, §. 227, 228. Therefore [they can exist only] in contingent substances, §. 202.",[]],"baum:230":["A substance is either simple or composite, §. 224. The former is called a monad (*monas*) (atom, perfect unity).",[]],"baum:231":["Every part of a composite substance is either a substantial or an accident. Accidents are not outside the substantial, §. 196. Therefore the substantials of a composite substance are posited outside one another, §. 124.",[]],"baum:232":["A composite substance has powers, and hence has substances posited outside one another as its parts, §. 231, 198; hence it is a composite being in the stricter sense, §. 225.",[]],"baum:233":["A composite substance cannot exist except as an aggregate (*complexus*) of other substances posited outside one another, §. 232, 155, and composed in a certain mode, §. 226. Therefore it cannot exist except as a determination of others, §. 36, 38. Therefore it is an accident, §. 191, and, if it seems to subsist by itself and power is attributed to it, it is a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 193, 201.",[]],"baum:234":["Every substance is a monad, §. 233, 230; a composite being in the stricter sense is not a monad, §. 225. Therefore it is a substantiated phenomenon, §. 193, 201.",[]],"baum:235":["A composite in the stricter sense consists of monads, §. 225, 234.",[]],"baum:236":["A monad cannot originate except from nothing. For its parts are the substantial and the accidents inhering in it, §. 196. The substantial does not pre-exist the substance; for when it exists, power exists, and hence substance, §. 198. No accident pre-exists its substance, §. 194. Therefore no part of a monad that is to originate pre-exists; hence it cannot originate except from nothing, §. 228.",[]],"baum:237":["While the substantial (*substantiale*) survives, the substance has not yet perished, §. 198, but no accident survives its substance, §. 194. Therefore a monad (*monas*) cannot perish except by annihilation (*annihilatio*), §. 228, 196.",[]],"baum:238":["Things conjoined (*coniuncta*) and placed beside one another are simultaneous (*simultanea*)@@1@@, those placed after one another are successive (*successiva*)@@2@@. A whole of simultaneous things is a simultaneous being (*ens simultaneum*), a whole of successive things a successive being (*ens successivum*).",[]],"baum:239":["The order of simultaneous things posited outside one another is space (*spatium*)@@1@@, that of successive things is time (*tempus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:240":["When simultaneous things are posited outside one another, space is posited. When space is posited, simultaneous things are posited outside one another. When successive things are posited, time is posited; and when time is posited, diverse things are posited, §. 74, succeeding one another, §. 239, 78.",[]],"baum:241":["That in which there is space is extended (*extensum*)@@1@@, and is said to fill space or place (to be in space or place, namely repletively). Now in every composite strictly so called there is space, §. 240, 224. Therefore every composite strictly so called is extended and fills space. Everything extended has parts outside simultaneous parts, §. 240, and hence is composite, §. 224.",[]],"baum:242":["A monad (*monas*) is not extended, nor does it fill space, §. 241, 230. But the whole of monads, §. 235, is extended, §. 241.",[]],"baum:243":["Every composite (*compositum*) has magnitude (*magnitudo*), §. 159, 224. The magnitude of a composite strictly, and more strictly, so called is quantitative (*quantitativa*)@@1@@. Hence a monad has no quantitative magnitude, §. 230.",[]],"baum:244":["Physical division (*divisio physica*)@@1@@ is a diminution of quantitative magnitude. Just as the logical division of an individual is impossible, §. 148, so that is indivisible (*indivisibile*)@@2@@ whose physical division is impossible. Therefore indivisibility is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 17. Nothing absolutely indivisible is hypothetically divisible. Something absolutely divisible is hypothetically indivisible, §. 18. Hence a monad is indivisible, §. 243, and indeed through itself, §. 15.",[]],"baum:245":["Composites cannot exist except as determinations of others, §. 225, 233. Now there are no others besides composites except simples, §. 224, 38. Therefore, when a composite exists, monads exist, §. 230, 233.",[]],"baum:246":["The quantity of a quality is its degree (*gradus*)@@1@@ (quantity of power, *quantitas virtutis*). Hence we can understand a degree only by assuming another, §. 69.",[]],"baum:247":["The lowest or least degree is that than which a lesser is impossible; that than which a greater is impossible is the greatest, §. 246, 161. Greater degrees are wholes composed of several least ones, §. 155, 160. Hence the inference is valid from an affirmed greater degree to the affirming of a lesser, and from a denied lesser degree to the denying of a greater, §. 157. In any greater degree there is a multitude of degrees, which is called intension (*intensio*)@@1@@, §. 159. If this is increased, the quality whose degree it is is intensified (*intenditur*)@@2@@; if it is diminished, the quality whose degree it is is remitted (*remittitur*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:248":["To be a real being (*ens reale*) is a quality, §. 69, belonging to every being, §. 136. And since in every being there is a certain number of realities, §. 136, 159, every being has a certain degree of reality, §. 246, 159. Hence this will be either the greatest or not the greatest, §. 10, 247. Now the degree of reality than which a greater is possible, that is, which is not the greatest, §. 247, is called a limit (*limes*) (boundary, *terminus*, cf. §. 350; end, *finis*, cf. §. 341); a being that has a limit will be finite (*finitum*) (cf. §. 341, limited, *limitatum*), and one that has no limit will be infinite (*infinitum*) (real, unlimited, *illimitatum*). Therefore a being having the greatest degree of reality, or the most real being (*realissimum*), §. 190, is infinite; all the rest are finite. A finite thing whose limits we either cannot determine or do not care to determine is indefinite (*indefinitum*) (the imaginary infinite, *infinitum imaginarium*, infinite in the mathematical sense).",[]],"baum:249":["Finite things have a limit, hence a degree, §. 248, and therefore a quantity, §. 246; hence of all finite things both philosophical and mathematical cognition is possible, §. 93, 22. The limit of a given finite thing, on this side of which a further 'more' in the same is impossible through its essence, is essential (*essentialis*)@@1@@. The mathematics of things not extended is [a mathematics] of intensive things (*intensorum*)@@2@@, §. 247.",[]],"baum:250":["In every finite thing some reality must be removed, §. 248, 247. Therefore some negation must be posited, §. 135, 81, and hence an imperfection, §. 142, both essential and accidental, §. 143, and hence absolutely necessary, §. 107; a negation (*negatio*) strictly so called, §. 36, 137, a metaphysical evil (*malum metaphysicum*), §. 146.",[]],"baum:251":["The greatest degree of reality of the infinite is absolutely necessary, §. 248, 102, and hence absolutely immutable, §. 130.",[]],"baum:252":["If the infinite being were internally mutable, the determinations that succeeded others would change the degree of reality, §. 246, 125. But this degree is internally immutable, §. 251. Therefore the infinite being is internally immutable.",[]],"baum:253":["What is internally immutable is, in respect of its internal determinations, actually whatever it can be, §. 125. Therefore the infinite being, in respect of its internal determinations, is actually whatever it can be, §. 252.",[]],"baum:254":["What is not actually, in respect of its internal determinations, whatever it can be, is a finite being, §. 253, 248.",[]],"baum:255":["What is internally mutable is a finite being, §. 252, 126. Therefore every contingent being is finite, §. 133, although it may in many respects be indefinite and mathematically infinite, §. 248.",[]],"baum:256":["The infinite being is a necessary being, §. 255, 109.",[]],"baum:257":["That which has a degree of reality than which a greater is possible has a certain quality, §. 248, and hence an internal determination which can be intensified, §. 247, 69, and hence changed, §. 247, 162. Therefore a finite being is internally mutable, §. 248, 126, and hence is not a necessary being, §. 132; its existence is a mode, §. 134, and it is itself a contingent being, §. 109.",[]],"baum:258":["A necessary being is infinite, §. 257, 248.",[]],"baum:259":["That which is actually, in respect of its internal determinations, whatever it can be, is a necessary being, §. 132, and therefore infinite, §. 258. Hence the infinite being can be defined as the being which is actually, in respect of its internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 253.",[]],"baum:260":["A contingent being is not actually, in respect of its internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 133. A finite being is a contingent being, §. 257. Therefore a finite being can be defined as a being which is not actually, in respect of its internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 254. This is also evident from the fact that, of contradictorily opposed things, when one is defined affirmatively, the other can be defined by the same marks, but negative ones, §. 81, 248.",[]],"baum:261":["Infinity (*infinitudo*) is a reality, §. 36, whose ground is the greatest degree of reality, §. 248, 14; finitude (*finitudo*), or limitation (*limitatio*), is a negation, §. 36, whose ground is a limit, §. 248, 14.",[]],"baum:262":["The modes and relations of a being are either realities or negations, §. 36; hence, when they are changed in a finite being, its limit is changed, §. 248. Now every change of a finite being is a change of mode or relation, §. 52, 132. Therefore every change of a finite being is a change of its limits, and hence also of its limitation, §. 261, 30.",[]],"baum:263":["A finite being has modes, §. 257, 112. These are either realities or negations, §. 36. The opposite of each is absolutely possible in a finite being, §. 108. Hence privations are absolutely possible in every finite being, §. 137. Hence also imperfection and contingent evil, §. 144, 146 (physical evil, taken broadly).",[]],"baum:264":["Every finite being is partly evil, partly good, §. 147, 137, and in every finite thing contingent good and evil are intrinsically possible, §. 147, 263.",[]],"baum:265":["The least similarity (*similitudo*) is in two things in which a single least quality is common, §. 174, 70. Now in all beings certain qualities are common, §. 8-100. Therefore all beings are similar to one another in some degree, §. 246. There is hence among beings a certain similarity, and thus a universal identity, §. 70. The more numerous and the greater the qualities, and the more things to which they are common, the greater is the similarity, §. 174, 70. The least equality (*aequalitas*) is in two things in which a single least quantity is common; therefore the more numerous the common quantities, the more things in which they are common, and the greater they are, the greater is the equality, §. 174, 70.",[]],"baum:266":["The least congruence (*congruentia*) is the least similarity and equality together; the greater these are at once, the greater is the congruence, §. 265, 70. The identity, similarity, equality and congruence of essentials are called essential (*essentiales*)@@1@@, those of essentials and attributes necessary (*necessariae*)@@2@@, those of modes contingent, and those of affections accidental (*accidentales*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:267":["Identity and diversity, in respect of each single predicate, are total (*totalis*)@@1@@; in respect of some, partial (*partialis*)@@2@@. Therefore similarity, equality and congruence are either total or partial, §. 70.",[]],"baum:268":["Since there is a partial similarity of all beings, §. 265, beings are not totally diverse, §. 267. Let us call this proposition the principle of denied total dissimilarity and diversity.",[]],"baum:269":["The total identity of singulars is numerical (*numerica*). Two singulars outside one another that are wholly or totally the same are impossible. For when two are posited, many are posited, hence partly the same and partly diverse, §. 74. Therefore they are not totally the same, §. 267. Singulars that are totally the same are the same in number, and not partly the same and partly diverse, §. 267. Hence they are not many, nor two, §. 74. This proposition is called the principle of indiscernibles (of identity), taken broadly, or the principle of denied total identity.",[]],"baum:270":["Several actual things outside one another that are totally congruent are impossible. For while they existed outside one another, to each there would belong its own and proper existence, §. 86, different from the existence of the other, §. 38; hence either an attribute or a mode, at least a single one, §. 77, would be in the one which would not be in the other, §. 38, 55, and thus either a quality or a quantity, §. 69, nor would both therefore be totally congruent, §. 70, 267. Things totally congruent, in respect of all internal differences, are the same, §. 267, 70, hence the existence of one is not different from the existence of the other, §. 70, and therefore they do not exist outside one another. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total congruence.",[]],"baum:271":["Several actual things outside one another (*extra se invicem actualia*) that are wholly similar (*totaliter similia*) are impossible. For they would be either also wholly equal (*totaliter aequalia*), or less so. If the former, they would be wholly congruent (*totaliter congruentia*), §. 70, 267, q.a., §. 270. If they were not wholly equal, there would be in one a quantity that would not be in the other, §. 70, 38. This would have a sufficient reason (*ratio sufficiens*), §. 22. Hence there would be in one of the wholly similar things a quality that would not be in the other, §. 69, 14, q.a., §. 70, 267. This proposition is the principle (of identity) of indiscernibles (*principium indiscernibilium*), taken strictly, or the principle of denied total similarity.",[]],"baum:272":["Several actual things outside one another (*extra se invicem actualia*) that are wholly equal (*totaliter aequalia*) are impossible. For they will be either at the same time wholly similar (*totaliter similia*), or only partially so, §. 265. If the former, they would be wholly congruent (*totaliter congruentia*), §. 70, 267, q.a., §. 270. If they were only partially similar, there would be a quality in one that would not be in the other, §. 267, 70. Hence the degree of reality (*gradus realitatis*) in both would not be wholly the same, §. 248, and so a certain quantity of the one would not be a quantity of the other, §. 246. Therefore they would not be wholly equal either, §. 267, 70. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total equality.",[]],"baum:273":["All actual things outside one another (*extra se invicem actualia*) are partially diverse (*partialiter diversa*), §. 268, non-congruent (*discongruentia*), §. 270, 70, dissimilar (*dissimilia*), §. 271, unequal (*inaequalia*), §. 272.",[]],"baum:274":["Things A and C that are the same as a third thing B are the same as each other. For what is in B is in A, §. 38. What is in C is in B, §. 38. Therefore what is in C is in A. Hence A and C are the same, §. 38.",[]],"baum:275":["Things congruent (*congruentia*), similar (*similia*), equal (*aequalia*) to a third are congruent, similar, equal to each other, §. 70, 274.",[]],"baum:276":["When the same, congruent, similar, equal sufficient reason (*ratio sufficiens*) is posited, the same, congruent, similar, equal thing grounded (*rationatum*) is posited, and conversely, §. 38, 70.",[]],"baum:277":["When the same essence (*essentia*) is posited, the same attributes (*attributa*) are posited, and conversely, §. 276, 50.",[]],"baum:278":["When a diverse, non-congruent, dissimilar, unequal sufficient reason (*ratio sufficiens*) is posited, a diverse, dissimilar, non-congruent, unequal thing grounded (*rationatum*) is posited, and conversely. Hence when a diverse essence is posited, diverse attributes are posited, and conversely, §. 277, 276.",[]],"baum:279":["Identity (*identitas*), diversity (*diversitas*), §. 38, congruence (*congruentia*), non-congruence (*discongruentia*), similarity (*similitudo*), dissimilarity (*dissimilitudo*), equality (*aequalitas*), inequality (*inaequalitas*), §. 70, of actual things outside one another (*extra se invicem actualium*) cannot be represented in any one of them unless it is regarded in connection with the things that are posited outside it, §. 14. Hence they are relations (*relationes*), §. 37. Therefore those things between which they obtain are connected, §. 14, 19. Now they obtain between each of the actual things outside one another, §. 265-273. Therefore each of the actual things is connected, §. 47. Hence in all actual things there is a universal nexus (*nexus*), indeed a universal harmony (*harmonia*), §. 48, 167.",[]],"baum:280":["Since extension (*extensio*) is a quality of a composite being by which it fills space (*spatium*), §. 69, 241, and space can be greater and smaller, §. 239, 175, a degree of extension is possible in what is extended, §. 246, i.e. figure (*figura*). Hence a monad does not admit figure, but a whole of monads does, §. 242. A composite of greater extension is spacious@@1@@, of lesser extension narrow@@2@@.",[]],"baum:281":["The position (*positus*) of a simultaneous thing outside other actual things is its place (*locus*)@@1@@; the position of a successive thing is its age (*aetas*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:282":["The place (*locus*) of any simultaneous thing is determined by its conjunction with the diverse actual things outside it, §. 85, 281. Hence it is diverse from the place of things that coexist, §. 38. Therefore simultaneous things posited outside one another are not in the same place, §. 281. The age (*aetas*) of any successive thing is determined by its conjunction with the actual things posited before and after it, §. 85, 78. Hence it is diverse from their age, §. 38. Hence successive things cannot be in the same age with one another.",[]],"baum:283":["A change of place is motion (*motus*)@@1@@. Hence all motion is a change of relation, §. 281, 85, and it would be the smallest if only a single position of a single smallest actual thing relative to a single smallest thing outside it were changed, §. 161. Hence it is the greater, the more numerous the things, the more numerous the things whose position, the greater the things whose position, is changed relative to the more numerous, the greater things outside the movable things. Things that are not moved are at rest (*quiescunt*)@@2@@, and the absence of motion is rest (*quies*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:284":["Coordinate things that touch each other are contiguous (*contigua*)@@1@@; those not contiguous are distant (*distant*)@@2@@. The place of things distant from one another is their situation (*situs*)@@3@@. Action, or immediate and proximate influx (*influxus*), on a distant thing is impossible in and through itself, §. 223, 15.",[]],"baum:285":["A being simultaneous and successive, of contiguous parts, is continuous (*continuum*)@@1@@; but a being simultaneous and successive, of parts distant from one another, is interrupted (*interruptum*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:286":["The parts of an extended thing (*extensum*) posited outside one another are either simple or composite, §. 224. The former, insofar as they are not extended, §. 242, are called points (*puncta*). A continuous series of points interposed between distant points is a line (*linea*).",[]],"baum:287":["The extension of a line is determined by the number of points of which it consists, §. 241, 286, and the line in which the fewest points are interposed between given distant points is the shortest of these lines between them (*brevissima*)@@1@@. The shortest line between given points is straight (*recta*)@@2@@; if not straight, it is curved (*curva*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:288":["A straight line between distant things is their distance (*distantia*)@@1@@, and the greater this is, the more the distant things are distant from each other, §. 287, 284. If the distance is greater, the distant things are more remote (*remotiora*)@@2@@; if smaller, nearer (*propiora*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:289":["A continuous series of lines interposed between distant lines is a surface (*superficies*)@@1@@. The extension of a surface is determined by the number of lines of which it consists, §. 241, 175, and the shortest surface between given distant lines is a plane surface (*superficies plana*)@@2@@; if not plane, it is curved (*curva*) (convex)@@3@@. A continuous series of surfaces interposed between distant surfaces is the mathematically solid (*mathematice solidum*)@@4@@ (the mathematical body, *corpus*, cf. §. 296).",[]],"baum:290":["Extension possible in a line alone is length (*longitudo*)@@1@@; what is added to length in a surface is breadth (*latitudo*)@@2@@; what is added to the extension of surfaces in mathematical solids is (height, *altitudo*) or depth (*profunditas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:291":["If from a quantity taken as one we understand another homogeneous, or similar, quantity, we measure (*metimur*)@@3@@ this, the measured (*mensuratum*)@@1@@, by that measure (*mensura*)@@2@@, and this very action is called dimension (*dimensio*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:292":["A line admits only a single dimension of extension, §. 291, 287; a surface a double, §. 291, 289; the mathematically solid a triple, §. 280, 246.",[]],"baum:293":["Since the parts of space (*spatium*) are in simultaneous things posited outside one another, §. 239, 155, they are simultaneous, and space is therefore a simultaneous being, §. 238.",[]],"baum:294":["Since motion (*motus*) is an accident (*accidens*), §. 283, it cannot exist except in substances (*substantiae*), and indeed when a power (*vis*) is posited, §. 210, which is called motive power (*vis motrix*)@@1@@. And since rest is an impediment of motion, §. 221, 283, when rest is posited, a power resisting motion is posited, §. 222, which is called the power of inertia (*vis inertiae*)@@2@@ (resistance to impact, sluggishness, innate power).",[]],"baum:295":["An extended thing (*extensum*) to which the power of inertia (*vis inertiae*) is ascribed is matter (*materia*), cf. §. 344, and a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 234, 201. Matter to which this power alone is ascribed is first matter (*materia prima*), cf. §. 423 (merely passive).",[]],"baum:296":["Matter to which motive power (*vis motrix*) is ascribed is a physical body (*corpus physicum*) (cf. §. 289; second matter, *materia secunda*, cf. §. 295) and a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 295, 201.",[]],"baum:297":["The time simultaneous with the thought of it is the present (*praesens*)@@1@@; what the present succeeds is the past (*praeteritum*)@@2@@; what is successive to the present is the future (*futurum*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:298":["Actual things (*actualia*) of the present time are existent things (*exsistentia*)@@1@@ (present in time, beings in act, *entia actu*). Actual things of past time, if they are not at the same time beings in act, or if they no longer exist, are past things (*praeterita*)@@2@@ (matters of fact, *res facti*). Actual things of future time are future things (*futura*)@@3@@, and these, if they are not at the same time beings in act, are beings in potency (*entia in potentia*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:299":["The continuation of existence is duration (*duratio*)@@1@@. That in which duration is possible is called enduring (*perdurabile*)@@2@@. But an actual thing that is not enduring is instantaneous (*instantaneum*)@@3@@ (momentary). An enduring thing of greater duration is lasting (*diuturnum*)@@4@@ (stable, constant, perpetual, eternal); one of lesser duration is brief (*breve*)@@5@@ (fleeting, transitory, inconstant).",[]],"baum:300":["The time simultaneous with a momentary thing is called an instant (*instans*) (moment, *momentum*)@@1@@. That which is successive to others is later (*posterius*)@@2@@; that to which other things are successive is earlier (*prius*)@@3@@; what is earlier than all is first (*primum*)@@4@@; what is later than all is last (*postremum*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:301":["The change of a being into the present is its beginning (*initium*)@@1@@; into the past, its end (*finis*)@@2@@. Uniting (*unitio*) is the beginning of union, §. 79, 205; the beginning of contact is impact (*ictus*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:302":["Eternity (*aeternitas*)@@1@@ (strictly speaking, cf. §. 299) is duration without beginning and end. Duration without end only is everlastingness (*aeviternitas*)@@2@@, and that which is simultaneous with all time is sempiternity (*sempiternitas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:303":["A thing continuing its existence without beginning is simultaneous with all past and present time, §. 301, 297; one that will continue the same without end will be simultaneous with all future time, §. 301, 297. Therefore everything eternal is sempiternal, §. 302.",[]],"baum:304":["A being in potency (*ens in potentia*) either has the proximate sufficient reason of its existence in beings in act, or does not, §. 27, 10. The former is a being in proximate potency (*ens in potentia proxima*)@@1@@, the latter a being in remote potency (*ens in potentia remota*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:305":["A being in act (*ens actu*), while it endures, cannot fail to endure, §. 9, 7. Hence it exists necessarily, §. 299, 101; or, whatever is, while it is, is necessarily.",[]],"baum:306":["Things that mutually determine place and age for one another are connected, §. 281, 85. Hence simultaneous things are connected insofar as they are in space, successive things insofar as they are in time, §. 238, 239. Now each of the actual things posited outside one another is either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 298. Therefore between each of the actual things there obtains a universal nexus and harmony (*nexus et harmonia universalis*), §. 48, 167.",[]],"baum:307":["That which contains the reason of another is its principle (*principium*)@@1@@. What depends on a principle is derived from a principle (*principiatum*)@@2@@. The principle of existence is a cause (*causa*)@@3@@, and what is derived from a cause is caused (*causatum*)@@4@@. That which cannot exist except as caused by another posited outside itself is a being from another (*ens ab alio*)@@5@@ (dependent); that which can also exist even if it is not caused by another posited outside itself is a being from itself (*ens a se*)@@6@@ (independent).",[]],"baum:308":["Existence is a mode (*modus*) of a contingent being (*ens contingens*), and hence of a finite one, §. 134, 257. Hence it is not sufficiently determined by the essence of a contingent and finite being, §. 65. Therefore it is not so determined by its attributes either, §. 64, 25. Therefore in the internal determinations of a contingent and finite being there is no sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of its existence, §. 52. But such a ground is nevertheless necessary for a contingent and finite being, if it is to exist, §. 22, 101. Therefore a sufficient ground of its existence, that it be, must lie outside the contingent and finite being. And since what contain [such a ground] are causes, §. 307, a finite and contingent being cannot exist except as the effect (*causatum*) of something posited outside itself, i.e. it is a being from another (*ens ab alio*), §. 307.",[]],"baum:309":["A being from itself (*ens a se*) is neither contingent nor finite, §. 308, 307, and hence is necessary, §. 109, and infinite, §. 258.",[]],"baum:310":["A necessary and infinite being, §. 258, can exist even though it is not the effect of another posited outside itself, §. 109, 102. Therefore it is a being from itself and independent, §. 307.",[]],"baum:311":["The principle of possibility is called the principle of being (*principium essendi*)@@1@@ (of composition); the cause is called the principle of becoming (*principium fiendi*)@@2@@ (of generation); the principle of cognition (*principium cognitionis*) is called the principle of knowing (*principium cognoscendi*)@@3@@. And if from it neither more nor fewer things can be known than the given thing grounded (*principiatum*), it is called adequate (*adaequatum*)@@4@@ to that thing grounded. Essence is the principle of being and of knowing the modes, §. 65, 50.",[]],"baum:312":["The principle of things related (*relata*) is called the term of the relation (*terminus relationis*)@@1@@, but that which is dependent is called the subject of the relation (*subiectum relationis*)@@2@@. Things of which either can become the term of the relation of the other are correlates (*correlata*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:313":["Between cause and effect there is a connexion (*nexus*), §. 307, 14, which is called causal (*causalis*)@@1@@, and, in so far as it is ascribed to the cause, causality (*causalitas*)@@2@@, in so far as it is ascribed to the effect, dependence (*dependentia*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:314":["Several causes of one and the same effect are joint causes (*concausae*)@@1@@, and are said to concur (*concurrere*)@@2@@ in the effect. A cause which has no joint cause is solitary (*solitaria*)@@3@@. Of the joint causes, that which contains the greatest part of the ground of the effect among the rest is the principal (*principalis*, or primary)@@4@@. The joint causes of the principal cause are (less principal, i.e.) secondary (*secundariae*)@@5@@. All joint causes are connected with one another, §. 313, 33.",[]],"baum:315":["Of joint causes, one is either the cause of another, or not, §. 10. In the former case the joint causes are subordinate (*subordinatae*)@@1@@, in the latter coordinate (*coordinatae*)@@2@@. Of all subordinate causes, one is the first cause (*causa prima*)@@3@@, the rest are second (*secundae*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:316":["In subordinate joint causes, either in the nearer cause the same thing will depend on the more remote cause on which the effect of the nearer depends, or something else, §. 315, 27. If the former, the joint causes are subordinated essentially (*essentialiter*)@@1@@; if the latter, accidentally (*accidentaliter*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:317":["The cause of a cause that is essentially subordinated to it is also the cause of the effect, §. 316, 25.",[]],"baum:318":["A cause is either sufficient (*sufficiens*) or insufficient (*insufficiens*), §. 307, 21, and the latter is such either in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) or without qualification (*simpliciter*), §. 28. A cause is either mediate (*mediata*), on which a given effect depends through another, or immediate (*immediata*), on which a given effect depends, but not through another, §. 27.",[]],"baum:319":["The cause of reality through action is efficient (*efficiens*)@@1@@, but the cause of negation is deficient (*deficiens*)@@2@@. Both efficient and deficient causes act, and hence are substances, §. 210; and accidents, regarded as efficient causes, are phenomena of substances (*phaenomena substantiata*), §. 201, 200. The effects of an efficient or deficient cause are results (*effectus*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:320":["An efficient and deficient cause is either mediate, which brings about the result through another efficient or deficient cause, or immediate, which brings about the result, but not through another efficient or deficient cause. An efficient and deficient cause coordinate with another efficient and deficient cause is called its associate (*socia*)@@1@@, and if the one or the other associate has been insufficient for a given result, the other associate is called auxiliary (*auxiliaris*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:321":["An aid (*auxilium*)@@1@@ is the complement to the result of an insufficient associate cause. To be an auxiliary cause is to help (*iuvare*)@@2@@. Therefore what helps is not a solitary cause, §. 320, and a solitary cause does not help, §. 314.",[]],"baum:322":["An efficient or deficient cause, less principal and subordinate to an efficient or deficient one, is instrumental (*instrumentalis*)@@1@@ (an assistant, and ministerial).",[]],"baum:323":["A single action together with its result is called an event (*eventus*)@@1@@. A relation of the event is a circumstance (*circumstantia*)@@2@@. The complex of relations concurring in the event is the occasion (*occasio*)@@3@@, and its cause is the occasional cause (*causa occasionalis*)@@4@@. The least agreement, with the event, of the very fewest and least circumstances is the least occasion, §. 161. The more numerous and the greater the circumstances, and the more they agree toward it, the greater is the occasion, §. 160, until the greatest agreement of the most numerous and greatest circumstances toward the given event is the greatest occasion, §. 161. A greater occasion, with respect to place, is opportunity (*opportunitas*), and its opposite inopportuneness (*inopportunitas*); with respect to time it is timeliness (*tempestivitas*), and its opposite untimeliness (*intempestivitas*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:324":["If there is in event A even the least circumstance which is not in event B, events A and B are not wholly the same, §. 267, 323, and hence are partially different, §. 267; i.e. the least circumstance varies the thing externally.",[]],"baum:325":["Let events A and B be internally the same in some degree, and let them be regarded in the most diverse circumstances; the diversity depending on this is only in relations, §. 323, and hence is external, §. 37. Therefore the internal identity of events is neither increased nor diminished by diversity of circumstances, of place, of age [or time], §. 281, 85, etc., §. 162; i.e. place and time do not vary a thing internally.",[]],"baum:326":["When the principle (*principium*) is posited, the thing grounded (*principiatum*) is posited, §. 307, 30. When the cause is posited, the effect (*causatum*) is posited, §. 311. When an efficient and deficient cause is posited, the result (*effectus*) is posited, §. 319; when the occasional cause is posited, the occasion is posited, §. 323; and vice versa, §. 29.",[]],"baum:327":["When the same, a similar, an equal, a congruent principle is posited, and hence the same, similar, equal, congruent causes, §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, occasional, §. 323, there are posited the same, similar, equal, congruent things grounded, §. 276, 307, effects (*causata*), results, occasions; and vice versa.",[]],"baum:328":["When the principle is removed, and hence the cause, §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, and occasional, §. 223, the thing grounded, the effect, the result, the occasion is removed, §. 307, 31; and vice versa, §. 32.",[]],"baum:329":["Every result is similar to the deficient or efficient cause, §. 265; i.e. as the cause, so the result, §. 70. The result is either similar to the efficient cause, apart from the specific difference of the two, or less so, §. 10. The former result is called univocal (*univocus*)@@1@@, the latter equivocal (*aequivocus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:330":["A result which has in a given action the proximate ground of its existence is its immediate result (*effectus immediatus*)@@1@@ (proximate, and continuous, cf. §. 285). Mediate results (*mediati*)@@2@@ (remote) are those which have in the given action only a more remote ground of their existence. All the results of an action taken in every degree of intensity are the full result (*effectus plenus*)@@3@@. Less than full (*minus plenus*)@@4@@ is that which is either only some of them, or regarded in a lesser degree.",[]],"baum:331":["The full result is as great as the action by which it is brought about, §. 330, 214, and hence is equal to the action, §. 70. Now the action is as great as the living force (*vis viva*) by which it is brought about, §. 220, 166, and hence the action is equal to the living forces by which it is brought about, §. 70. Therefore the action of an efficient cause is equal to its living forces, §. 319. Hence the full result is equal (proportionate) to the living forces of the efficient cause, §. 275.",[]],"baum:332":["The nobility or dignity of any result of an effect that is to be essentially subordinated to some efficient cause belongs to the full result of this cause, §. 330, 317, 166. Therefore a result is not nobler than its efficient cause, to which it is essentially subordinated, §. 331, 160.",[]],"baum:333":["From the result the qualities, §. 329, and quantities, §. 331, and hence the determinations of the cause can be known, §. 70, 67. Therefore the result is the principle of knowing the cause, §. 311; i.e. the result bears witness to the cause.",[]],"baum:334":["Every contingent and finite being is a being from another, §. 308. Therefore existence does not inhere in what exists by a force proper to it, §. 307, and hence a foreign force posited outside the finite and contingent real thing is the sufficient ground of the existence inhering in the finite and contingent real being, §. 210. Therefore a substance posited outside it acts upon it, producing existence by influx, §. 211. Hence every contingent and finite real being is a result, §. 319, and has an efficient cause, §. 326.",[]],"baum:335":["The causal connexion between an efficient, deficient cause and the result is called effective (*effectivus*)@@1@@. Hence associate causes are connected by an effective connexion, §. 320, 314.",[]],"baum:336":["The useful (*utile*)@@1@@ is what is good for another; what is not good for another is useless (*inutile*)@@2@@; what is bad for another is harmful (*noxium*)@@3@@. Hence usefulness (*utilitas*)@@4@@ is relative goodness, §. 37, which, if ascribed to the thing to which another can be of benefit, may be called passive (*passiva*)@@5@@, if to that which can be of benefit, active (*activa*)@@6@@.",[]],"baum:337":["The least usefulness is that in which one single minimum posits one single minimum perfection in another single minimum, §. 336, 161; and it is increased the more numerous and greater the things in the more numerous and greater things, and the more numerous and greater the perfections they posit, §. 187. The degree of usefulness is called value (*valor*)@@1@@, and the judgement about value price (*pretium*)@@2@@ (estimation). Hence estimation with price is either true, or apparent or imaginary, §. 12.",[]],"baum:338":["Use (*usus*)@@1@@ is the actualisation of usefulness. Abuse (*abusus*) is either apparent use (*usus apparens*)@@2@@, or use by which the useful perishes (*interit*)@@3@@. And since from the useful the perfection of that to which it is of benefit can be known, §. 336, 100, the useful and that to which it can be of benefit are connected, and their connexion may be called the connexion of usefulness (*nexus utilitatis*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:339":["If anyone uses the useful, the perfection of another is actualised through it, §. 338, 100, and the useful through use becomes the cause of the perfection of that to which it is of benefit, §. 307. And this causal connexion may be called the connexion of uses (*nexus usuum*)@@1@@. What anyone uses is useful, §. 336, 57; no one uses what is wholly useless, §. 338, 58; yet some useful thing lacks use, §. 59, and hence something which no one uses is none the less useful, §. 60.",[]],"baum:340":["Things useful to one and the same thing are connected with it, §. 339. Therefore all things useful to one are connected with one another, §. 314. If the useful A is useful to the useful B essentially subordinated to it, it is useful also to that C to which B is of benefit, §. 317.",[]],"baum:341":["If anyone uses or abuses something in order to actualise a good that seems to him: the good itself that seems so to the agent is called the end (*finis*)@@1@@, cf. §. 248; the causes by which he can use or abuse it for the end are called the means (*media*)@@2@@ (the designated, the limited, cf. §. 248, remedies); and the representation of the end is called the intention (*intentio*)@@3@@. Now the end is the principle of use or abuse, §. 338, 307, and hence a final cause (*causa finalis*), §. 338, 307.",[]],"baum:342":["The grounds of an intention in the one who intends are entitled impulsive causes (*causae impulsivae*)@@1@@. An end (*finis*) is the effect of an action and of the means which the agent uses or misuses, §. 341, 319. Hence the full end is equal to the action and the means. The means and the action are equal (proportioned) to the full end, §. 331. The seizing of an occasion, §. 323, and the removal of impediments, §. 221, are means, §. 341.",[]],"baum:343":["The causal nexus (*nexus causalis*) of means and ends is final@@1@@ if all co-ends are connected with one another, §. 314, 341. The end of ends essentially subordinated to one another is also the end of the means, §. 317, 341. The first end, that is, the one to which all co-ends are subordinated, is entitled the last end or goal (*scopus*)@@2@@. This is either simply last, or last in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) within a given subordination of ends, §. 28. The rest are intermediate@@3@@.",[]],"baum:344":["If a being is conceived as determinable, it is matter, cf. §. 295, 296, out of which (*ex qua*)@@1@@. In the very act of determination it is matter about which (*circa quam*) (object, subject of the occupation)@@2@@. Once the determination has been made, it is matter in which (*in qua*). This last, together with the matter out of which, is called the subject.",[]],"baum:345":["Since matter and form contain the ground of an actual determination, §. 344, 40, they are causes, §. 307. The former is material, the latter formal. Material and formal co-causes are connected with one another, §. 314. Their nexus with their effects (*principiata*), §. 307, and with one another, §. 314, may be called subjective in the former case and formal (essential) in the latter.",[]],"baum:346":["An exemplar (*exemplar*)@@1@@ is that to which something is intended to be similar. Since it is an impulsive cause, §. 342, it is a cause, §. 307, which is called exemplary, and its effect is called the exemplate (*exemplatum*)@@2@@ (ectype, copy). An exemplar which has no other [exemplar] is an archetype (*archetypon*)@@3@@ (original). Exemplar, exemplate and co-exemplars are connected by a causal nexus, §. 313, which is an exemplary (typical) nexus.",[]],"baum:347":["A means of knowing another existence is a sign (*signum*)@@1@@; the end of the sign is the signified (*signatum*)@@2@@. Hence a sign is the principle of knowing (*principium cognoscendi*) of the signified, §. 311. The nexus between sign and signified is significative@@3@@, and the signification (*significatus*)@@4@@ attributed to a sign is called its meaning (force, power).",[]],"baum:348":["The actual signified, §. 347, is either present, in which case the sign is called demonstrative (*demonstrativum*)@@1@@; or past, in which case the sign is called mnemonic (*mnemonicum*)@@2@@ (rememorative, μνημοσυνον); or future, §. 298, in which case the sign is called prognostic (*prognosticon*).",[]],"baum:349":["The science of signs (semiotic, philosophical semiology, symbolics) is characteristic (*characteristica*)@@1@@. It is, I. heuristic (*heuristica*)@@2@@, concerned with the discovery of signs, both primitive ones@@3@@, which do not have signs as parts, and derivative ones@@4@@, which are composed of signs. If the mode of composition of these is more similar to the essence of the signified, they are essential@@5@@. The heuristic of derivative signs is combinatorics (*combinatoria*)@@6@@. It is, II. hermeneutic (*hermeneutica*)@@7@@, concerned with knowing the signified of signs. This hermeneutic is universal@@8@@. The characteristic of prognostics is mantic (*mantica*)@@9@@.",[]],"baum:350":["A sign of a representation is a term (*terminus*) (cf. §. 248, symbol). The terms more commonly used by means of the human voice are words (*vocabula*)@@1@@. A series of words signifying connected representations is speech (*oratio*) (in the wider sense)@@2@@. The complex of words more commonly used in a certain larger region is a particular language (*lingua particularis*)@@3@@. A language more commonly used in a certain smaller region, differing from itself as it is more commonly used in other smaller regions, is an idiom (*idioma*)@@4@@. A more perfect idiom is a dialect (*dialectus*). Terms, strokes to be observed with the eyes, are characters in the stricter sense (*characteres*)@@5@@, cf. §. 67. These are either nominal@@6@@, [characters] of words, or real@@7@@, [characters] of the things to be signified, [standing] immediately [for them] by means of words. If these signify a thing other than that to which they are most similar, they are hieroglyphic (*hieroglyphici*); [they are] the insignia of societies@@8@@. If they can be read in every particular language, their complex is a universal language (*lingua universalis*)@@9@@.",[]],"baum:351":["General cosmology (*cosmologia generalis*) is the science of the general predicates of the world. It is either empirical (*empirica*), if [these are drawn] more closely from experience, or rational (*rationalis*), if from the notion of the world.",[]],"baum:352":["Cosmology, since it contains the first principles of psychology, theologies, physics, teleology and practical philosophy, is rightly referred, §. 2, to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:353":["Cosmology teaches of the world 1) its notion, 2) its parts, 3) its perfection.",[]],"baum:354":["The world (*mundus*)@@1@@ (cf. §. 91, 403, 434; universe, παν) is a series (multitude, whole) of actual finite things, which is not a part of another.",[]],"baum:355":["This world exists. Therefore the world is in itself possible, §. 57, 18.",[]],"baum:356":["In this world there are actual things posited outside one another, and hence an actual universal nexus, §. 279, 306.",[]],"baum:357":["In every world there are actual parts, §. 354, 155. These parts are severally connected with the whole, §. 14, 157, and hence severally connected with one another, §. 33. Therefore in every world there is a nexus of the parts and a universal harmony, §. 48; that is, in the world there is no island. Otherwise: the parts of the world are either actual things posited outside one another, and are severally connected with one another, §. 279, 306; or they are its internal determinations, not posited outside one another, §. 10, 37, and are likewise severally connected with one another, §. 49. Ουκ εστιν επεισοδιωδησ εστιν ωσπερ στρατευμα [It is not episodic; it is like an army].",[]],"baum:358":["In this world there exists an efficient nexus (the realm of power), §. 335; [a nexus] of utility, §. 338; of uses, §. 339; a final nexus (the realm of wisdom), §. 343; a subjective and a formal nexus, §. 345; an exemplary nexus, §. 346; a significative nexus, §. 347. Hence nexuses of this kind are possible in the world, §. 57.",[]],"baum:359":["Since every world is a being, §. 355, 62, it will be one, §. 73 (§. 354, 155), and true, §. 90 (§. 357, 355, 354, 92). Hence in every world there is order, §. 89, and common rules, §. 86. A fabulous world is not a world, §. 120.",[]],"baum:360":["Every world is perfect, §. 99, 359, and good, §. 100.",[]],"baum:361":["The several parts of every world are contingent beings, §. 354, 257. Hence their existence is a mode, §. 134. Now the existence of the several parts of the world taken together is the existence of the world, §. 155. Therefore the existence of the world is a mode, and every world is a contingent being, §. 111. Otherwise: suppose some world to be a necessary being. All its internal determinations will then be absolutely necessary, and hence no part of that world will have modes, §. 108, 157, nor will it be a contingent being, §. 134; but every part of that world will be a necessary being, §. 109, and hence infinite, §. 258, which is absurd (*q.a.*), §. 354.",[]],"baum:362":["Every world is one, §. 359, yet has modes, §. 361, 112, and hence determinations separable in themselves, §. 72, 65, and hence a hypothetical unity, §. 76, which is intrinsically contingent, §. 115.",[]],"baum:363":["The conjunction of the parts of no world is absolutely necessary, §. 362, 102; there is nevertheless a coordination, §. 78. Therefore in every world there is an order contingent in itself, §. 117, and hence also a truth intrinsically contingent, §. 119.",[]],"baum:364":["All the parts of the world are real, §. 136, and agree together toward the degree of reality to be attributed to their world, §. 248, 140; yet in such a way that in themselves they could also fail to agree, or agree to a lesser extent, inasmuch as they are contingent beings, §. 354, 257. Therefore in every world there is a perfection contingent in itself, §. 122.",[]],"baum:365":["Every world is absolutely and internally mutable, §. 361, 133.",[]],"baum:366":["In every world there are absolutely necessary realities, §. 359, 360, and hence metaphysical good, §. 147; and there are realities contingent in themselves, §. 362-364, and hence physical good, broadly speaking, §. 147.",[]],"baum:367":["This world is one, §. 359, contingently in itself, §. 362. In this world there are order, §. 359, and truth, which are intrinsically contingent, §. 363. There are also perfection and goodness, intrinsically contingent, §. 364, metaphysical good and physical good broadly speaking, §. 366. And this world is absolutely, and is internally mutable, §. 365, 154.",[]],"baum:368":["The smallest world would be the whole of the fewest and smallest actual things, §. 161, 354. Hence the more, and the greater, finite things it has as parts, the greater it becomes, §. 160, until it is the greatest, an aggregate of the most numerous and greatest among finite things, §. 161.",[]],"baum:369":["The state of the world (*status mundi*)@@1@@ is the whole of all the states simultaneous in its parts. Now this world has parts in which fixed things coexist with mutable things, §. 367. Therefore this world has a state, §. 205.",[]],"baum:370":["The existence of every world, and of this one, is a mode, §. 361, 134, and hence can be absent or present with the essence of the world remaining safe, §. 65. And since the actuality of every world, and of this one, is absolutely mutable, §. 133, the beginning and the end of every world is absolutely possible, §. 301, 65.",[]],"baum:371":["A world cannot arise except out of nothing. For suppose some world to arise in such a way that some part of it had already pre-existed. Since, and as long as, this part of it existed, it was in part existing, as it will always be if it has existed, and not non-existing; hence it does not arise after the pre-existing part, though it seems to arise, §. 227. Since such a world had not yet arisen, it was nevertheless no longer merely possible, inasmuch as it would be the whole of an existing part, and so already determined as to existence, §. 54. Therefore a world not arising out of nothing does not arise, §. 228. Likewise a world not annihilated does not perish, since, while a part survives, it remains in part existing, as it always was if it existed, and hence is not changed into the non-existing, §. 227. Therefore a world cannot perish except by annihilation, §. 228.",[]],"baum:372":["Every world, and this one, is a finite being, §. 255, 361, and hence a philosophical and mathematical cognition of it is possible, §. 249. Every world, and this one, has metaphysical evil, §. 250, and contingent evil, or physical evil broadly speaking, an imperfection contingent in itself and absolutely possible, §. 263, being partly good, partly bad, §. 264.",[]],"baum:373":["All worlds are partially similar to the infinite being, to one another, and to any of their parts, §. 265, 267. But it is impossible that several worlds, actual outside one another, should be totally the same, §. 269, [or] diverse, §. 268, congruent, §. 270, similar, §. 271, [or] equal, §. 272.",[]],"baum:374":["Since the parts of the world are either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 354, if they are posited outside one another, they will be connected in the world either through time, or through space, or through both, §. 239, 306.",[]],"baum:375":["Every world is a being from another (*ens ab alio*), §. 361, or dependent (*dependens*), §. 308, and this world has an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) posited outside itself, and is its effect (*effectus*), §. 334, bearing witness to a cause, §. 333.",[]],"baum:376":["The parts of every world are actual things (*actualia*), §. 354. Hence each part in its own world, and so the parts of this world in this world, have truth (*veritas*), §. 90, and certainty (*certitudo*), §. 93, as internal determinations (*determinationes internae*), §. 37, 93, which are determinate (*determinatae*), §. 54.",[]],"baum:377":["Finite things which are possible not merely absolutely, nor merely hypothetically in some nexus or other, but also in the universal nexus of some world, are called the possibles of that world (*possibilia illius mundi*)@@1@@. Hence the possibles of this world are those which, though regarded in its universal nexus, are still hypothetically possible, and hence have a greater degree of possibility, §. 165, 246.",[]],"baum:378":["If even a single part of this world were other than it is, this world would not be wholly (*totaliter*) the same as it is, §. 155, 267. Now all the parts of this universe can be other than they are, § 354, 260. Therefore there are possible worlds, partly different from this one, §. 38, partly the same as it, §. 265, i.e. several, §. 74.",[]],"baum:379":["This world is uniquely singular, §. 77. For suppose there were several; together with this one they would constitute a multitude or series, §. 74. Hence this would not be a world, §. 354, or all those worlds would be only parts of this [world], which alone is unique, §. 354, 77.",[]],"baum:380":["A progress (regress) to infinity (*progressus in infinitum*)@@1@@ would be a series of contingent beings posited outside one another, of which one is the cause of another in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), without a cause that is such simply (*simpliciter*). If in this progress to infinity what is caused is taken as the cause of itself, it would be curvilinear@@2@@ (circular); if in it what is caused is not taken as the cause of itself, it would be rectilinear@@3@@.",[]],"baum:381":["A progress to infinity, however great it be posited, would be a contingent being (*ens contingens*), §. 380, 155, and hence would have an efficient cause posited outside itself, §. 334. This cause could not be a contingent being; for since the latter would again be a being from another, §. 308, it would not be the cause of the progress to infinity, except as such in a certain respect, §. 28. Therefore the cause is not posited outside the progress itself, but is a part of it, §. 155, 380. Therefore the efficient cause of the progress to infinity would have to be a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 109, and independent, §. 310. In whatever way this can exist, in that way it exists, §. 259. But it can exist although it is not the effect of another posited outside itself, §. 310. Therefore it is not the effect of another posited outside itself, and so is the cause of its effects simply such, §. 28. Hence a progress to infinity, which would have to be without a cause simply such, §. 380, and yet have one, is impossible, §. 7, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:382":["Fate (*fatum*)@@1@@ is the necessity of events in the world. Fate arising from the absolute necessity of the world would be Spinozistic (*spinosisticum*), a non-being, §. 361, 105, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:383":["An event in the world whose sufficient reason (*ratio sufficiens*) is unknown is a chance (*casus*)@@1@@. A chance for which there is also no sufficient reason would be pure@@2@@ [chance], and impossible, §. 22, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:384":["An ordinary event (*eventus ordinarius*)@@1@@ is one whose existence is determined according to the rules of some definite order; an extraordinary one (*extraordinarius*)@@2@@ is one that is not ordinary. Absolutely@@3@@ extraordinary would be an event whose existence were determined according to no rules of any order; relatively@@4@@ such is one that does not occur through the rules of a given definite order. An event extraordinary because of confusion is disordered (*inordinatus*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:385":["The absolutely extraordinary (*absolute extraordinarium*) would not be true, §. 384, 89, and hence is impossible, §. 90, 62, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:386":["An event without any proximate sufficient reason would be an absolute leap (*saltus absolutus*)@@1@@. An ordinary event without proximate sufficient reason is a relative leap (*saltus respectivus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:387":["What would exist without any proximate sufficient reason, §. 27, would exist through pure chance, §. 22, 383. Hence an absolute leap is impossible, §. 386, 284, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58. Every relative leap also, if it is not something disordered, is nevertheless something extraordinary, §. 384.",[]],"baum:388":["The world is neither an infinite substance (*substantia infinita*), §. 372, 248, nor an internal determination of it, §. 365, 252, and hence neither the essence of an infinite being (*essentia entis infiniti*), §. 40, nor an attribute (*attributum*), nor a mode (*modus*), §. 50, nor a modification (*modificatio*), §. 209. Hence every world is to be posited outside infinite substance, and so this world also exists outside the infinite being, which is therefore called the extramundane being (*ens extramundanum*)@@1@@, the being actual outside this world.",[]],"baum:389":["Every world, and hence this one also, is either a substance (*substantia*) or an accident (*accidens*), §. 191. If this world is a substance, it subsists outside infinite substance, §. 388, so that infinite substance is not the sole substance, §. 77.",[]],"baum:390":["If this world is an accident, it is nevertheless not an accident of infinite substance, otherwise it would not be going to exist outside its substantial [subject] (*substantiale*), §. 196, 388, nor can it exist except in a substance or in substances, §. 194. Therefore if this world is an accident, infinite substance is not then the sole substance either, §. 77, 389. Now one or other case is necessary, §. 191, 10. Therefore infinite substance is not the sole substance.",[]],"baum:391":["Infinite force (*vis infinita*) is not the sole force, §. 390, 198; in every world, and in this one also, there are finite forces, §. 390, 388.",[]],"baum:392":["Every world is either a simple (*simplex*) or a composite (*compositum*) being, §. 224; this one is a composite being. He who thinks this world a simple being is an egoist (*egoista*).",[]],"baum:393":["The parts of a composite world, singly and taken together, cannot be accidents. For since such a world could not exist except in substances, §. 155, 191, and yet could not inhere in an infinite one, §. 388, nor in a single finite one, §. 224, 194, it would require several finite substances in which it would inhere, §. 77. But these would constitute a series of finite actual things, which, if it were not a part of the world composed of mere accidents, would nevertheless together with that world constitute a series of finite actual things greater than that world itself, §. 160, and hence that world would not itself be a world, §. 354. If that series which the finite substances would constitute were a part of that world, the world would not be composed of mere accidents, §. 155. Hence a world composed of mere accidents is impossible, §. 61, 62. Every composite world is a composite in the stricter sense (*compositum strictius dictum*), §. 225. Every world is either a substance, §. 389, 392, or a composite in the stricter sense.",[]],"baum:394":["The parts of a composite world are either substantial or accidental, §. 393, and the former indeed are monads (*monades*), §. 235. Hence every composite world, and this one also, consists of monads, §. 392. Whence it is again clear that no world can arise except from nothing, §. 236, nor perish except by annihilation, §. 237, 393.",[]],"baum:395":["This world, a composite in the stricter sense, §. 393, consists of monads, and there is nothing substantial in it besides monads, §. 394. He who denies the existence of monads is a universal materialist (*materialista universalis*). He who denies the existence of monads as parts of the universe, e.g. of this one, is a cosmological materialist (*materialista cosmologicus*) (cf. §. 757).",[]],"baum:396":["The monads of every composite world, and hence of this one, are possibles (*possibilia*), §. 8, rational things (*rationalia*), §. 24, beings, §. 63, one, §. 73, true, §. 90, objectively certain, §. 93, perfect, §. 99, good, §. 100, contingent beings, §. 257, mutable, §. 133, real, §. 136, universally connected, §. 357. They are endowed with force, indeed are forces, even in the stricter sense, §. 199; they have an internal state, §. 206, and an external state, §. 207, which are modifiable, §. 209. Singly they are neither extended nor space-filling, but when aggregated [they are], §. 242, nor do they have quantitative magnitude, §. 243; they are indivisible, §. 244, and finite, §. 354, and hence have a definite limit of forces, §. 249, [which is] metaphysical evil, §. 250. They are partially similar to one another, §. 265, 268, but partially also both dissimilar and unequal, §. 273; singly they do not admit of figure, yet in such a way that their whole admits of figure, §. 280.",[]],"baum:397":["The monads of every composite world, and hence of this one, since they are actual and posited outside one another, §. 354, 224, will be either simultaneous beings or successive beings, or both, §. 238. Hence singly they have position (*situs*) [*positum*], §. 148, either a place (*locus*) among simultaneous things, or an age (*aetas*) among successive things, or both, §. 281, although singly they do not fill a place, §. 396.",[]],"baum:398":["Of the monads of every finite universe, and hence of this one, §. 354, any one, if it be posited as subsisting outside the rest and as about to subsist (*substitura*), §. 192, cannot be wholly (*totaliter*) in the same place with any other, §. 282. A substance in whose place no other posited outside it can be is impenetrable (*impenetrabilis*)@@1@@ (solid). Therefore every substance, and hence monads also, both of this and of every composite world, are impenetrable, §. 230.",[]],"baum:399":["The monads of this and of every composite, and hence extended, world, §. 241, 393, are points, §. 286, but by no means mathematical ones, in which nothing is posited beyond non-extension, §. 396-398; nor, when posited beside one another, do they coincide or fuse, §. 70, 396. Rather, when several of them are posited as coexisting, because each is impenetrable, §. 398, they are posited as simultaneous outside one another in a certain order, §. 396, 78, and hence in their aggregate there is space, §. 239. Therefore every aggregate of the monads of this and of every composite world is extended, §. 241. The mathematical point (*punctum mathematicum*), an abstract possible, §. 149, if it be feigned to exist, is a Zenonian point, a fictitious being, §. 62. As to the physical point (*punctum physicum*): if you call it actual and, besides simplicity, determined in every respect, some monads of this universe are physical points, namely those [monads] of whose aggregate the extended [is formed] [*nempe quarum ex aggregato extensum*; the Latin is elliptical].",[]],"baum:400":["All monads of every composite world, and hence of this world, stand in universal connection (§. 357). Hence each of them is, in relation to each of the others, either a ground (*ratio*) or a consequence (*rationatum*), or both (§. 14, 48). From the consequence the ground can be known (§. 29). Therefore from any one monad of any composite world, and so of this world, the several parts of the world to which it belongs can be known (§. 14). That is, the several monads of every composite world, and hence of this world, are powers (*vires*) representative of their universe (§. 199). They are its active mirrors (§. 210), indivisible (§. 244), microcosms, the world in epitome, and concentrations of their world; or, in other words, they have a representative power of their universe, and are endowed with it.",[]],"baum:401":["Monads that represent (*repraesentantes*) their world (§. 400) either represent it to themselves with consciousness of their perception, at least in part, or without such consciousness (§. 10). Hence the monads of this universe also represent this world either only obscurely, or clearly, at least in part. The former are bare monads@@1@@ (*monades nudae*; dormant, *sopitae*).",[]],"baum:402":["Monads that clearly represent the world represent it to themselves either distinctly, at least in part, or not (§. 10). The former understand (*intelligunt*, §. 69). Therefore they have the faculty of knowing distinctly (§. 216), that is, understanding (*intellectus*)@@1@@ (in the stricter sense, cf. §. 519). An intellectual substance@@2@@, that is, one endowed with understanding, is a spirit@@3@@ (*spiritus*; intelligence, person). Therefore the intellectual monads of this universe are spirits (§. 230). One who admits only spirits in this world is an idealist (*idealista*).",[]],"baum:403":["The connection of the spirits of any world with one another is pneumatic@@1@@ (*pneumaticus*). Now in every world, this one included, in which spirits are present, each is connected with each. Therefore in this and in every world in which spirits are present there is a universal pneumatic connection (§. 357). (World, cf. §. 354; pneumatic, intellectual, moral, cf. §. 723; kingdom of grace.)",[]],"baum:404":["Every substance is a monad (§. 234). Every spirit is a substance (§. 402). Therefore [a spirit] is a monad, and also a simple being (§. 230).",[]],"baum:405":["All spirits of every world, and of this world, in which spirits are present, are monads (§. 404), and are powers representative of their world, at least in part distinctly (§. 402). They are unextended, no one of them filling space, nor have they quantitative magnitude. They are indivisible, finite, endowed only with a certain degree of powers, subject to metaphysical evil, partially similar to one another, but each also partially dissimilar and unequal, and none of them admits of figure (§. 396). One alone of all is most perfect (§. 77, 185).",[]],"baum:406":["A whole of monads is a monadate (*monadatum*). Therefore this world, and every composite world, is a monadate (§. 394).",[]],"baum:407":["Monads in a composite world either have also its parts as their monadates, or not (§. 10, 406). In either case such a monadate will be extended (§. 396), of impenetrable parts (§. 398), and partially dissimilar (§. 396), that is, partially heterogeneous (*difforme, dissimilare*). Hence it will not be an extended thing totally homogeneous (*uniforme, similare*), that is, one of totally similar parts (§. 271).",[]],"baum:408":["The simultaneous monads of this world determine for one another place, and the successive determine for one another age (§. 281, 85). Hence they influence one another mutually (§. 211), being placed in conflict (§. 213). Therefore in this world there is a universal influence (*influxus*) and conflict (§. 48, 306): a war of all against all, a discordant concord, a concordant discord (§. 364).",[]],"baum:409":["Of the monads in this world some influence one another mutually more remotely (§. 408, 27); some more closely, being present to one another (§. 223); and, since an absolute leap in the world is impossible (§. 387), some most closely, touching one another (§. 223).",[]],"baum:410":["Because all monads of this world influence one another mutually (§. 408), their action is not transient (*actio transiens*), that is, influence without reaction (§. 213).",[]],"baum:411":["Transient action, or the influence of parts of the world on others outside them, is either simple or composite (§. 215). The simple is also the least (§. 247, 214). To this there corresponds a reaction which is either the least or a greater (§. 160, 161). If it is the least, it is equal to the influence (§. 70). If a greater reaction be posited, that by which the reaction exceeds the influence posits a reaction of the [part] influencing (§. 410), and hence its influence will not be simple (§. 215), which is contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore to a simple influence there corresponds an equal reaction (§. 70).",[]],"baum:412":["The composite influence of parts of the world on others outside them is a whole of several simple influences (§. 215, 214). To each of these there corresponds an equal reaction (§. 411). Now if you add equals to equals, the sums are equal (§. 70). Therefore to every composite influence of parts of the world there corresponds an equal reaction. Hence every reaction of parts of the world on others outside them is equal to the influence or transient action (§. 411).",[]],"baum:413":["The living forces (*vires vivae*) of monads in the world, conflicting with one another by mutual contact, are equal (§. 412, 331). And since the same forces cannot be proximate grounds of contradictories (§. 140, 36), these monads, which proximately determine for one another place (§. 408, 409), and hence conjunction (§. 281, 85), do not contain the proximate ground of mutual separation (§. 72). Hence, unless a third force intervenes, they touch one another inseparably (§. 386); they constitute one (§. 73), and are united (§. 79).",[]],"baum:414":["Things that touch one another in such a way that they can be separated only by a third force cohere@@1@@ (*cohaerent*). Therefore monads of the world touching one another cohere (§. 413). There is no contact without cohesion. Therefore certain monads of this world cohere (§. 409), constituting one (§. 413), an extended thing (§. 407).",[]],"baum:415":["No change occurs in a composite world without motion. For let A be that which is to be changed from B into non-B. It had coexisted, as B, with its simultaneous things posited outside it; now it will coexist as non-B. Hence it will acquire a different relation to them (§. 37, 38), a position (§. 85) and a place (§. 281), and motion will arise (§. 283, 125). As often as such a change, such a motion, occurs in a composite world, the state of the thing changed, and of the universe of which it is a part, is partly the same as the preceding states (§. 265), and partly different (§. 125). Hence, just as there was a certain motion in so far as the new state differs from the former, so, in so far as the state has remained the same, this duration of state is in a composite world at the same time duration of place (§. 299), absence of a certain motion, that is, rest (§. 183), and was a hindrance of a certain motion (§. 221) and a resistance (§. 222).",[]],"baum:416":["Monads in the universe that constitute an extended thing (§. 414) always act (§. 216, 285), by their power (§. 400), representing the several states of their universe and their own, including future states (§. 298). In so far as these states are the same as the antecedent ones, and persist, they hinder a certain motion and resist a certain motion (§. 415, 210); in so far as these states are different from the antecedent ones, they effect, or move, a certain other motion (§. 415, 210). The aggregate of predicates belonging to the several parts is attributed to the whole (§. 155). Hence certain monads of this universe constitute an extended thing to which a force of inertia (*vis inertiae*) is attributed (§. 294), and hence matter (§. 295). No matter, of this universe or of any, can be totally homogeneous (§. 407).",[]],"baum:417":["The monads of this universe that constitute matter do not exhibit it as first matter in the sense of §. 295, and merely passive, but as matter to which moving force (*vis motrix*) is attributed (§. 416), that is, second matter, and a physical body (§. 296). If one part of this world is moved, its relation to each of the other simultaneous parts is changed (§. 283, 281). Hence there is in the world no particular motion@@1@@, that is, motion of some part of the world, without a universal one (§. 283). Hence in this world all matter is in motion (§. 415), and its rest is only relative@@2@@, that is, the absence of some determinate motion (§. 283). If this rest be so great that no motion of the resting thing is observed, the motion will be an evanescent one@@3@@. There is in the world no absolute rest, that is, absence of all motion.",[]],"baum:418":["An extended thing, being an aggregate of monads each of which is impenetrable, will on the one hand resist the motion of things posited outside it which are about to occupy its place (§. 398, 222), and on the other hand will exert a force of inertia (§. 294). An extended thing, being an aggregate of monads, no force of any of which is totally equal to that of any other posited outside it, and no whole of which, as regards forces, is totally equal to any other monadate posited outside the former (§. 272, 406), is never in total equilibrium@@1@@, that is, equality of forces among the things in conflict, and on this account will exert moving force (§. 294). Now every monadate in this world is an extended thing of this kind (§. 414, 396). Therefore monadates in this world, by reason of one and the same representative power of their monads (§. 400), will manifest force of inertia in so far as this power contains, in the impenetrable, the ground of enduring place, of rest, and of the absence of a certain motion; and moving force in so far as the same power, in things never totally in equilibrium, contains the ground of change of place and of a certain motion. They are matter (§. 416) and physical bodies (§. 417). These two forces are so far from being opposed to each other that, if the one be posited, the other could not at the same time be posited in the same subject in a different respect (§. 87); on the contrary, in relation to different motions they are simultaneously present in any extended thing whatever of this world (§. 417). And one and the same force attributed to a given extended thing is moving force in respect of a given motion, and force of inertia in respect of the motion opposed to it, and vice versa (§. 415).",[]],"baum:419":["Since forces are attributed to bodies (§. 296), they are composites in the stricter sense (§. 225, 198). Hence the mode of composition is their essence (§. 226); they consist of monads (§. 235); they have quantitative magnitude (§. 243).",[]],"baum:420":["Bodies have parts outside parts (§. 296, 224). The actual first parts of bodies, outside one another, are called elements@@1@@ (*elementa*).",[]],"baum:421":["Elements are either parts of bodies that are absolutely first, that is, which do not themselves again have parts outside parts, or are such relatively, beyond which it is not desired to proceed in attending to composition (§. 420). The former are simple beings, or monads (§. 419, 224).",[]],"baum:422":["That which is not matter is called immaterial (*immateriale*); that which is matter is called material (*materiale*). The elements of bodies, absolutely such, are immaterial, §. 421, 295. The incorporeal (*incorporeum*) is that which is not a body; that which is a body is corporeal (*corporeum*). Now everything corporeal is material, §. 296. Therefore the elements of bodies, absolutely such, are incorporeal, and hence none of them is a body, §. 296.",[]],"baum:423":["A philosophy which takes what is relatively first for what is absolutely first is entitled the philosophy of the lazy (*philosophia pigrorum*). Since this is false, the elements relatively such, §. 421, which are material and corporeal, §. 422, are not to be taken for elements absolutely such, which are immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422. Hence the latter are more fittingly called elements simply, the former first matters (*materiae primae*)@@1@@, cf. §. 295.",[]],"baum:424":["A being indivisible through itself is entitled an atom (*atomus*)@@1@@. Every monad is indivisible through itself, §. 244. Hence every monad is an atom. Therefore the elements are atoms, §. 423, and hence are called atoms of nature.",[]],"baum:425":["We call observable (*observabilia*)@@1@@ (phenomena) those things which we can know through the senses (confusedly). Smaller bodies which are not observable by us are called corpuscles (*corpuscula*). The philosophy which explains the phenomena of bodies from corpuscles is corpuscularian (*corpuscularis*).",[]],"baum:426":["Corpuscles either have other corpuscles in turn for their parts, or they do not, §. 10, 155. The latter are called primitive (*primitiva*)@@1@@, the former derivative (*derivativa*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:427":["The elements constitute all matter, such as there is in this world, §. 418, 419. Hence the quantitative magnitude (*magnitudo quantitativa*) which is to be ascribed to it, §. 419, 423, is the multitude of the elements united to constitute a certain matter, §. 159, 413. Hence the elements, as parts of a certain matter, are in the same place with it, as with their monadate [*monadatum*, the whole made up of monads], §. 155, 406. Therefore, if their place is so changed that they are no longer in the same place with the matter which they previously constituted, they do not remain parts of that matter, §. 155. Now every change of relation is possible in a being considered in itself, §. 133. Therefore every change of place is possible in all the elements considered in themselves, §. 281, 85. Therefore that motion also is possible by which they cease to be parts of the matter of which they had been parts, §. 283. If they are so moved, the matter is divided, §. 244, 162. Therefore all matter, such as there is in the world, is divisible, at least in and through itself, §. 18.",[]],"baum:428":["If matter in the world is said to be divisible to infinity, this is understood either absolutely, [namely] that it has no indivisible parts, and then it is false, §. 424, 419; or respectively, [namely] that in dividing it there are for us nothing but unobservable parts, themselves again divisible, §. 248, and then it is true that matter is divisible indefinitely (*in indefinitum*), §. 425.",[]],"baum:429":["A material atom would be a corpuscle indivisible through itself, which is nothing, §. 427, 425. Therefore the atomistic philosophy (*philosophia atomistica*), which explains the phenomena of bodies from material atoms, is mistaken.",[]],"baum:430":["The nature (*natura*) of a being (cf. §. 431, 466) is the complex of those of its internal determinations which are principles of its changes, or, in general, of the accidents inhering in it. Hence to the nature of a being belong: 1) its essentials, §. 39; 2) its essence, §. 40; 3) its faculties; 4) its receptivities, §. 216; 5) all the powers with which it is equipped, §. 197. The beginning of a nature is origin (*origo*)@@1@@, its duration is life (*vita*)@@2@@, its end is death (*mors*)@@3@@, cf. §. 556.",[]],"baum:431":["The nature of bodies is the mode of their composition, §. 419, together with every faculty, receptivity, force of inertia and motive force with which they are endowed, §. 296, 430. It is also sometimes called simply nature, cf. §. 430, 466.",[]],"baum:432":["Since there is universal truth in the world, §. 363, 90, all its changes have common norms, §. 86, 89, according to which the nature of its parts is determined, §. 431, and hence also the motions of bodies in the world, §. 283. Of these norms the higher are called laws (*leges*)@@1@@, the lower rules of motion (*regulae motus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:433":["A machine (*machina*) is a composite, strictly so called, movable according to laws of motion. Therefore every body in the world is a machine, §. 419, 432. Nor is there given in the world any machine except a contingent being, §. 361. The nature of a machine, determined through laws of motion, is mechanism (*mechanismus*). But whatever is not composite is not a machine; hence no monad is a machine, §. 230.",[]],"baum:434":["The connection of machines is mechanical (*mechanicus*); hence the connection of bodies in the world is mechanical (a world which, cf. §. 354, is corporeal, material, a kingdom of nature), §. 433. But in a world in which there are spirits and bodies, bodies are also connected with spirits, §. 357. Hence in a world in which there are spirits and bodies there is a connection 1) of bodies among themselves; 2) of spirits among themselves, §. 403; 3) a mutual connection of bodies and spirits, pneumatico-mechanical and mechanico-pneumatical (the harmony of the kingdom of nature and the kingdom of grace).",[]],"baum:435":["Mechanical philosophy (*philosophia mechanica*) is that which explains the phenomena of bodies from their mechanism. Fate (*fatum*) from the mechanism of bodies is physico-mechanical (*physicomechanicum*) (physical or mechanical simply). But if any event of the world is determined through it, that event is in itself contingent, §. 361, 354, and necessary only hypothetically, §. 382, 105.",[]],"baum:436":["The most perfect world (*mundus perfectissimus*) is that in which the greatest of very many parts, and very many of the greatest parts, compossible in the world, agree toward one [end] to the extent that can come about in a world. Hence the most perfect world has a maximally composite perfection, and any world to which only a simple perfection belongs is not the most perfect, §. 185.",[]],"baum:437":["If the most perfect world is posited, the highest perfection that is possible in a world is posited, §. 436. Hence the most perfect world is also the best of all possible worlds, §. 187. Now the parts of the world are actual things (*actualia*), §. 354, and these are either simultaneous or successive, §. 306. Therefore the most perfect world embraces 1) as many simultaneous things, 2) as many successive things, and 3) things as great, as are compossible in the best world; that is, 1) extensively, 2) protensively, 3) intensively, it is the best and greatest of worlds, §. 436, 368.",[]],"baum:438":["If even only two contingent things, posited outside one another, are compossible in the world, whether as simultaneous or as successive, and the perfection of one of them detracts from the perfection of the other either nothing or not so much as it adds to the perfection of the whole, then the egoistic world (*mundus egoisticus*), such as the egoist posits this one to be, is not the most perfect. If there is even a single non-intellectual monad, possible in itself and compossible in the world with spirits, whose perfection detracts from the perfection of the spirits either nothing or not so much as it adds to the perfection of the whole, then the idealistic world (*mundus idealisticus*), such as the idealist posits this one to be, is not the most perfect, §. 437.",[]],"baum:439":["The materialist denies that there is a monad in this world, §. 395, and hence forms for himself an impossible world, §. 394. Therefore the materialistic world (*mundus materialisticus*), such as the materialist feigns this one to be, is neither any world at all nor the most perfect, §. 436.",[]],"baum:440":["The most perfect world is also finite, §. 372, and all its parts, even the most perfect, are finite, §. 354, 257. Therefore neither it nor anything in it is infinite in reality (*realiter*), §. 248. Yet not only can it itself and many of its parts be mathematically infinite for many reasons, but it itself is also, for all who either cannot or do not wish to determine the limits of the greatest contingent being, [a world] of mathematically infinite extension, protension and intension, or such, that is, indefinite intensively, extensively and protensively, §. 248, 437.",[]],"baum:441":["In the most perfect world there is the greatest universal connection (*nexus universalis*) that is possible in a world, §. 437, 94, [and] harmony and agreement, §. 436, 357.",[]],"baum:442":["Since every world is perfect, §. 360, the most imperfect world (*mundus imperfectissimus*) is that in which there is the least perfection that can be in a world, [that is] the least agreement of the fewest least things in one least thing, §. 354, 185, and hence a simple [perfection], §. 96, without exceptions, §. 97. The most imperfect world is the least, §. 368.",[]],"baum:443":["Even if the most perfect world is posited as the best of worlds, §. 437, it is not on that account posited as the highest good, or the metaphysical optimum (*optimum metaphysicum*). For even if there are posited in it the greatest number of realities that are compossible in any world, there are not thereby posited the absolutely greatest number of the greatest realities, §. 190, still less absolutely necessary ones, since the existence of each is a mode, §. 361. Even if the greatest degree of reality attainable in a given universe is ascribed to the most perfect world, that degree will in a contingent being be nothing but a limit, §. 248, 250, and is quite distinct, §. 38, from the absolutely greatest, necessary and immutable degree of reality of that which is really infinite, §. 251.",[]],"baum:444":["In all perfection there is order (*ordo*), §. 95. Hence in the most perfect world there is the greatest order that is possible in a world, §. 437, 175. Therefore there are very many common rules of perfection, e.g. the more, the greater, the more spacious, §. 280, the more lasting, §. 299, other things being equal, the better, §. 437, 187; and the order is maximally composite, §. 183, yet in such a way that the lower and higher rules can all finally be known as reduced to one, [derived] from one highest rule of perfection, which is also the strongest, §. 182, 185.",[]],"baum:445":["In the most perfect world, since there are very many rules of perfection, §. 444, there can also be quite many exceptions, §. 97, 372, provided they do not remove the greatest agreement, §. 440. Hence, other things being equal, they are the fewest that can be, §. 161, and the least, §. 186.",[]],"baum:446":["If in the most perfect world a sufficient and an insufficient reason of perfection collide, the exception is made from the insufficient one; if a more fruitful and a less fruitful, the exception is made from the less fruitful; if a weightier and a less weighty, from the less weighty; if a more remote one and a nearer one subordinated to the more remote, from the nearer; if one sufficient in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) and one sufficient simply (*simpliciter*), from the one sufficient in a certain respect; if a lower and a higher, from the lower. Whatever rule of perfection in the most perfect world finally collides with the highest, is set aside as an exception by the latter, §. 186, 445.",[]],"baum:447":["Of the most perfect world there hold not only §. 361-376, 354, 436, but also those things are furthest from it which §. 380-388, 247, 354 teach to be absent from every world. E.g. Spinozistic fate (*fatum spinosisticum*), §. 382, is so far from it that contingency and mutability, indefinite extensively, intensively and protensively, even hypothetical [contingency], §. 104, 127, are rather to be ascribed to it, §. 440, 437.",[]],"baum:448":["Since in this world there is harmony (*harmonia*), §. 356, and universal influx (*influxus universalis*), §. 408, this [influx] will be either everywhere real, or everywhere ideal, or at times real and at times ideal, §. 212. The harmony of the monads of the world, flowing into one another mutually, is the commerce of worldly substances (*commercium*)@@1@@. Therefore the substances of this whole world also are in universal commerce, §. 357. Opinions that seem apt to explain this are called systems for explaining the commerce between the substances of the world@@2@@; and if they are applied to explaining all the commerce of all [substances], they are called universal (*universalia*)@@3@@, cf. §. 761 (general). Changes of the parts of the world placed in commerce are harmonic (*harmonicae*)@@4@@ when, among those occurring in one part of the commerce, the sufficient ground can be known from the force of another part of the commerce. The ideal influx of all the substances of the world into one another is universal pre-established harmony (*harmonia praestabilita universalis*)@@5@@, cf. §. 462; and he who posits it in this world is a universal harmonist (*harmonista universalis*), whose system is called the system of universal pre-established harmony@@6@@.",[]],"baum:449":["The system of universal pre-established harmony (*systema harmoniae praestabilitae universalis*) does not abolish the influx of the substances of this world into one another, but posits it, §. 448. It does not abolish [the fact] that one substance of the world suffers (*pati*) from another, §. 448, 212; but it posits that each change which a substance suffers from another substance of this world it nevertheless produces by its own force, §. 210. It does not abolish the mutual conflict of the parts of this world, §. 213, nor the faculty and receptivity of transeunt actions, §. 217, much less the influx of the infinite substance and the receptivity of that influx in finite [substances], §. 448. It not only does not abolish [the possibility] that spirits can act on bodies and bodies on spirits, but even posits that in this world bodies and spirits mutually influence one another, §. 408, 434, and can mutually touch one another, §. 223, 409.",[]],"baum:450":["The real influx of a substance, a part of the world, into another part of the world is physical (*physicus*). Hence universal physical influx (*influxus physicus universalis*) is the universal harmony of substances in the world by which one really flows into another; and he who posits it in this world is a universal influxionist (*influxionista universalis*). His system is the system of universal physical influx.",[]],"baum:451":["The system of universal physical influx does not abolish the mutual harmony of the substances of the world, and indeed a universal one, §. 450, 48, but [abolishes] a pre-established one, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the substances of this world flow into one another, conflict with one another, suffer from one another, and that each can [act on] bodies in respect of spirits and spirits in respect of bodies, indeed that they can mutually touch one another, is a universal influxionist, §. 449, 450. The universal influxionist (1) denies that any substance of this world, when it suffers from another substance of this world, acts and produces its passions by its own force, §. 450, 212; (2) posits that the passions of one substance of this world are really produced by another substance of this world flowing into it, §. 450. Hence, according to the system of universal physical influx, no substance, [being] a part of the world, acts by its own force in any of its harmonic changes, §. 448. Now all the changes of a worldly substance can be known sufficiently [*sufficientur*, sic; read *sufficienter*] from the force of any other monad belonging to the same world, §. 354, 400. Therefore all are harmonic, §. 448; and hence, according to the system of universal physical influx, no worldly substance acts in any of its changes, being in all really passive under other worldly substances, which, for the same reason, would not act at all, §. 210, and hence would not be forces (*vires*) either, §. 197, q.c., §. 199.",[]],"baum:452":["He who posits that in all the substances of this world which seem to suffer from other substances of this world, and which by his own opinion indeed really suffer, only the infinite substance really flows in, is a universal occasionalist (*occasionalista universalis*) (assistentian), and his system is the universal system of occasional causes (*systema causarum occasionalium universale*)@@1@@ (the Cartesian, or rather the Malebranchian, of assistance). It contradicts §. 400, 408, and hence is false. The same is evident from this, that by occasionalism no harmonic change in any worldly substance belongs to that substance itself, but only to the action of the infinite substance, §. 448. Hence this system also abolishes all force and energy [*energian*, sic] in finite [substances], §. 451.",[]],"baum:453":["The system of occasional causes, if not harmony, at any rate abolishes the true universal mutual influx of the substances of this world, §. 452, 448: this [influx] as physical, §. 450, and as ideal, §. 212 (unless you so call it imaginary, apparent, §. 12); that [harmony] as pre-established, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the infinite substance really flows into all the passions of finite substances, nor everyone who posits that the infinite substance alone really flows into them, is a universal occasionalist; but [he is one] who posits (1) that all the passions of finite substances are real, and (2) that they depend on the influx of the infinite substance alone, §. 452.",[]],"baum:454":["The systems of universal pre-established harmony and of universal physical influx are alike in this, §. 70: (1) that, being universal, neither of them abolishes the mutual influx of the substances of this world, nor harmony, nor the real influx of the infinite substance into them, §. 448, 450; (2) that they posit that the passions of the substances of this world are actions of some finite substance. They are unlike in this, that the latter excludes the suffering substance from action, the former does not exclude it, §. 449, 451.",[]],"baum:455":["The universal systems of pre-established harmony and of occasional causes are alike in this, that they abolish the real influx of a substance of this world into another, a part of the same [world], §. 70. They are unlike in this, §. 70, that the former posits as ideal certain of their passions which the latter posits as real, §. 449, 453.",[]],"baum:456":["The universal systems of physical influx and of occasional causes are alike in this, that both posit the passions of one substance in this world as real [and as coming] from another part of the same world. They are unlike in this, §. 70, that the former abolishes [the doctrine] that the infinite substance alone flows into them, the latter posits it, §. 451, 452.",[]],"baum:457":["Universal systems for explaining the commerce of worldly substances are called simple (*simplicia*)@@1@@ if they explain the single commerces of the single substances of the world in a similar manner. Therefore the universal systems of pre-established harmony, of physical influx and of occasional causes are simple systems, §. 448, 456. Universal systems are composite (*composita*)@@2@@ which explain certain commerces of certain substances of the world in a different manner from others.",[]],"baum:458":["Besides the universal systems of pre-established harmony, of physical influx and of occasional causes, a fourth simple universal system is not possible, §. 457, 448, 452; the composite ones can be infinite, or rather indefinite, in number, §. 457, 248.",[]],"baum:459":["By pre-established harmony, [that is,] by the ideal influx of several substances of the world placed in commerce into one another, a greater connection (*nexus*) is actualized than by their physical influx, §. 167. For in physical influx the passion of the really suffering [substance] does not have its sufficient ground in the forces of the suffering [substance], §. 450, 212. In pre-established harmony the passion of the suffering substance has its sufficient ground (1) in its own forces, (2) in the ideally influencing substance, §. 449. Hence the influencing substance in pre-established harmony is equally fruitful as in physical influx, but the suffering substance is more fruitful in pre-established harmony than in physical influx, §. 166.",[]],"baum:460":["By the influx of the infinite substance, ascribed to it alone, into substances placed in commerce and really suffering, [in respect of] whatever they suffer, the suffering substance is not more fruitful than by physical influx, §. 453; and the other, finite part of the commerce is still less fruitful, §. 453, 166. Therefore by pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance a greater connection is actualized in the substances placed in commerce than by the influx ascribed to the infinite substance alone, §. 167.",[]],"baum:461":["In the most perfect world the connection of the possibles in the world is greatest, §. 441. Therefore the substances, such and as many as can [be so], are connected in it by pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance, §. 460, 459.",[]],"baum:462":["If universal pre-established harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance is possible in the most perfect world, that [world] is such as the universal harmonist posits it to be, §. 448, 461, and not such as one who is about to adopt some composite system may conceive; on the other hand, every particular pre-established harmony@@1@@, i.e. of certain parts of the world, is to be admitted in it, §. 154.",[]],"baum:463":["Since the condition *sine qua non* of universal pre-established harmony is its possibility in the most perfect world, this has to be demonstrated by one who is about to demonstrate pre-established harmony, by §. 462. Otherwise it is evident thus: from any monad of any world the single parts of the world to which it belongs can be known, §. 400, and therefore the single worldly changes also, §. 354, 155. Such, however, are all the passions of any worldly monad which it suffers from another monad of its world, §. 210. But the suffering monad is a force, §. 199. Therefore that very monad suffering from another worldly monad is the ground of this its passion and of its single parts, §. 354, 155, hence the sufficient ground of this its passion, §. 14, 21, and so its given passion is at the same time the action of the suffering [monad], §. 210. Therefore all the passions of the monads of any world, which they suffer from other worldly monads, are only ideal, §. 212. Therefore such also is always the influx of a worldly monad into another, never real, never physical, §. 450. Hence in this world also all its substances are in universal commerce only by pre-established harmony, §. 448, none ever by physical influx. The system of universal pre-established harmony is the true opinion; therefore every particular pre-established harmony also is true, §. 462, and all physical influx, both universal and particular, and its system, §. 450, and the system of occasional causes, §. 452, are false.",[]],"baum:464":["In the most perfect world the connection is the greatest that is possible in a world, §. 441. Therefore, if there are bodies and spirits in the most perfect world, §. 438, the mechanical, pneumatic and pneumatico-mechanical connection in it is greatest, §. 441 (the greatest harmony of the realm of nature and of grace, §. 434, 403, [being] pre-established, §. 463).",[]],"baum:465":["A dualist (*dualista*) is one who posits that this world consists of spirits and of bodies outside themselves. Hence the most perfect world, [being] neither egoistic nor idealistic, §. 438, is such as the dualist conceives it, §. 439.",[]],"baum:466":["The complex of natures in the single parts of the world, and in those parts taken together, is universal nature (*natura universa*; *naturata*, cf. §. 859). Hence the nature of this most perfect universe is the aggregate or complex of all essentials, essences, faculties, receptivities and forces with which all its parts (monads, elements, spirits, matters, bodies) are equipped, §. 430. Hence every mode of composition of all the bodies in it, every force of inertia, every motive force, and the mechanism, is a part of universal nature, §. 431, 433.",[]],"baum:467":["The essence of the world and its mode of composition of the composite, §. 226, together with the essences of its single parts, is only a quite small part (§. 161) of universal nature, §. 466, 155.",[]],"baum:468":["Universal nature has mutable internal parts, §. 466, and the universal nature of the most perfect world has very many of them, §. 437. Hence the universal nature of any world is a contingent (*contingens*) and finite being, §. 131, 255. Indeed, the universal nature of the most perfect world has a mathematically threefold infinite contingency, §. 447.",[]],"baum:469":["An event to be actualized by the nature of any contingent being is natural (*naturalis*), as contradistinguished from the supernatural (*supernaturale*), cf. §. 474. But an event to be actualized by the determinate nature of a determinate contingent being is natural as contradistinguished from the preternatural (*praeternaturale*), cf. §. 474. Whatever can be actualized by the nature of something is physically possible (*physice possibile*) for it; whatever cannot be so actualized is physically impossible (*physice impossibile*) for it. Some things possible in themselves cannot nevertheless be actualized by just any nature, §. 15, 430; therefore they can be physically impossible for many. Not everything physically impossible for some is also absolutely such. Things physically impossible for some contingent being are such either in whatever state its nature is considered, and are then naturally impossible simply (*simpliciter*; merely, wholly, altogether), or only when its nature is considered in a certain state, and are then naturally impossible in a certain respect (*secundum quid*; for the present, and thus). The natural impossibility simply of such realities is merely natural impotence (*impotentia mere naturalis*). Not every natural impossibility is merely natural impotence or absolute impossibility, §. 467. The opposite of the physically impossible is the physically necessary (*physice necessarium*); the opposite of the physically possible is the physically contingent (*physice contingens*). Not everything physically necessary is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Some things contingent in themselves can be physically necessary for many, §. 104. Things physically necessary are such either simply or in a certain respect.",[]],"baum:470":["What is natural for a certain body is what is to be actualized by its determinate nature. For a certain corporeal world it is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the bodies of that world, §. 466. What is natural for a certain contingent spirit is to be actualized through its determinate nature. What is natural for a certain pneumatic world is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the spirits of that world, §. 469.",[]],"baum:471":["The succession of natural things in the world is the course of nature (*cursus naturae*). Hence the course of nature is opposed to the succession either of supernatural or of preternatural things, §. 469. It is considered either in some body, or in some whole corporeal world, or in some spirit, or in some whole pneumatic world, or in universal nature, §. 470, 466.",[]],"baum:472":["The order of natural things in the world is the order of nature (*ordo naturae*). It is opposed to the conjunction either of supernatural or of preternatural things, and is considered either in a certain body only, or corporeal world, or spirit, or pneumatic world, §. 469, 470, or in universal nature, §. 466. Each of these orders has its own common norms, §. 86. Therefore in this world there are common laws and rules of the natural order, diverse, §. 38, yet similar, §. 70. A complex of similar laws is a body of law (*ius*@@1@@), cf. §. 971 (broadly speaking). The complex of the laws of the order of nature is the broadest law of nature (*ius naturae*); its parts are the laws and rules of motion, §. 432, and the laws of the nature of spirits.",[]],"baum:473":["If the natural follows the natural, it happens according to the course of nature, and that according to the course of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain nexus in a certain material world, or of a certain spirit, or also of a certain nexus in a certain pneumatic world, §. 471, or at least according to the course of universal nature, §. 466. If it succeeds or coexists with the natural, it happens according to the order of nature, and that according to the order of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain nexus in a certain material world, or of a certain spirit, or also of a certain nexus in a certain pneumatic world, §. 472, or at least according to the order of universal nature, §. 466. Therefore the natural, succeeding and coexisting with natural things, is actualized according to natural laws, §. 472. What is natural for a certain body, or a certain nexus in the material world, is actualized according to the laws and rules of motion through the mechanism of bodies, §. 433, and hence can be explained mechanically, §. 435, 433.",[]],"baum:474":["An event of the world actualized by the nature of no contingent being is supernatural (*supernaturalis*). An event not actualized by the determinate nature of a certain contingent being in which it occurs is, with respect to that being, preternatural (*praeternaturalis*). Hence supernatural things are preternatural with respect to universal nature, and things preternatural with respect to universal nature are supernatural, §. 466. A supernatural event, in so far as it is regarded as extraordinary, is a miracle (*miraculum*@@1@@). Hence a miracle is preternatural with respect to universal nature. Every miracle is supernatural, but not everything supernatural is referred to miracles.",[]],"baum:475":["Supernatural things and miracles are the opposites of natural things and of things naturally ordinary, §. 81, 474. Now natural things and things naturally ordinary are contingent in every world, §. 354, 257. Therefore supernatural things and miracles are possible, §. 101.",[]],"baum:476":["What is actualized through the nature of a contingent being in the world is neither supernatural nor a miracle, §. 474. Hence what is actualized through the nature of a spirit, which is a part of the world, is neither supernatural nor a miracle, §. 354, 257.",[]],"baum:477":["Actions of finite spirits upon other parts of the world, not actualized through the determinate nature of the patients (and hence preternatural with respect to the patients, §. 474, and extraordinary, §. 472, 384), are nevertheless neither supernatural nor miracles, §. 474. Hence if they are called comparative miracles (*miracula comparativa*@@1@@), comparative miracles are not miracles; the latter, in contradistinction from them, are called strict (*rigorosa*@@2@@). If such actions are posited as above nature, this can be conceded only with respect to the particular nature of a certain body or corporeal world, §. 470.",[]],"baum:478":["An extraordinary event whose natural cause we do not know is a prodigy (*prodigium*@@1@@) (a sign, cf. §. 347; an omen, a portent). Prodigies are either strict miracles, §. 474, 477, or natural events, which are miracles as far as we are concerned (*quoad nos*@@2@@).",[]],"baum:479":["Since supernatural things and miracles are not actualized through universal nature, §. 474, 466, and are nevertheless contingent beings, §. 474, 354, and hence effects, §. 334, they have, when they exist, as their efficient cause an extramundane being, §. 388, to which they bear witness, §. 333.",[]],"baum:480":["A non-natural event succeeding natural things happens against the course of nature (*contra cursum naturae*), §. 471, 473. Now the supernatural and a miracle is not a natural event, §. 474, 469. Therefore the supernatural and a miracle occurs against the course of nature, succeeding natural things, and indeed against the course of universal nature, §. 471. What is preternatural for a certain body, or a certain corporeal world, a certain spirit, or a certain pneumatic world, occurs against only some course of nature, §. 474, 471. Hence not everything that happens against any course of nature whatever is supernatural or a miracle, §. 474.",[]],"baum:481":["Supernatural things and miracles are not absolutely extraordinary, §. 475, 385, nor against the whole order of the world, §. 359, 7; yet neither are they naturally ordinary, §. 472, 474. And since a non-natural event coexisting with or succeeding natural things happens against the order of nature, supernatural things and miracles are against the whole order of universal nature. Therefore they do not happen according to the common norms of a certain order of nature, §. 472. Therefore they are extraordinary relatively to the order of nature, §. 384. Supernatural things and miracles are extraordinary with respect to the order of universal nature, §. 472. The preternatural is extraordinary with respect to a certain body, or corporeal world, a certain spirit, or pneumatic nexus, and the order of nature determined in them, §. 474, 472, so that it happens against this order, §. 384. Therefore not everything extraordinary with respect to any order of nature whatever, and not everything that is against any order of nature whatever, is supernatural, much less a miracle, §. 474.",[]],"baum:482":["The law of order in the most perfect world, §. 84, and that one of the higher laws, §. 182, is this: Let the best of the compossibles (*compossibilia*) in the most perfect world be joined with the best, §. 444. This law is called the law of the best in the world (*lex optimi in mundo*), cf. §. 822. Subordinate to it is the law of the best in nature: let the best of natural things in the most perfect world be joined with the best, §. 469, 444.",[]],"baum:483":["Posit in the most perfect world a natural event, hence one that is to happen according to the order of nature, §. 473, and compossible with the rest. Posit under the same circumstances a supernatural event, hence one that is to happen against the whole order of nature, §. 481, compossible with the rest but better than the natural. Since opposite events, the natural and the supernatural (e.g. the miraculous), cannot exist at once, §. 81, in this case the rules of the order of universal nature and the higher rule of perfection, §. 482, will collide, §. 97.",[]],"baum:484":["Since the rules of the order of nature, both particular and universal, are in the most perfect world inferior and subordinate to the law of the best in the world, §. 182, 482, in the case posited by §. 483 an exception will be made to the laws of the order of nature, §. 446, and something supernatural, indeed a miracle, will occur, in the fullest accord with the highest order of the most perfect world, §. 483, 444.",[]],"baum:485":["Supernatural things and miracles by an absolute leap (*saltus absolutus*) would be impossible, §. 387; hence no supernatural things and no miracles occur by an absolute leap, §. 475. But since the proximate sufficient reason of events in the world that are naturally ordinary is the nature of some contingent being, §. 472, 469, when supernatural things and miracles occur, they occur by a relative leap (*saltus respectivus*), §. 474, 386.",[]],"baum:486":["Supernatural things and miracles occurring in the corporeal world cannot be explained mechanically, §. 435, 433, much less those occurring in the pneumatic world (the kingdom of grace), §. 433, 403.",[]],"baum:487":["If a supernatural and miraculous event is posited in the world in place of a natural event, the world is not wholly the same as it would be if the natural event were a part of it, §. 267, 155. Therefore, when the supernatural or a miracle is posited in the world in place of a natural effect, there is posited a state of the world which, with the supernatural given or the miracle coexisting, is partially other than it would be had the supernatural or miracle not been wrought, §. 378, 369.",[]],"baum:488":["Nothing whatever is barren (*sterile*), §. 23; hence nothing supernatural (*supernaturale*) and no miracle (*miraculum*) is without a corollary (*corollarium*), that is, a consequence (*rationatum*). When this is posited, something is again posited, §. 14, hence its consequence in turn, §. 23, and so on. Therefore the consequences of every supernatural event and of every miracle are posited indefinitely, §. 248, through the states of the world that succeed one another after the supernatural event, after the miracle, §. 30, 369.",[]],"baum:489":["Suppose that, in place of the supernatural event or miracle, a natural event had been posited in the world in the case mentioned in §. 487, 488. This too would have had its consequences indefinitely through all the states of the world succeeding it, §. 23, 369. But the consequences of a natural effect and of a supernatural or miraculous one cannot be wholly the same, §. 267, 38. Therefore, if the supernatural or the miracle is posited in the world in place of a natural effect, all the states of the world that succeed the supernatural event or miracle are in part other than they would have been, had the supernatural or the miracle not been performed, §. 369.",[]],"baum:490":["In the system of occasionalism (*systema occasionalismi*) every passion (*passio*) of a finite substance is supernatural, §. 474, 453. Yet some passion can be conceived which is not a miracle, in so far as it is regarded as ordinary, §. 384.",[]],"baum:491":["Since the supernatural and the miracle are an action (*actio*), §. 474, 323, it will be either simple or composite, §. 215. And the composite will be either a simultaneous being, or a successive being, or both, §. 238. Both the simple and the composite, if only a simultaneous being, occur in an instant, destitute of prior and posterior parts, §. 300.",[]],"baum:492":["The more fecund and the nobler the supernatural and the miracle, §. 166, the greater it is, §. 491, 214. Hence the greatest will be the most fecund, the noblest, and the most composite, §. 214, 215.",[]],"baum:493":["A naturalist in the wider sense (*naturalista latius dictus*), cf. §. 999, is one who removes all supernatural events from this world. He therefore removes the existence of miracles in this world, §. 474. If he does so because he denies their possibility, he errs, §. 475.",[]],"baum:494":["Given that the nature of this world, as it is, is of mathematically infinite extension (*extensio*), intension (*intensio*) and protension (*protensio*), §. 440, 466, it is also of infinite contingency of that kind, §. 468, 361. It cannot exist except as the effect of an extramundane cause, to which it bears witness, §. 375, just as supernatural events and miracles, when they exist, [bear witness to such a cause], §. 479. Hence one who does not confound the limits of his theory of the universal nature of this world with the limits of that nature itself, and who does not conclude confidently from his mere ignorance of a natural cause to the outright denial of such a cause, is not thereby injurious to the cause common to universal nature and to supernatural events and miracles, §. 375, 479, nor is he a naturalist, §. 493.",[]],"baum:495":["Suppose a natural event and a supernatural event in the most perfect world (*mundus perfectissimus*), each compossible under the same circumstances with the remaining parts of the world, but equally good as regards their consequences. The natural event will satisfy the law of the best in the world (*lex optimi*) equally as well as the supernatural, §. 482, and at the same time the rules of perfection which the order of nature prescribes, §. 472, hence more rules than the supernatural does, §. 481. In this case, therefore, nothing supernatural, and no miracle, will occur in the most perfect world, §. 482, 445.",[]],"baum:496":["Since the universal nature even of the most perfect world is finite, §. 468, it is not a sufficient principle of all the accidents that can inhere in the best world, §. 430, 259. Suppose, then, an event compossible with the rest in the best world, the best of the best, but not to be actualised naturally and ordinarily; and suppose that the same event can be actualised supernaturally and extraordinarily. Then in this case also, as in §. 484, something supernatural will occur, indeed a miracle, most fitting to the order of the most perfect world, §. 482, 444.",[]],"baum:497":["As many and as great goods as can occur naturally and ordinarily in the best world, occur in it naturally, §. 495. As many and as great goods as either cannot occur in it naturally and ordinarily at all, or cannot occur equally well so, occur in it as the best supernaturally and miraculously, §. 484, 496. The former [kind] can be called supplementary miracles (*miracula supplentia*)@@1@@, the latter emendatory (*emendantia*)@@2@@. The reasoning is the same for supernatural events.",[]],"baum:498":["The number of supernatural events and miracles in the most perfect world is as great as the number of its possibles which cannot be equally well actualised naturally and according to the order of nature, §. 159, 497; it is neither greater nor less, §. 160.",[]],"baum:499":["If any supernatural event in the best world is ever better than the natural, also as regards its consequences, that is, if any possible event of the best world is naturally impossible through universal nature, then the most perfect world is not such as the naturalist in the more general sense holds this world to be, §. 498, 493.",[]],"baum:500":["Not everyone who denies that certain preternatural things (*praeternaturalia*), §. 474, are miracles in this world is a naturalist in the more general sense, §. 493. [The things in question are] extraordinary effects of finite spirits, §. 476, comparative miracles, §. 477, prodigies and miracles as regards us, §. 478, and events occurring against the course of nature, §. 480, and against some order, §. 481.",[]],"baum:501":["Psychology (*psychologia*) is the science of the general predicates of the soul (*anima*).",[]],"baum:502":["Psychology, which contains the first principles of theologies, aesthetics, logic and the practical sciences, is rightly (*cum ratione*) referred, §. 501, §. 2, to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:503":["Psychology deduces its assertions either 1) more directly from experience, and [is then] empirical (*empirica*), or 2) from the notion of the soul by a longer series of ratiocinations, and [is then] rational (*rationalis*).",[]],"baum:700":["I undergo change (*mutor*), §. 505-699, internally, §. 126. Therefore I am a finite being, §. 254, and a contingent being, §. 257. Therefore my existence is a mode (*modus*), §. 134, and hence all my states are in themselves contingent, §. 205, 108. Therefore also the changes of all of them, §. 124, 125. Therefore all my actions and all my passions are in themselves contingent, §. 210. Hence also all my future actions, §. 298. Hence none of my actions and none of my passions is absolutely or intrinsically necessary, §. 105.",[]],"baum:701":["Necessitation (*necessitatio*)@@1@@ (coercion, *coactio*) is the change of something from the contingent into the necessary. Hence it is either active@@2@@, [the necessitation] of the necessitating [agent] (*necessitans*), which is properly only a substance, §. 198; or passive@@3@@, [the necessitation] of the necessitated, which is attributed both to accidents, whether action or passion, that are changed from contingent into necessary, and to the substance in which such necessitated accidents inhere.",[]],"baum:702":["A necessitation (coercion) would be absolute@@1@@ if it changed what is in itself contingent into the absolutely necessary. Now nothing can be changed into the absolutely necessary, §. 130. Therefore no action can be, and therefore no action of mine. Therefore absolute necessitation of any action of mine is impossible, §. 7. All my actions are, and always remain, in the act itself and after it, in themselves contingent; hence also their opposite is in itself possible, §. 700, 104.",[]],"baum:703":["Of the opposite of many of my actions there is a very great hypothetical possibility (*possibilitas hypothetica*), §. 168. Hence in many of my actions there is a very great contingency, hypothetical also, §. 188, 700. Hence my future [actions] are very contingent futures, §. 298.",[]],"baum:704":["An action depending on a sufficient principle which is internal to the agent is spontaneous (*spontanea*)@@1@@. Hence spontaneity (*spontaneitas*) is attributed 1) to an action which depends on a sufficient principle internal to the agent, and 2) to a substance performing such actions. Now every action properly so called depends on a principle internal to the agent, §. 210, 37. Therefore every action properly so called is spontaneous. Since, however, changes composed of actions and passions are sometimes named actions from the predominant part, in so far as they are passions they are conceived as not spontaneous, §. 210.",[]],"baum:705":["Many of my actions, indeed all those properly so called, and hence the actions of my soul, are spontaneous. Accordingly spontaneity is truly attributed both to them and to my soul, §. 704. If an automaton (*automaton*) is defined as that which changes itself, the soul will be an automaton.",[]],"baum:706":["Spontaneity is least if the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices for only one least action, §. 704, 161. Therefore, the more numerous and the greater the actions for which the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices, the greater is its spontaneity, until it is greatest in that agent in which the principle suffices for the most and the greatest actions. A very great spontaneity belongs to my soul, §. 705.",[]],"baum:707":["External necessitation (*necessitatio externa*)@@1@@ (coercion from without) is that which depends on a force existing outside the necessitated substance, and is either ideal or real, §. 701, 212. An action externally and really necessitated would not be spontaneous, nor properly an action, §. 704, but a real passion, §. 210. Real external necessitation is external necessitation (coercion) simply so called (*simpliciter talis*)@@2@@. Therefore actions coerced by external necessitation simply so called would be real passions. A substance and actions that are not absolutely necessitated are free (cf. §. 719) from absolute coercion@@3@@. A substance and actions that are not necessitated by external coercion simply so called are free from external coercion simply so called@@4@@. Therefore all my changes are free from absolute coercion, §. 702; and all my actions, all the spontaneous actions of my soul, that is, all actions properly so called, §. 704, and my soul in so far as it acts spontaneously, are free from external coercion simply so called.",[]],"baum:708":["Actions physically possible to me are placed in my power (*in potestate mea positae*)@@1@@; realities physically impossible to me are placed outside my power@@2@@. Therefore there is some action placed in my power either simply only, or also in a certain respect (*secundum quid*); and there is some [action] placed outside my power either also simply, or only in a certain respect, §. 469. Of actions placed in the power of some agent, the opposite is either also placed in the power of the same agent, or outside it, §. 9; and each of these again either simply or in a certain respect, §. 469. Those [actions] which, together with their opposites, are at least simply placed in someone's power are free to him (cf. §. 719) as regards execution@@3@@; those whose opposite is simply placed beyond someone's power are to him merely natural. A free action as regards execution, whose opposite has an equal physical possibility with respect to a given agent, is physically indifferent to him@@4@@ (indifferent, as regards the exercise of the act).",[]],"baum:709":["Merely natural actions are naturally necessary. Hence actions free as regards execution, being contradistinguished from these, are physically contingent, §. 708, 469. Hence future [actions] are sometimes called future contingents simply (*futura contingentia simpliciter*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:710":["Internal necessitation (*necessitatio interna*)@@1@@ (compulsion, *coactio*) is that which depends on the internal determination of a necessitated substance. It is ascribed to substances and their actions, 1) when the actions of substances are thought to be necessitated by their essence alone: absolute@@2@@ (essential) [necessitation]. Such necessitation, since it would change actions into absolutely necessary ones, §. 702, 107, is altogether impossible, §. 702. 2) When, through the nature of a substance, certain actions that are otherwise physically contingent are changed into physically necessary ones, being such for that nature either simply or in a certain respect, §. 469: physical (natural) [necessitation]. Hence merely natural actions can be said to be necessitated by internal physical compulsion, §. 709, 708. Actions and substances that are internally compelled in neither of these senses are free (cf. §. 719) from internal necessitation, both absolute and physical@@3@@. Now actions free in respect of execution, and the substances which, and in so far as, they perform such actions, are internally compelled in neither of these senses, §. 709, 708. Therefore they are free from internal compulsion, both absolute and physical.",[]],"baum:711":["Many of my actions, many spontaneous actions of my soul, are free in respect of execution, §. 708. Therefore freedom from external compulsion simply so called, §. 707, and from internal compulsion, both absolute and physical, §. 710, belongs both to these actions themselves and to the soul in so far as it performs them.",[]],"baum:712":["Pleasure (*lubitus*)@@1@@ is a knowledge possessed by a substance, from which, according to the laws of desire and aversion, it can be known why the substance determines itself thus and not otherwise with regard to a free action in respect of execution. But this can be known from foresight, presentiment, pleasure or displeasure, §. 665, and from stimuli and motives, §. 677, 690. Therefore the foresight, presentiment, pleasure or displeasure, stimuli and motives that are known by a given substance constitute its pleasure [*lubitus*]. If a substance, with regard to a free action in respect of execution, so determines its power as can be known from its pleasure, it desires or turns away from [something] according to pleasure@@2@@. Whoever, then, should desire or turn away from something unforeseen, or from something which he had in no way presentiment would come about by any effort of his, or from something neither pleasing nor displeasing, without any stimuli and any motives, would not desire and would not turn away according to pleasure. I desire many things and turn away from many things according to my own pleasure. Therefore I have the faculty of desiring and turning away according to my pleasure, that is, choice (*arbitrium*)@@3@@. The actions which it lies within the power of some substance to determine through choice are for it arbitrary (*arbitrariae*)@@4@@. Many of my actions are arbitrary.",[]],"baum:713":["I desire or turn away willingly (*lubenter*)@@1@@ 1) whatever I desire or turn away from according to pleasure; and then I desire nothing and turn away from nothing unwillingly or against my will (*illubenter*, *invitus*)@@2@@, §. 712, 665; 2) if the pleasure contains either mere displeasure, or pure pleasure, or an immense preponderance. Then I desire or turn away against my will (unwillingly, contrary to pleasure) where there is no very great preponderance in the pleasure, that is, where many and great [considerations] also seem to urge towards the opposite of what I desire or turn away from. In this last sense, although I desire or turn away against my will, I do so according to pleasure; and in this sense an action done against the will is nevertheless arbitrary, §. 712.",[]],"baum:714":["Since compulsion in the strict sense@@1@@, cf. §. 701, is the production of an action against the will, a compelled or involuntary action through compulsion taken simply as external@@2@@ would be one which I should perform according to no pleasure, contrary to all pleasure, through external compulsion taken simply; but this would not be an action properly so called, §. 707. If I do something against my will according to pleasure, in the sense of §. 713, the preponderance of what I desire or turn away from is considered either as produced by myself, and I am said to have compelled myself@@3@@, or as produced by another outside me, §. 22; and the action is said to be involuntary or compelled through external compulsion in a certain respect@@4@@ (mixed, namely [compounded] of the arbitrary and of the involuntary through external compulsion taken simply).",[]],"baum:715":["Involuntary actions, to which I am said to compel myself or to be externally compelled in a certain respect by others, come about according to my pleasure, §. 714; hence they are arbitrary, §. 712. They are called necessitated in so far as they are conceived as less necessary, [namely] if either I myself or others had not produced the preponderance that produced them, §. 701, 188.",[]],"baum:716":["Actions are called involuntary through ignorance or error@@1@@ when I should not have performed them according to pleasure unless I had been ignorant of something or had erred. Since these nevertheless come about according to pleasure, they are also arbitrary, §. 712.",[]],"baum:717":["Choice would be least if it determined only a single action according to the least pleasure, §. 161. Therefore the greater the pleasure according to which, and the more numerous and the greater the actions which, it determines, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is greatest, determining the greatest and most numerous actions from the greatest pleasure, §. 161, 712.",[]],"baum:718":["Since choice is the faculty of desiring or turning away according to one's own pleasure, §. 712, a substance endowed with choice will have either only the faculty of desiring or turning away sensitively, or only that of willing or refusing according to pleasure, or the faculty both of willing or refusing and of sensitively desiring or turning away according to its own pleasure, §. 676, 689.",[]],"baum:719":["The faculty of desiring or turning away sensitively according to one's own pleasure is sensitive choice (*arbitrium sensitivum*)@@1@@. The faculty of willing or refusing according to one's own pleasure is (free choice, *liberum arbitrium*) freedom (*libertas*)@@2@@, cf. §. 707, 708, 710 (moral freedom, so called simply). Freedom of purely willing or refusing is pure freedom (*libertas pura*)@@3@@. Therefore a substance endowed with choice will have either sensitive choice only, or pure freedom only, or the same mixed with sensitive choice, §. 718. Actions which it lies within the power of some substance to determine itself to through freedom are free (*liberae*)@@4@@, and the substance itself, which can perform free actions, and in so far as it can, is free (*libera*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:720":["I desire and turn away from many things sensitively according to my pleasure. Therefore I have sensitive choice, §. 216, 719. I will and refuse many things according to my pleasure. Therefore I have freedom, §. 216, 719. Many of my actions, many actions of my soul, and the soul in many of its actions, are free. Something sensitive is mingled with all my volitions and refusals, §. 692. Hence pure freedom does not belong to me; in my freest actions my freedom is mixed with sensitive choice, §. 719. Both sensitive and free choice are actuated through the representative power of the soul [representing] the universe according to the position of my body in it, §. 712, 667.",[]],"baum:721":["Voluntary actions (*actiones voluntariae*)@@1@@ are so called 1) [in the sense that] all actions are so called which are determined through the higher appetitive faculty; in that case those are involuntary (*involuntariae*)@@2@@ which are not determined through the higher appetitive faculty. In this sense all voluntary actions are free, but not all free actions are voluntary, §. 719. For suppose that I determine myself through sensitive choice where it lay within my power to determine myself through freedom; such an action will be involuntary and yet free, §. 719.",[]],"baum:722":["Voluntary actions (*actiones voluntariae*)@@1@@ are so called 2) [in the sense that] all actions are so called which are determined through the higher appetitive faculty in no way against the will. In that case those are involuntary (*involuntariae*)@@2@@ which I will against my will. In this sense all voluntary and involuntary actions are free actions, §. 719. But not all free actions are either voluntary or involuntary in this sense, §. 721.",[]],"baum:723":["The moral (*morale*), broadly speaking@@1@@, cf. §. 787, is more closely connected with freedom. Hence free determinations are moral@@2@@, the habits of free actions are moral@@3@@, the laws of moral determinations are moral@@4@@, the philosophy and theology that teach them are moral, and the state resulting from them is moral@@5@@. Hence the morally possible (*moraliter possibile*) is 1) in the broader sense, that which can come about only through freedom, or in a free substance as such@@6@@; 2) in the stricter sense, that which can come about only through freedom determined in conformity with moral laws, that is, the lawful (*licitum*)@@7@@. The morally impossible (*moraliter impossibile*) is 1) in the broader sense, that which cannot come about in a free substance on account of freedom alone@@8@@; 2) in the stricter sense, that which is impossible through freedom to be determined in conformity with moral laws, that is, the unlawful (*illicitum*)@@9@@. The morally necessary (*moraliter necessarium*) is that whose opposite is morally impossible; therefore 1) in the broader sense, that whose opposite is impossible only through freedom, or in a substance in so far as it is free@@10@@; 2) in the stricter sense, that whose opposite is unlawful@@11@@. Moral necessitation is obligation (*obligatio*)@@12@@. Obligation to an action against the will will be moral compulsion (*coactio moralis*)@@13@@.",[]],"baum:724":["Moral necessity, in neither sense, can exist where there is no freedom, §. 723. Therefore it does not remove freedom, nor is it its opposite, §. 81, but is its consequent or consequence (*rationatum*, *consectarium*), §. 14. Hence actions that are morally necessary, necessitated and compelled can not only be free, but are necessarily so, §. 723. Indeed, given a most universal moral law@@1@@, that is, one determining the free actions of all free substances, cf. §. 822, all free actions are morally necessary or unlawful, §. 723.",[]],"baum:725":["The least freedom is that which actuates only a single volition or refusal according to the least pleasure; therefore the more numerous and the greater [the volitions or refusals], and the greater the pleasure according to which it actuates them, the greater it is, until it is greatest, actuating the greatest and most numerous volitions or refusals according to the highest pleasure, §. 719. Therefore the more distinct the pleasure according to which I will or refuse something, the more freely I will or refuse it. Hence the more I am conscious of my motives, the more freely I will, §. 712. It is morally necessary that one who wills or refuses most freely should discern most distinctly the motives of willing or refusing, whenever he wills or does not will, §. 723.",[]],"baum:726":["This is the law of choice: of what is free in respect of execution, I desire what pleases, I turn away from what pleases. Hence the rule of freedom: of what is free in respect of execution, I will what pleases, I refuse what pleases. My free actions, while they are determined according to pleasure, are not necessitated by their impulsive causes, stimuli or motives, either by physical compulsion or by external [compulsion], §. 707; for stimuli and motives are my representations, §. 677, 690, hence my internal determinations, §. 37. Nor are my free actions necessitated by their impulsive causes through internal physical compulsion, since, the motives being posited, they are and remain free in respect of execution, §. 711, 710. Indeed, speaking accurately, motives and stimuli do not even act upon the soul, and hence do not compel it, §. 714, nor necessitate it morally, §. 723, 701, since they are nothing but accidents of my soul, §. 505.",[]],"baum:727":["If I am said to compel myself (*cogere me*), it lies in my power to determine myself by freedom (*libertas*) to that to which I compel myself; hence I compel myself freely, §. 714, 719. If I am externally compelled by another in a certain respect (*externe secundum quid*), I am determined by a liking (*lubitus*) which is conceived as produced by another posited outside me, §. 714. But if in that case it lay in my power to determine myself by freedom to that to which I am externally compelled in a certain respect, an action of this kind is nevertheless free, §. 719. Many of my actions, to which I compel myself or am externally compelled in a certain respect, are free.",[]],"baum:728":["External compulsions in a certain respect (*coactiones externae secundum quid*) will be: 1) productions of stimuli from pleasing things, allurements (*illecebrae*)@@1@@; 2) [productions of stimuli] from displeasing things, threats (*minae*)@@2@@; 3) [productions] of motives from pleasing things, persuasions (*suasiones*)@@3@@; 4) [productions of motives] from displeasing things, dissuasions (*dissuasiones*)@@4@@; 5) the actual bringing about of displeasing things, to be continued until the compeller can be certain of an overbalance (*superpondium*) following upon the unwilling action, extortion (*extorsio*)@@5@@, §. 714. Many of my actions, although I am compelled to them by threats and allurements, by persuasions and dissuasions, and even by extortions, are nevertheless free, §. 727.",[]],"baum:729":["By the system of pre-established harmony (*systema harmoniae praestabilitae*) all actions of the soul that are unwilling, [and] that would have to be produced by any finite being through external compulsion taken simply, are removed, §. 714, 449; and in the case of things compelled by external force in a certain respect, that very liking from which they arise, as really produced by another finite being outside the soul, is removed, §. 727, 449. And in so far as by that system the soul never suffers really from any finite being, nor in any of its free actions, total independence from the whole world and every part of it is in this sense ascribed to the soul, §. 354, 307.",[]],"baum:730":["My free volitions and nolitions (*volitiones nolitionesque*) are called elicited acts of the soul (*actus eliciti*); the free actions of the remaining faculties are commanded acts (*actus imperati*), and in so far as they depend on the freedom of the soul, command (*imperium*) over them is ascribed to it. Hence the soul's command over itself@@1@@ is the faculty of producing, according to a distinct liking, now the actions of this faculty, now of that, now the opposite of producing them. The greater, therefore, the freedom, the greater the command of the free being over itself, §. 725. A notable lack of command over oneself is moral servitude in the wide sense@@2@@. What tends to increase command over oneself is (ingenuous) liberal@@3@@; what promotes moral servitude is servile@@4@@.",[]],"baum:731":["If actions of the soul depend on freedom only through several intermediate acts, they are subject to it indirectly (mediately)@@1@@; but directly@@2@@ (immediately) subject are those which I perform or omit through freedom without several other observable intermediate acts. Actions which are subject to freedom even in the highest degree only indirectly are nevertheless free, §. 719.",[]],"baum:732":["The determinate proportion of the appetitive faculties among themselves in a certain subject is its disposition (*indoles*)@@1@@, either upright (*erecta*)@@2@@, when the higher faculties habitually dominate, or abject (*abiecta*)@@3@@, when the lower habitually dominate; the greatest of which is called the dominant passion@@4@@. And since a certain proportion of them is borne more easily to a certain kind of things desirable or to be shunned, various kinds of temperaments of the soul@@5@@ are hence constituted. Therefore the temperament of the soul can be much changed, and often by exercises and custom, §. 650, 577.",[]],"baum:733":["Many motions of my body depend on my choice (*arbitrium*), §. 14. Motions of the body that depend on a choice of which I am conscious are called arbitrary@@1@@, §. 712; and arbitrary motions depending on the higher faculty are voluntary@@2@@, §. 721. The rule of the soul over the body@@3@@ (*regimen animae in corpus*) is the dependence of the latter's motions on the former's choice. Hence my soul has rule over my body.",[]],"baum:734":["In the arbitrary and voluntary motions of my body it can be sufficiently known from the soul why an event in the body happens, and happens at this time and place rather than another, §. 733. Therefore the soul acts on the body, §. 210, and flows into it (*influit*), §. 211.",[]],"baum:735":["In the affects of the soul a motion conforming to them coexists in the body; since this can be sufficiently known from those same affects, it gives a new argument for the influence of the soul on the body, §. 734.",[]],"baum:736":["In external sensations it can be sufficiently known from the force of the body why a certain change occurs in the soul. Therefore the body acts on the soul, §. 210, and flows into it, §. 211. Therefore there is between my soul and my body a mutual influence (*influxus mutuus*), §. 734, 735, a mutual harmony, §. 14, and a commerce, §. 448.",[]],"baum:737":["I do not experience that the arbitrary motions of the body are sufficiently determined by the force proper to the body, or that sensations are sufficiently determined by the force proper to the soul. Whoever, therefore, assumes this major premiss: 'whatever I do not experience does not exist', will conclude from it: 'Therefore the arbitrary motions of the body are not sufficiently determined by the force proper to the body, nor external sensations by the force proper to the soul. Hence the body really flows into the soul, and the soul into the body, §. 212, by physical influence (*influxus physicus*), §. 450 (by experience).' If he holds this conclusion as a sensation, his fallacies of the senses (*fallaciae sensuum*) arise from it, a false major having been assumed, §. 548, through the vice of subreption (*vitium subreptionis*), §. 546.",[]],"baum:738":["The changes of the body depending on the soul, and of the soul depending on the body, are harmonic, §. 448. In the harmonic changes of soul and body, a change of the soul coexists with, or follows upon, a change of the body, and a change of the body coexists with, or follows upon, a change of the soul, §. 733-736. Whoever, therefore, assumes this major premiss: 'Of things that coexist or follow upon one another, the one really flows into the other', will conclude from it: 'Therefore in harmonic changes the body really flows into the soul, and the soul into the body (by experience).' If he holds this conclusion as a sensation, his fallacies of the senses arise from it, §. 548, through the vice of subreption, §. 546.",[]],"baum:739":["My soul and my body together constitute one [being], myself. Therefore they are united (*unita*) with each other, §. 73, 79. Their commerce, in so far as one man persists through it, is union (*unio*), §. 205; and since this is very great, §. 734-736, and as great as exists between my soul and no other body, §. 508, it is the closest (*artissima*).",[]],"baum:740":["The human soul@@1@@ is a soul which is in the closest commerce with a human body. And since a soul, together with the body with which it is in the closest commerce, constitutes an animal@@2@@, the human soul, together with the body with which it is in the closest commerce, constitutes the animal which we call man@@3@@.",[]],"baum:741":["The human soul represents its body to itself at its choice, §. 740. Therefore it acts, §. 210; it also moves its body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it desires and shuns, §. 712. Therefore it acts and is a representative force (*vis repraesentativa*) of its body, §. 210. The human body is matter, §. 296, hence divisible, §. 427, and so internally mutable, §. 244, and so a finite being, §. 255, actual, a part of the world, §. 354. From the position of the human body in the universe it can be known why the human soul represents these things obscurely, clearly, distinctly, and not others, §. 740, 736. Therefore the human soul@@1@@ is a representative force of the universe according to the position of the human body in it, §. 513, 155.",[]],"baum:742":["For thought (*cogitatio*) there are required: 1) the perception of a thing; 2) marks sufficient to distinguish it, §. 524; 3) its very distinction, §. 67. But thought is an accident, §. 191. Therefore it cannot exist except in a substance, or in an aggregate of substances, §. 194. An aggregate of substances, from whose collected changes or accidents, in none of which there would yet be any thought, thought in the whole would at length result, would be thinking matter. This in the world would be an aggregate of finite substances, §. 354, one of which would contribute more to the thinking than the rest, §. 272, from which it would suffer aids, §. 210, 321. But it would suffer them only ideally, §. 451, 463. Therefore in the world, in all thinking matter there would be one substance in whose single force all that is required for thought, and to be furnished by finite beings, would have its sufficient reason, §. 210, 212; and yet 1) this dominant substance of the given aggregate would never think through itself, which is against §. 30, 220; 2) all that is required for thought, and to be furnished by finite beings, would have its sufficient reason in the given dominant substance, by what has been demonstrated, and at the same time would have to be actuated only through an aggregate of substances, i.e. would have its sufficient reason only in the force of several finite substances, §. 210, q.a., §. 7. Thinking matter is impossible in the world. Whatever can think is either a substance, a monad (*monas*), §. 234, or a whole of which a substance that can think is a part. Therefore every soul is a substance, a monad, §. 504. Whatever can understand can think, §. 69. Therefore whatever can understand is either a substance, a monad, a spirit (*spiritus*), §. 402, or a whole of which a spirit is a part. A whole of spirits is a (moral person) mystical body (*corpus mysticum*). An intellectual soul is a spirit, §. 504, cf. §. 402, 296. The human soul is a substance, §. 740. Therefore a monad, a spirit, §. 741.",[]],"baum:743":["The human soul subsists through itself, §. 742, 192. Therefore it is not a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 193. Since, however, it represents according to the position of the human body in the universe, which is in perpetual motion, §. 417, 296, and hence always changes its position, §. 283, 281, its representations are always changed, §. 512. As these are internal determinations of the human soul, §. 37, 741, the human soul is an internally mutable substance, hence contingent, §. 202, and finite, §. 255. That every human soul is a finite and contingent substance is also evident thus: the human soul represents according to the position of its body, §. 741. Therefore it perceives some things distinctly, others less distinctly, §. 512. Now to conceive something distinctly is a reality, §. 515, 531. Therefore the human soul has a degree of reality that is not the greatest, §. 161, hence a limit, §. 248. Therefore it is finite and contingent, §. 257, a substance, §. 742.",[]],"baum:744":["The human soul (*anima humana*) knows, desires and averts, §. 741. These are partially diverse actions (*actiones*), §. 267. Therefore the human soul has partially diverse faculties (*facultates*), §. 216. These are not strictly called powers (*vires*), §. 197, 59, but are rather conceived through the one representative power of the soul (*vis repraesentativa*), taken strictly, §. 521-720. Nor are they posited outside one another, since accidents (*accidentia*), §. 191, are not outside their substance, §. 194. Nor are they accurately said to act upon one another, since action belongs properly only to a substance, §. 210, much less to flow into one another (*influere*), §. 211.",[]],"baum:745":["The human soul is a monad (*monas*), §. 742, and contingent, §. 743. It cannot arise except from nothing, §. 236, and cannot perish except by annihilation, §. 237. It is not extended and does not fill space, §. 241, i.e. it is not in a place repletively, §. 241. Yet, coexisting with simultaneous things posited outside it, it is in space, §. 239, and in a place, §. 281, in such a way that some things posited outside it are nearer to it, others more remote, §. 288, 282. If the complex of simultaneous things nearer to the human soul is called its seat (*sedes*)@@1@@, then the human body, in preference to other bodies, and certain parts of it, in preference to other members, are the seat of the soul, §. 409.",[]],"baum:746":["The human soul has no quantitative magnitude, §. 744, 243, and is indivisible, §. 244. Perishing by division is physical corruption (*corruptio physica*)@@1@@. Therefore physical corruption is in itself impossible for the human soul, §. 15, 745, i.e. the human soul is absolutely incorruptible physically@@2@@.",[]],"baum:747":["The human soul indeed does not admit the threefold dimension of extended things, §. 290, 745; yet a philosophical and mathematical knowledge (*cognitio*) of it is possible, as of the human body, §. 743, 249. Man consists of a soul and a body, both finite, §. 741, 743, and hence internally mutable, §. 740; he is a finite and contingent being (*ens*), §. 202, 257. Therefore a philosophical and mathematical knowledge of man is possible, §. 249, i.e. a philosophical and mathematical anthropology, or anthropometry (*anthropometria*), as there is an empirical one through experience. The complex of rules to be observed in knowing man is anthropognosia (*anthropognosia*).",[]],"baum:748":["If a finite being were changed into an infinite one, an infinite would arise, §. 125, 227, and it would not be eternal, §. 302, which is absolutely impossible, §. 252. The change of man into an infinite being is apotheosis (*apotheosis*)@@1@@; therefore apotheosis is absolutely impossible.",[]],"baum:749":["Total similarity (*similitudo*) and equality (*aequalitas*) of human souls, even of only two, is impossible, §. 271, 272. Therefore total agreement or identity of senses, imaginations, foresights, judgments, tastes, disgusts, pleasures, stimuli, motives, errors, desires, aversions, volitions and nolitions in many men, or even in very few, is a chimera, §. 590.",[]],"baum:750":["The human soul moves its own body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it has a faculty of moving something posited outside itself, i.e. a locomotive [faculty] (*locomotiva*), §. 216. Like the other faculties of the soul, §. 744, this is actuated by its representative power of the universe in accordance with the position of its body, §. 741, 417.",[]],"baum:751":["The human soul represents to itself certain parts of this universe as singular, §. 740, 736, and therefore as determined in every respect, §. 148. To the determination in every respect of the parts of this world belongs their connection with each of the remaining parts, §. 357. Therefore the human soul represents certain things to itself as connected with each of the parts of the world. Now one thing cannot be known as connected with another unless the other is known in some way or other, §. 14. Therefore the human soul perceives each of the parts of this universe, and therefore also perceives each of the states of this universe, §. 369.",[]],"baum:752":["In accordance with the position of its body, the human soul represents to itself the present state of the world, i.e. it senses (*sentit*), §. 534, 751; the past, i.e. it imagines (*imaginatur*), §. 557; the future, i.e. it foresees (*praevidet*), §. 595. The sensations (*sensationes*) of the human soul are representations of the single parts of the world simultaneous with it, less or more distinct, confused or obscure, according to how their objects stand to the human body, §. 751. The imaginations (*imaginationes*) of the human soul are representations of the single parts of the world that are past, which preceded the sensing soul, or existed before that act of sensation; and these imaginations too are distinct, confused or obscure, more or less, according to how their objects stood to the human body, §. 751. The foresights (*praevisiones*) of the human soul are representations of the single parts of the world that are future, which will exist after the act of the sensing soul, less or more distinct, confused or obscure, according to how their objects will stand to the human body, §. 751.",[]],"baum:753":["In the same way in which §. 752 proved of sensation, for example, that it is in every human soul, the same can be shown of the remaining acts of the soul that are to be found in the human soul through experience and enumerated by empirical psychology (*psychologia empirica*), §. 576 ff.",[]],"baum:754":["In accordance with the position of its body, the human soul represents distinctly, §. 741. Therefore it understands (*intelligit*), §. 402, and is a spirit (*spiritus*), §. 742, 216. Hence it is again evident that all human souls are unequal, and that one of them all is the most perfect, §. 405. The human soul is a finite spirit, §. 743, and a part of this world, §. 355.",[]],"baum:755":["Every spirit is a substance (*substantia*), §. 402. Therefore it is a power (*vis*), §. 199, and hence the sufficient reason for the inherence of certain accidents, §. 197, and so an agent, §. 210. And since it is an intellectual substance, §. 402, it has the faculty of actuating certain distinct representations, §. 216. Therefore it has the faculty of determining its power toward them, §. 210, and hence of desiring (*appetendi*), §. 663. Whatever it desires, it averts the opposite of; hence a spirit has the faculty of averting (*aversandi*), §. 663. The desires and aversions of a spirit are its internal determinations, §. 37. Therefore they are in universal connection with its other internal determinations, §. 49. Therefore also with the rest of its knowledge, which is an internal determination, §. 37, and so they come about according to the pleasure of the spirit (*lubitus*), §. 712. Therefore a spirit has choice (*arbitrium*), §. 712, and since its pleasure can be distinct, §. 402, [it has] free [choice] (*liberum*) or freedom (*libertas*), §. 719.",[]],"baum:756":["The human soul is a spirit, §. 754. Therefore it has freedom, §. 755. And since spirituality, intellectuality, personality, §. 641, 754, freedom, absolute simplicity, §. 744, and incorruptibility belong to it with absolute necessity, §. 746, they are not its modes (*modi*), §. 108, and hence are either essentials (*essentialia*) or attributes (*attributa*), because they are internal determinations, §. 37, 52. If any one of them were removed, the human soul would be removed, §. 63. Hence a human soul that could not at all conceive anything distinctly, nor determine itself from a distinct pleasure, thereby losing all personality and freedom; a soul consisting of several powers as parts to be posited outside one another; and a soul that is to be corrupted physically: these are chimeras, §. 590.",[]],"baum:757":["The human soul is immaterial and incorporeal, §. 744, 422. He who denies that the human soul is an immaterial substance is a materialist (*materialista*) in the psychological sense, cf. §. 395, and is mistaken, whether he holds it to be a mere accident of the body, §. 742, or a material atom, §. 429, or any very subtle corpuscle whatever, §. 426. The universal materialist is also such in the psychological sense, §. 395, but not everyone who thinks the human soul a material substance is necessarily a universal materialist, §. 395.",[]],"baum:758":["The representative power of the universe in accordance with the position of the human body in it, together with the modes of the soul depending on this, is the complex of the soul's internal determinations which are the principles of its changes and of the accidents inhering in it, §. 751-755. Therefore the same power, with its determinations depending on the position of the body, is the nature (*natura*) of the human soul, §. 430. Hence whatever is actuated in the human soul through this power, as determined according to the laws and rules of the single faculties, is natural to the human soul (*naturale*), §. 470, in so far as the natural is contradistinguished from the supernatural (*supernaturale*), §. 469. In this sense the free and moral actions of the soul also are natural, §. 756, 755, although they are not merely natural, §. 709. Besides this proper meaning of nature in the human soul, and hence of the natural in it, there are other improper and synecdochic meanings. Some arose wrongly from a confusion of part with whole; others arose because a particular name attached to what is conceivable from a particular mode, modification or state of nature in the human soul. In that case, if the remaining natural things, lacking a particular name, had to be contradistinguished from those that had acquired one, the name of the genus remained in its stricter meaning. Thus, for example, innate things (*connata*) are sometimes called natural as contradistinguished from acquired ones, and natural things are contradistinguished from artificial, social, arbitrary things, and from those finally obtained by custom, etc.; cf. §. 710.",[]],"baum:759":["Whatever things in the human soul succeed one another naturally for the human soul, according to laws prescribed by its power, come about with respect to the human soul according to the course of nature (*cursus naturae*), §. 758, 471; and together with those which coexist in the human soul according to the same laws, they happen according to the order of nature (*ordo naturae*) with respect to the human soul, §. 473. Preternatural things (*praeternaturalia*) are those which are not actuated through its representative power in accordance with the position of its body, according to the laws and rules of its faculties; if they are not actuated either through the whole of nature, they are supernatural, §. 474.",[]],"baum:760":["The human soul of the best world is in the greatest universal pneumatic, and pneumatic-mechanical, connection (*nexus*), §. 464, 754. Therefore it is connected with each of the spirits and bodies of the best world, §. 48, but unequally, §. 272. Therefore it is connected most closely with one body and with one spirit, §. 161.",[]],"baum:761":["Psychological systems (*systemata psychologica*) are opinions that seem fitted to explain the commerce (*commercium*) of soul and body in man. Therefore psychological systems are particular systems, §. 462, either simple or composite, §. 457. None of them is possible, except perhaps the psychological system of pre-established harmony (*harmonia praestabilita*), of physical influx (*influxus physicus*), and of occasional causes (*causae occasionales*), §. 458.",[]],"baum:762":["If one of the simple general systems is posited, one of the simple psychological systems is also posited, §. 761, 457. Therefore, once universal pre-established harmony has been demonstrated, §. 463, psychological pre-established harmony has been demonstrated at the same time. Yet if one of the simple psychological systems is posited, it is not necessarily the case that one of the general systems, or a very similar one, is to be posited, §. 761, 457. If the human soul and body can be in the closest commerce through pre-established harmony, they are also connected through pre-established harmony in the best world, §. 461, so that physical influx or the system of occasional causes is not to be admitted, except where commerce through pre-established harmony is impossible, §. 462.",[]],"baum:763":["The psychological system of physical influx (*systema influxus physici psychologicum*), since it is simple, posits that all harmonic changes (*mutationes harmonicae*) of all souls and human bodies come about through physical influx (*influxum physicum*), §. 457. Therefore, if even a single harmonic change of one soul or human body can be proved possible through pre-established harmony (*harmoniam praestabilitam*) in the best world (*mundo optimo*), the system of physical influx can no longer be defended as having a place in the best world, §. 762. The psychological system of pre-established harmony, since it is simple, posits that all harmonic changes of all souls and human bodies come about through pre-established harmony. For the same reason the psychological system of occasional causes (*systema causarum occasionalium*) posits that these same changes come about through infinite power (*vim infinitam*) alone, §. 457. Therefore, if even a single harmonic change of one soul or human body were impossible in the best world through pre-established harmony, its psychological system could no longer be defended as having a place in the best world. If even a single harmonic change of one soul or human body is possible in the best world through pre-established harmony or physical influx, then neither can the system of occasional causes any longer be defended as having a place in the best world, §. 762, 460. The psychological system of physical influx is no more evident through experience (*experientiam*) than the system of pre-established harmony and that of occasional causes, §. 737, 738.",[]],"baum:764":["The psychological system of physical influx posits that the human soul really (*realiter*) influences its body, and the human body its soul, in harmonic changes, §. 761, 450. Hence, according to the psychological system of physical influx, 1) in none of the harmonic changes occurring in the human body does the body act by its own force (*vi propria*), §. 212. Now all changes of the human body, as a part of the world which the soul represents, can be sufficiently known from the force of the soul, §. 354, 751. Therefore all of them are harmonic, §. 448, and in none of its changes does the body, according to the psychological system of physical influx, act by its own force; in all of them it is really passive (*patiens*) under the soul. If the body never acts, neither does it react, §. 213. Therefore, according to the system of physical influx, there is in the world a transeunt action (*actio transiens*) of the soul upon the human body without reaction, whenever the soul acts upon its body; and if this happened even once, it would be contrary to §. 410.",[]],"baum:765":["According to the psychological system of physical influx it is posited 2) that the human body really influences its soul whenever a change occurs in the human soul which can be sufficiently known from the force of the body, §. 764, 448. Therefore, in the harmonic changes met with in the soul, the body, according to the psychological system of physical influx, really exerts influence; yet by the same system it must be posited that in none of its changes does it ever act, §. 764. According to the system of physical influx the soul does not act at all in sensing, §. 212, although it represents to itself by its own proper force the present state of the world no less than its other states, §. 751.",[]],"baum:766":["The human body consists of elements (*elementis*), §. 420, that is, of monads representing their world, and from each of these its individual parts can be known, §. 400; hence [can be known] also the individual changes of the soul, which are parts of the world, §. 354. Therefore every action of the soul is a harmonic change, §. 448, 22. Now every volition and nolition of the soul is an action of it, §. 210, 690. Therefore every volition and nolition of the human soul is a harmonic change of the same. Now, according to the psychological system of physical influx, the human soul in none of its harmonic changes acts by its own force, but really suffers from the body. Therefore, according to the system of physical influx, the soul in all its volitions and nolitions, doing nothing whatever, is passive under the body; and this is contrary to freedom, §. 755.",[]],"baum:767":["According to the psychological system of occasional causes, 1) the human body does not act in any of its harmonic changes, but the infinite being (*ens infinitum*) does, §. 761, 452. Hence it is evident, in the same manner as was shown in §. 764, that according to the system of assistance (*systema assistentiae*) the body acts in none of its changes, but only the infinite being. 2) According to the same system the human soul does not act in its harmonic changes, but the infinite being alone, §. 761, 452. Hence it is evident, in the same manner as was shown in §. 766, that according to the psychological system of assistance the human soul does nothing whatever in any of its volitions and nolitions, and in them is only really passive under the infinite being; and this likewise is contrary to freedom, §. 755.",[]],"baum:768":["According to the psychological system of pre-established harmony, each party to the commerce (*commercii*) actualises by its own force the harmonic changes occurring in itself, being only ideally (*idealiter*) passive with respect to the other party, §. 761, 448. Hence it is posited by the psychological system of pre-established harmony 1) that the voluntary motions of the body, no less than the merely natural and vital ones, are sufficiently determined by the mechanism of the body itself and of the bodies surrounding it; and 2) that the sensations of the soul, no less than its freest thoughts of any kind, are sufficiently determined by its own representative force (*vim repraesentativam*), §. 758, 433.",[]],"baum:769":["Suppose a psychological harmonist is to give the reason for some harmonic change in man, and seeks it 1) from the force of that very part of the commerce in which the harmonic change is observed, 2) from the other part of the commerce, which he himself supposes to influence it ideally, 3) from the infinite force which he holds to concur. Suppose, I say, that he is in error: he has nevertheless most certainly given the true reason, §. 448, 450, even though by his hypothesis he has added a false one. Suppose that in the same case the influxionist or the occasionalist is in error: he has not merely passed over a large part of the true reason, but has even denied it, §. 450, 452.",[]],"baum:770":["When a human being is conceived, the soul, which is to be in the closest commerce with its body, such as obtains in men on this earth, either already pre-existed, or begins to exist only in the conception itself, or a little after it. He who posits the first is called a pre-existentian (*praeexsistentianus*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:771":["Those who posit that the existence of the human soul begins only in the conception itself, or a little after it, either hold that it arises from the parents, and are called traducians (*traduciani*)@@1@@, or admit that it then first exists, [coming] out of nothing, and are called inducians (*induciani*)@@2@@ (infusians, coexistentians).",[]],"baum:772":["He who posits that the human soul is actualised out of nothing is called a creatian (*creatianus*)@@1@@. Hence an inducian is a creatian, §. 771. A pre-existentian can be a creatian, §. 770. A traducian is not a creatian, and conversely, §. 771. Whether traducians derive the soul that is to arise from the soul of the parents, as flame from flame, or from their body, they are bound to admit that the new soul arises either from a simple part of the parents or from a composite, §. 224. If the former, the soul does not arise but pre-exists, §. 227; if the latter, the new soul will not be a monad but a composite in the strict sense, §. 225, which is contrary to §. 756, 757. The soul cannot arise except out of nothing, §. 745. Therefore it does not arise from the parents, §. 228.",[]],"baum:773":["Let the propagation of human souls by traduction (*per traducem*)@@1@@ be called the union of ever new human souls with a body, such as obtains in men on this earth, through the continuous succession of parents and offspring in the world, by which the human race, as it is on this earth, is so propagated that the souls pass over with the bodies of the offspring and are transferred from the bodies of the parents to their own proper places, where they may dwell more apart. It can be conceived, 1) as involving at the same time the origin of the soul; that is, the soul, which with the intervening act of conception is to enter into the closest commerce with a body such as man has on this earth, is posited as originating at the same time. And this again [is conceived] either as from the parents, of which §. 772, or as by the parents, on the assumption that God can implant in the human soul a determinate force by which it can produce out of nothing only some individuals of its own species, his creative force concurring and supplying the very many realities in the soul that is to arise, while only some quite limited realities proceed from the soul of the parents. He who posits this opinion can be called a concreatian (*concreatianus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:774":["The propagation of human souls by traduction can be conceived, 2) as not involving the origin of the soul, but the soul is posited as pre-existing, as having already existed before the intervening act of conception in the closest commerce with a part of the seed, or more accurately with a spermatic animalcule, and so backwards through many generations. Its representative force has been gradually increased and developed, according as the body of the chosen spermatic animalcule, which was to come forth upon a greater stage, has little by little come nearer and nearer to its great transformation and growth, §. 773.",[]],"baum:775":["The origin of the soul and the propagation of the soul by traduction can be distinguished, §. 773, 771. Those also who posit that the origin by traduction is from the parents can be distinguished from those who posit that it is to be derived by the parents, §. 773, 772. Therefore not everyone who admits traduction, however it be understood, is guilty of psychological materialism, §. 772, 757.",[]],"baum:776":["As long as even a single minimal change naturally inheres in a natural being, or, generally, a single minimal accident naturally inheres in it, its nature endures, §. 469, 29, and the being lives, §. 430. But if all the changes natural to the being, or, generally, all the accidents naturally inhering in it, are ended or cease, its nature ends, §. 469, 23, and the being dies, §. 430. Therefore the life of the human body@@1@@, that is, the duration of its nature, by which it has power as a human body, continues so long as even a single minimal vital or voluntary motion, natural to it as a human body, survives, §. 733, 740. The death of the human body@@2@@, that is, the end of its nature, by which it has power as a human body, is the complete cessation of vital and voluntary motions. Therefore the death defined in §. 556 is the death of the human body.",[]],"baum:777":["Since an animal is composed of soul and body, with which the soul is in the closest commerce, §. 740, its nature is composed of the nature of the body and of the soul placed in the closest commerce, §. 430. Hence, while certain harmonic changes of the soul and body placed in the closest commerce exist, the animal lives@@1@@. The cessation of all harmonic actions of the soul and body placed in the closest commerce is the death of the animal@@2@@. Now through the death of the body all harmonic changes of the soul and of the dying body placed in the closest commerce cease, §. 776, 448. Therefore the death of the body is also the death of the animal, and the death of the human body is the death of the man, §. 740. Therefore the death defined in §. 556 is the death of the man, §. 776.",[]],"baum:778":["Animal death (*mors animalis*) is either absolute@@1@@, the cessation of all the harmonious changes of the soul with every animal body, or merely respective@@2@@ (animal transformation and metamorphosis), the cessation of the harmonious changes of the soul with only some particular body, with which it had been in the closest commerce. The death of a man is either absolute or merely respective. That is, when the body dies (such a body as we find men to have in these lands), either all the closest commerce of the human soul with any body ceases, or the soul enters with another body into a new commerce of the same kind, §. 776, 777.",[]],"baum:779":["Since certain parts of the human body daily cease to be in the closest commerce (*commercium*) with the soul, while certain others enter into it, it is clear in what sense a man can be said to die daily and to revive daily, §. 777. The opinion which holds that the death of a man is nothing but an animal transformation is called the banishment of (absolute) death@@1@@.",[]],"baum:780":["The life of the human soul@@1@@ (*vita animae humanae*), or the duration of its nature, continues as long as even a single minimal accident remains that is natural to it as a human soul, §. 776. Now as long as the human soul endures, there is in it perception (*perceptio*), that is, representation of the state of the world and of its own state, past, present and future, §. 742, 400. This representation can be distinct according to the position of a certain body, §. 741, and is an accident, §. 191, natural to the soul, §. 758. Therefore the human soul lives as long as it exists. The death of the human soul@@2@@, or the end of its nature, is at the same time the end of its existence. Now all existence, nature and life of the human soul is in itself contingent, §. 743, 430. Therefore the death of the human soul is in itself possible, §. 81, 104.",[]],"baum:781":["The mortal (*mortale*)@@1@@ is that which can die, and the possibility of dying is mortality@@2@@. That which cannot die is immortal@@3@@, and the impossibility of dying is immortality@@4@@. Mortality and immortality are either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. A mortality that is not only absolute but also hypothetical, and sufficiently great, belongs to the human body and to man, §. 777, 168. To the soul belongs only absolute mortality, together with the hypothetical mortality of a thing that is to be annihilated, §. 745, 780. Absolute immortality, indeed, cannot be ascribed to the soul, §. 780. Yet since it is indestructible, §. 746, and so can fail to die in the six hundred ways in which a body can die, a quite considerable hypothetical immortality belongs to it. No substance of this world is annihilated, §. 354, 358, 227, 228. Therefore when the body dies (such a body as men have in these lands [the text reads *duale*; *quale* is evidently intended]), the human soul survives and lives immortally, §. 780, 742. The Thnetopsychites@@5@@, who hold that the human soul dies with the body, are in error.",[]],"baum:782":["After death (of the body and of the man, such as we experience them in these lands) the human soul preserves spirituality, freedom and personality, §. 781, 756. If you call the preserved intellectual memory of itself immortality, then in this sense also the human soul is immortal, §. 781, 641. As regards its use [of its powers], its total perceptions are for a longer time either only sensitive, or it soon acquires intellectual ones. Those who maintain the former are Hypnopsychites@@1@@, who, if they deny that the soul ever understands after the death of this body, are Psychopannychites@@2@@. Before death the human soul had known something clearly or distinctly, §. 754. This reality, §. 520, is never wholly barren, §. 517, and, so far as it is a reality, has in indefinitely many cases none but realities as its consequences, §. 140. It stands in indefinite universal connection with the spirituality, understanding and reason of the soul, which are again realities, §. 531, 49, and which, as such, have none but realities as their consequences without limit, §. 23, 140. Hence the connection between the clear or distinct knowledge (*cognitio*) of this life and the spirituality, understanding and reason of the soul is again a reality, having real consequences without limit, §. 140. But the natural real consequences of all these cannot be increased without limit apart from distinct perceptions, §. 631, ratiocinations, §. 642, and volitions or nolitions, §. 665, 690. Hence the human soul after death, preserving its nature, §. 780, 781, will naturally, at least in the end, put forth an intellectual life also through spiritual actions, §. 639, and the Psychopannychites err.",[]],"baum:783":["The human soul after death, when it understands, either manifests personality also so far as distinctly to recollect its state in this life, or does so less, §. 782. Those who maintain the latter may be called advocates of the Lethaean cup@@1@@. The former is more natural, §. 583, 561.",[]],"baum:784":["The state of the human soul after death can be considered: 1) on the supposition of the absolute death of man, §. 778. In that case the human soul after death is posited as deprived of every body, that is, there is no body with which it would again enter into the closest commerce, §. 742, 740. 2) On the supposition of merely respective death of man, so that the soul is again placed in the closest commerce with a new body, §. 778. The beginning of a new closest commerce with a new body is called palingenesis@@1@@ (regeneration, metensomatosis and metempsychosis taken more broadly). Those who maintain this either at the same time favour the Lethaean cup and posit a new body of the kind known in these lands, or posit neither of these along with it. The former defend, improbably, metempsychosis more strictly so called, and the gross kind@@2@@, §. 783.",[]],"baum:785":["The human soul, enduring after the death of the body which it had in these lands, §. 781, still represents the several parts of this universe, §. 752, 780, and therefore also their bodies, §. 155. The bodies simultaneous with it act upon it and are acted upon by it; it is acted upon by the bodies simultaneous with it and flows into them, §. 408. But it does not flow equally into two of them, nor is it acted upon equally by two of them, §. 272. Therefore there is one body with which the human soul, enduring after the death of the body, enters into the closest commerce, §. 448, 739. Hence, if a soul of this kind is said to be separated, this is to be understood relatively to the body such as we experience men to have in these lands; and the death of a man is nothing but an animal transformation, §. 779, and palingenesis, which, however, would be wrongly confused with gross metempsychosis, §. 784.",[]],"baum:786":["The human soul, enduring after the death of this body, is in the closest commerce with another, §. 785. That new body will in its different states be now less, now more congruent with this body, §. 270, 265. Therefore there will be some state of it in which it will be most congruent with the body which had been in the closest commerce with the soul in this life, §. 161, and hence the same, §. 70.",[]],"baum:787":["The perfection of a finite spirit is 1) either absolutely necessary or contingent, §. 147; 2) either natural or supernatural, §. 496; 3) either internal or external, §. 98; 4) either moral in the wide sense or less so, §. 723. In the same way the goods of a spirit, on whose positing that perfection is posited, §. 100, are 1) either metaphysical or contingent, §. 147; 2) either natural or supernatural, §. 469; 3) either domestic or adventitious, §. 660; 4) either moral in the wide sense or less so, §. 723. The goods morally so called in the wide sense are more closely connected with the freedom of the given spirit, either as grounds (*rationes*), §. 14, a priori, §. 24, and as antecedents of a certain state of it; or as things grounded (*rationata*), §. 14, a posteriori, §. 24, and as consequences of a certain state of it, §. 596; or in both ways, §. 24. Those which depend more closely on the given freedom are called strictly moral@@1@@, and sometimes simply so. Hence the goods of a spirit that depend more closely on its freedom are strictly moral@@2@@, and the perfection that is to be posited when these are posited is blessedness@@3@@ (*beatitudo*). The sum of the perfections belonging to a spirit is felicity@@4@@ (*felicitas*). The complement of blessedness to the felicity of a finite spirit is prosperity@@5@@ (*prosperitas*), and the goods on whose positing it is posited are prosperous goods@@6@@ (physical, strictly so called). The felicity of a finite spirit is the sum of prosperity and blessedness.",[]],"baum:788":["The evils of a spirit which depend more closely on its freedom are strictly moral@@1@@, §. 787 (evils of guilt, sins); the evils morally so called in the wide sense@@2@@ are those more closely connected with its freedom. The imperfection arising from the latter is moral corruption in the wide sense@@3@@, that arising from the former is moral corruption strictly so called@@4@@. The sum of the imperfections belonging to a spirit is unhappiness@@5@@ (*infelicitas*). The complement of strictly moral corruption to the unhappiness of a finite spirit is misery@@6@@ (*miseria*), and the evils on whose positing it is posited are harms in the wide sense@@7@@ (physical evils, strictly so called). Unhappiness is the sum of misery and moral corruption.",[]],"baum:789":["The human soul in this life@@1@@, that is, while it lives in the closest commerce with the body such as we experience men to have in these lands, is continually changed, §. 418, 754. None of these changes is absolutely indifferent objectively, §. 654. All of them, therefore, are either good or bad, or both. Those changes of the soul which are at once good and bad (as all are, §. 264) are either equally or unequally good and bad at once, §. 70. Those more good than bad posit more felicity than unhappiness; those more bad than good posit more unhappiness than felicity. Accordingly the former are to be denominated good, the latter bad, from the predominant element, §. 787, 788.",[]],"baum:790":["Suppose a thing to be totally bad to the same extent as it is good. It would not have the highest degree of reality, §. 246, 248. Therefore it would be a finite being, §. 248, and hence contingent, §. 257, and so its opposite would be possible, §. 101. And since its existence would be contingent, §. 109, the existence of its opposite would be possible, §. 101. But the opposite of such a thing could exist only outside it, §. 81, 7, and would be a good and an evil totally equal to the thing posited. Therefore two totally equal actual things outside one another would be possible, which is contrary to §. 272. Therefore a thing that would be as totally good as it is bad cannot exist. Therefore there are no changes of the human soul except those that are either good or bad. Therefore every human soul in this life is either happy or unhappy, §. 789.",[]],"baum:791":["The human soul that endures after the death of the body continues to be constantly changed, §. 781, 782. Therefore at every moment of its duration its happiness (*felicitas*) or unhappiness (*infelicitas*) is increased, §. 790, 162. Therefore the human soul that endures after the death of the body will either enjoy a greater happiness than in this life, and is blessed (*beata*)@@1@@, or will labour under a greater unhappiness, and is damned (*damnata*)@@2@@. The blessedness (*beatitudo*) which the soul is posited to have once entered upon after this life will either coexist with the soul as long as it endures, or damnation will at some time succeed it. The damnation (*damnatio*) which the soul is posited to have once entered upon after this life will either coexist with the soul as long as it endures, or blessedness will at some time succeed it, §. 790, 789. In either case the former is more natural, §. 139, 140.",[]],"baum:792":["Since every soul is that in a being which can be conscious of something, §. 504, it has a cognitive faculty (*facultas cognoscitiva*), §. 519, and this is either only the lower, or also the higher, §. 520, 524. The former will be a merely sensitive soul (*anima mere sensitiva*)@@1@@. An animal that has a merely sensitive soul is a brute (*brutum*)@@2@@; an animal whose soul is a spirit is a rational animal (*animal rationale*)@@3@@. Therefore man is a rational animal, §. 754, 740.",[]],"baum:793":["The souls of brutes are in the closest commerce (*commercium*) with the animal body, §. 740, and hence represent their body clearly and obscurely, §. 792, 736. Therefore they are representative powers of the universe (*vires repraesentativae universi*) according to the position of the bodies of brutes in it, §. 741. Hence they are substances, §. 198, monads, §. 234, simple beings, §. 230, having no parts outside parts, §. 224, finite, §. 202, 792, indivisible, §. 244, hence physically incorruptible, §. 746, immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422. They are equipped with sensation, imagination, foresight and the remaining faculties that are to be actuated without distinct cognition, through the representative power of the universe according to the position of the body, §. 792. Hence they are equipped with sensitive appetite and aversion, §. 667, and with choice (*arbitrium*), §. 718, and are to be impelled by instincts, flights and stimuli, §. 677, and even to affects, §. 678.",[]],"baum:794":["So that the easier questions, whether brutes have a soul and whether it is a rational one, §. 792, 793, may not be confounded with the following: 1) whether all the bodies appearing on this earth, apart from the human, are destitute of soul, §. 504; 2) whether some of them are seats of a rational soul, §. 745; 3) whether all that are animal bodies [are bodies of a soul] which either already now enjoys some use of reason, or will at length have enjoyed it, §. 782, 639.",[]],"baum:795":["The souls of brutes are destitute of intellect, §. 792. Therefore they are not spirits, §. 402; they lack personality, §. 641, reason, §. 640, will and nolition (*voluntas, noluntas*), §. 690, and freedom, §. 719; nor are they immortal, as human souls are, §. 781-784, nor capable of happiness or unhappiness, whether now or at some future time, §. 787, 788.",[]],"baum:796":["Spirits endowed with a greater essential degree of intellect than that of man are superior (*superiores*)@@1@@; those with a lesser, inferior (*inferiores*)@@2@@. Both are finite, and either happy or unhappy, §. 790; the former are good spirits (*agathodaemones*)@@3@@ (*calodaemones*), the latter evil spirits (*cacodaemones*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:797":["Every finite spirit, whether it be superior or inferior to man, has, if actual, a body with which it is in the closest commerce, §. 785, 796, and which either gravitates constantly towards a certain centre outside itself, or does so less. The former is the inhabitant of a certain body in the universe taken as a whole, and of one that is either fixed or wandering, and so on [*e.c.*].",[]],"baum:798":["Whatever degree of intellect a finite superior spirit may have had, it still does not have the greatest, §. 248, and hence does not represent all things most distinctly, §. 637, but in such a way that it can be known from its body why it represents these things and not others more purely, more deeply, more distinctly, confusedly, obscurely, §. 797, 512. Therefore every finite spirit has a lower cognitive faculty, §. 520.",[]],"baum:799":["No finite spirit, whether superior or inferior to man, can be physically corrupted, §. 746. Hence even if one of its bodies be dissolved to the greatest degree, it will nevertheless survive unless it is annihilated, §. 745. As long as it endures it preserves its personality, with the use of intellect, reason and freedom increasing rather than decreasing or ceasing altogether, §. 782, and it is more natural for it to remember distinctly its past state than to drink the Lethean cup, §. 783. Therefore every finite spirit is by its own nature, i.e. through its representative power of the universe, §. 782, immortal, like the human soul, §. 781, 783; and it is more natural for it, as long as it endures, to persist in the blessedness or damnation which it has once entered upon, §. 787, 791, than that one once conspicuously blessed should be damned, or vice versa, §. 791.",[]],"baum:800":["Natural theology (*theologia naturalis*) is the science of God, so far as he can be known without faith.",[]],"baum:801":["Natural theology contains the first principles of practical philosophy, of teleology and of revealed theology. Therefore it is referred, §. 2, along with reason, to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:802":["Natural theology considers 1) the concept of God, 2) his operations.",[]],"baum:803":["The most perfect being (*ens perfectissimum*)@@1@@ is that which has the highest perfection among beings; i.e. that in which as many, as great, and as much agree, in as many and as great things, as can agree in any being among the most numerous and greatest of the possibles, §. 185. Therefore in the most perfect being there is a certain plurality, with absolute necessity, §. 74.",[]],"baum:804":["The predicates of the most perfect being are called its perfections (*perfectiones*). In the most perfect being there are as many perfections agreeing to the highest degree as can be in a being at the same time, as many as are compossible, §. 803.",[]],"baum:805":["The perfection of every most perfect being is as great as can be in any being, §. 803, 804.",[]],"baum:806":["The most perfect being is a real being, §. 803, 135. Therefore there belongs to it as great a reality as can be in a being. The most perfect being is the most real (*realissimum*), §. 805, 804; in it are the most numerous and greatest realities, the highest good and the best metaphysically, §. 190.",[]],"baum:807":["All realities are truly positive, and no negation is a reality, §. 36. Therefore, even if all of them be united in a being to the greatest degree, a contradiction will never arise from them, §. 13. Therefore all realities are compossible in a being. Now the most perfect being is the most real of beings, §. 806. Therefore there belongs to the most perfect being the totality (*omnitudo*) of realities, and indeed of the greatest of those that can be in any being, §. 805, 190.",[]],"baum:808":["When a reality is posited, negation is removed, §. 36. Now in the most perfect being all realities are to be posited, §. 807. Therefore all negations are to be removed.",[]],"baum:809":["In everything impossible something is to be posited and removed at the same time, §. 7. This is either a negation or a reality, §. 36, 9. In the most perfect being no reality is to be removed, §. 807. In the most perfect being no negation is to be posited, §. 808. Therefore nothing in the most perfect being is to be removed and posited at the same time. The most perfect being is possible, §. 8.",[]],"baum:810":["Existence (*exsistentia*) is a reality compossible with the essence and the remaining realities, §. 66, 807. Therefore the most perfect being has existence, §. 807.",[]],"baum:811":["God (*deus*)@@1@@ is the most perfect being. Therefore God is actual, §. 810, 55.",[]],"baum:812":["The predicates of God are perfections, §. 811, 804, all of them the greatest realities, §. 807. His very possibility is the greatest: 1) internal and absolute, §. 165, in that in him the most numerous and greatest things are compossible, §. 805, 807; 2) external and hypothetical, in that the most fruitful and weightiest reasons in him have, in the whole connexion of all possible worlds, consequences that can be outside God, and these most fruitful and weightiest, §. 168.",[]],"baum:813":["In God there are no negations, §. 808; therefore neither negations strictly so called, nor privations, §. 137, nor metaphysical evil, nor contingent, §. 146, nor physical, nor moral, in whatever sense, §. 788.",[]],"baum:814":["In the concept of God notes that are wholly negative are impossible, §. 525, but so too are those that are in part such, §. 813, 808, although to us many of the things in him that are most real seem to be negative, §. 12, because we partly express negations in affirmative terms, and partly do not sufficiently remember to affirm the negation of a negation, §. 36, 81.",[]],"baum:815":["The perfections of God are in him either absolute or respective, and among these some are relative, §. 37; and they either can be represented by us without the note of action, and are quiescent (*quiescentes*)@@1@@, or cannot, and are operative (*operativae*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:816":["An internal perfection of God from which all the remaining internal perfections can finally be so deduced that it itself cannot afterwards be deduced from another internal perfection of God, by those who would avoid a circle, is the primary concept of God (*conceptus dei primus*), §. 40, 39. Now from the infinite perfections of God such a deduction of the rest is possible, §. 24, 49, since each is the greatest ground, §. 812, 166, and the most sufficient, §. 169, and hence also ultimate absolutely, §. 170, the greatest essence, §. 171. Therefore the primary concepts of God are infinite, and yet each of them, taken for the essence, is the one essence of God, §. 40, 77.",[]],"baum:817":["Although in God there is the greatest connexion, §. 808, 167, and the most universal, §. 172, so that in him all things are most truly from all things, §. 876, nevertheless we know the remaining perfections of God more easily from one perfection than from another, §. 527. Therefore it is best to take for the essence that perfection from which we hope to deduce the rest most easily, §. 816. So far is it from being the case that all plurality in God is impossible, that rather in his very essence, and through it, a certain plurality is posited with absolute necessity, §. 812, 816.",[]],"baum:818":["God is determined, besides the essence, whichever you may take, §. 817, with respect to the remaining internal perfections also, §. 816, as far as anything can be determined as internal, §. 812. Therefore God is actual, §. 54.",[]],"baum:819":["God is determined with respect to existence, §. 818, 811. Therefore God is a being, §. 61, 57, and neither a negative nor a privative non-being, §. 7, 54.",[]],"baum:820":["From the possibility of God to his being the consequence holds; i.e. his existence is sufficiently determined by his essence, §. 809-811, and indeed the greatest, §. 805, existence being the complement (*complementum*), §. 55, eternal, §. 302, of the complex of the most numerous and greatest affections compossible in any being, or of the greatest essence, §. 816, whichever you may take, §. 817.",[]],"baum:821":["Unity (*unitas*), §. 819, 73, the greatest, §. 812, belongs to God, [as does] the inseparability of the most numerous greatest realities, §. 173. Hence it is clear: 1) how it can be called eminently perfect unity, which may serve as an example of how even those among God's perfections that seem most sterile are nevertheless of his essence, §. 816; 2) that when God's highest unity is posited, a certain plurality of inseparable [realities] in him is not merely not removed but is even posited, §. 74.",[]],"baum:822":["Transcendental truth (*veritas transcendentalis*), §. 819, 90, the greatest, §. 812, belongs to God: the highest order of his perfections, §. 175, 89, the highest possibility, §. 812, the highest connection (*nexus*), §. 817, the highest agreement with the strongest rules, §. 179, 184. Of this kind is the law of the best in general: let the best be joined to the best, §. 482, 187; and the law of the best in beings: let the best of the compossibles in the most perfect being be joined to the best, §. 803.",[]],"baum:823":["If God were not actual (*actualis*), he would be a being enjoying all realities and yet lacking some, §. 66, 812; determinate in respect of all internal perfections, so far as a being can be determined in respect of what is internal, §. 818, and yet, in respect of some, not so determined, §. 54. Therefore the opposite of divine existence is in itself impossible, §. 15. The existence of God is absolutely necessary, §. 102. God is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 109, whose highest existence, §. 820, is his essence, §. 819, §. 817 being left unimpaired.",[]],"baum:824":["If God were not actual, the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*), §. 823, 7, the first principle of both the form and the matter of all our demonstrations, would be false. Therefore, although many sciences can be completely demonstrated without any theological premiss, §. 1-800, yet neither they themselves nor their objects, e.g. §. 61, 354, 504, would exist, indeed they would not even be possible, §. 8, unless God were actual.",[]],"baum:825":["God is a necessary being, §. 823, 824. In a necessary being there are no modes (*modi*), §. 111. Therefore in God there are no modes, that is, no predicable accidents (*accidentia praedicabilia*).",[]],"baum:826":["If we discover in a necessary being something that is partly similar to, partly different from what is represented in a contingent being, but do not sufficiently understand the differences, nor find a name peculiar to it, we call that an analogue (*analogon*) of what we noted as similar in the contingent being. It is attributed to God by analogy (*per analogiam*) if realities seem to reign in its concept, by eminence@@1@@ (excellence), if negations, by reduction@@2@@ (the way of negation).",[]],"baum:827":["If in a necessary being we discover certain things for which to be hypothetically necessary is absolutely necessary, then insofar as, in respect of hypothetical necessity, they are similar to modes, §. 108, they are called analogues of modes (*modorum analoga*)@@1@@, §. 826; insofar as for them to exist in God, [being] hypothetically necessary, is absolutely necessary through the essence, §. 823, 54, they are attributes (*attributa*), §. 107. Hence the divine attributes can be divided into those more similar to the attributes of finite things and those similar to the modes of finite things. The latter too are true attributes, since there is a sufficient reason in God's essence of: 1) their existence, insofar as it is contradistinguished from indetermination, §. 54. A contingent being can be indeterminate in respect of many things, §. 34, 134. A necessary being [is indeterminate] in respect of none, and indeed through its essence, §. 820. 2) Of the duration, eternity and immutability of those things that are once in God. In contingent beings the essential limit is the sufficient reason why they must be continually changed internally while they exist. In a necessary being essential infinitude is the sufficient reason of the opposite: 3) why others in place of these could have existed in God from eternity to eternity, and hence 4) why the existence of these, and not of others, is determined thus and not otherwise only through hypothetical necessity, §. 102.",[]],"baum:828":["Holiness (*sanctitas*)@@1@@ is the reality of a being by which several of its imperfections that are truly such are removed; hence the most holy (*sanctissimum*)@@2@@ is that by whose reality all its imperfections are removed. Now by the totality of the greatest realities in God all imperfections in him are removed, §. 142, 808. Therefore God is most holy.",[]],"baum:829":["In God there is no imperfection, §. 828. Therefore neither essential nor accidental, neither internal nor external, §. 121, 808. The same is clear from §. 813.",[]],"baum:830":["In God there is a sufficient reason why his perfections inhere in him, §. 822, 823. Therefore [he has] force (*vis*) in the strict sense, §. 197. Hence God is a substance (*substantia*), §. 199.",[]],"baum:831":["God has force, §. 830, the greatest, §. 812. Therefore [he has force] sufficient for actualizing the most numerous greatest accidents, §. 203.",[]],"baum:832":["Accidents do not exist outside their substances, §. 194. Therefore a force sufficient for actualizing the most numerous greatest accidents suffices for actualizing the most numerous substances. Therefore [it suffices] for all, §. 191, 247. A force sufficient for actualizing something is a power (*potentia*)@@1@@. Hence omnipotence (*omnipotentia*)@@2@@ is a force sufficient for actualizing all things. God is omnipotent, §. 831.",[]],"baum:833":["Absolutely impossible things are nothing, §. 7. Therefore one who could do the absolutely impossible would be able to do nothing, §. 469. God can do all things, §. 832. Therefore God's omnipotence does not extend to the absolutely impossible. Impossible to someone is that for whose actualization his forces do not suffice, §. 469. In this sense nothing is impossible to God. Therefore every thing impossible to us and to every finite being is possible to God, §. 832.",[]],"baum:834":["Miracles (*miracula*) are possible, §. 475. God's force suffices for actualizing all possibles, §. 832. Therefore God can perform miracles in the strict sense (*miracula rigorosa*), §. 477, 833.",[]],"baum:835":["God can actualize all possible worlds, §. 832. Therefore he can actualize also the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect, without exceptions, §. 442.",[]],"baum:836":["God is a substance, §. 830, and has no modes, §. 825. Therefore he is a necessary substance, §. 202, has no internal state, §. 206, and is not modifiable, §. 209. Whence it is again clear that the world cannot be a modification of God, §. 388.",[]],"baum:837":["All origin and perishing, [that is,] origin from nothing and annihilation of a necessary substance, are absolutely impossible; God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore all origin and perishing in God is absolutely impossible, §. 227, 228.",[]],"baum:838":["Every substance is a monad (*monas*), §. 234. God is a substance, §. 830. Therefore he is a monad and a simple being (*ens simplex*), §. 230. When God's highest simplicity is posited, his being in any way composed of parts outside parts is indeed removed, §. 224, but the diversity of the many [realities] in God, §. 817, the most real, §. 807, is not removed, because even in finite things it is false that all really diverse things are placed outside one another, §. 755.",[]],"baum:839":["God is a necessary being, §. 823, 824. The determinations of every necessary being are absolutely and internally immutable, §. 132. Therefore God is absolutely and internally immutable, §. 126, 127. The same is clear in this way: if God were absolutely and internally mutable, at least some internal perfection of his could exist after another, §. 124. Hence the existence of that which could have existed before it could be removed. This is a reality internal to God, §. 37. Therefore some of God's realities could be separated from others, §. 72, which is contrary to God's highest unity, §. 821.",[]],"baum:840":["If God were composite, he would be extended, §. 241, and the force of inertia (*vis inertiae*) would have to be attributed to him, §. 832, 294. Hence he would be matter, §. 295. Therefore divisible, §. 427, and so internally mutable, §. 244, 126, which is contrary to §. 839. Therefore God is a simple being, §. 224. And since he is a substance, §. 830, he is a monad, §. 230. It is again clear that the universal materialists err, §. 395.",[]],"baum:841":["In God there are no simultaneous things placed outside one another, [that is,] no parts of his, §. 840, and hence no space either, §. 239. Therefore God is not extended, nor does he fill space in the sense in which extended things are said to fill it, §. 241.",[]],"baum:842":["God has no quantitative magnitude, §. 838, 243, although he is the greatest being, §. 161, [possessed] of the most numerous greatest realities, §. 807, 812. It is again clear that God is indivisible, and indeed absolutely so, §. 244.",[]],"baum:843":["The totality of the greatest realities that can be is the greatest degree of reality, §. 247, 248. This belongs to God, the most real being (*ens realissimum*), §. 807, 812. Therefore God is an infinite real being (*ens infinitum reale*), §. 248. The same is clear from the fact that God is a necessary being, §. 823, 258.",[]],"baum:844":["All of God's perfections have the greatest degree of their reality that they can have in a being, §. 812. Hence they too are all infinite, §. 248. To the highest reality of each of these perfections belongs agreement (*consensus*) in the most perfect being, §. 139, 140. Therefore, in whom there is one infinite reality, in him are all; in whom all, in him the highest, §. 843. Therefore, in whom there is one infinite reality, there God is, §. 811. Hence it can be gathered in what way several of God's perfections can be taken, now these, now those, as first concepts, §. 816, and why no author of evil equally powerful with God himself can be admitted outside God. Manichaeism (*Manichaeismus*) is the opinion that posits that there exists an author of evil equally powerful with God, and it is an error.",[]],"baum:845":["God is in act whatever he can be in respect of internal perfections, §. 843, 259. It is again clear that God is internally immutable, §. 252, and a necessary being, provided a circle be avoided, §. 843, 256.",[]],"baum:846":["Several gods (*dii plures*) are impossible (*impossibiles*). For if there were several, they would in part be different (*diversi*), §. 74, and hence there would be in one something that would not be in the other, §. 38. This would be either a reality (*realitas*) or a negation (*negatio*), §. 36. If it were a reality, he who lacked it would not be a god (*deus*), §. 807. If it were a negation, he in whom it was present would not be a god, §. 808. If one reality were posited as being in one, but another equivalent (*aequipollens*) reality in the other, then in neither would there be the totality of realities (*omnitudo realitatum*), and hence neither would be god, §. 807. The god whom we have so far considered, §. 811-845, is so supremely one (*summe unum*) of all the greatest and absolutely inseparable realities, §. 821, that there is not, nor can be, any other or second distinct from him; and this we have so far seen to be the case because god is necessary, §. 811-845. Therefore the god whom we have so far considered is unique (*unicus*), §. 77. Polytheism@@1@@ is the opinion that posits several gods, and it is an error. We rather venerate in god the highest uniqueness (*unicitas*), by which, as the greatest being (*ens maximum*), §. 842, he is distinguished, §. 173, from all things, even those greatest in their own kind (for example, the whole nature of the best world and the greatest spirit among finite beings), by a great many greatest differences, §. 844. This greatest difference extends to the relations themselves, so that no such and so great a relation can intervene between a thing that is not the god so far considered and a third thing, as intervenes between this god and a given third thing, §. 812, 817. By this highest uniqueness of god there is indeed removed the equality with him of any thing that is not god, even if it is otherwise greatest; and there is removed also a similarity that is not merely almost total but would abolish the infinite dissimilarity that must be posited between god and all other things, §. 844. What is not removed, however, is the plurality of greatest things within god, together with the infinite respective difference of these same things in the highest degree, §. 37, 174; rather it is posited in the infinite characters by which god is distinguished from all things, §. 67.",[]],"baum:847":["The simplicity of god (*simplicitas*) is evident once more. For if god were composite (*compositus*), his parts would be substances (*substantiae*) posited outside one another, §. 225, 282. Only one of these would be an infinite substance, §. 846. The rest, therefore, would be finite, §. 77, 248. Hence certain absolutely necessary imperfections (*imperfectiones*) would have to be posited in god, §. 250, 155. This is contrary to §. 828.",[]],"baum:848":["God has no figure (*figura*), §. 280, 847. Anthropomorphism (*Anthropomorphismus*) in its cruder form is the error of those who ascribe to god a figure, for example a human one; in its subtler form it is the error of one who ascribes to god the imperfections of finite things, for example of men, §. 828.",[]],"baum:849":["There are no successive things (*successiva*) in god, §. 839, 124. Therefore there is no time (*tempus*) in god either, §. 239. He is not in time in such a way as to constitute a part of successive things, §. 124, 837. And since coming into being and passing away are themselves absolutely impossible for him, §. 837, he has the greatest duration (*duratio*), §. 299, §. 812, which alone is really infinite, §. 844, 846. Hence he is eternal (*aeternus*), §. 302, and, coexisting with all time, sempiternal (*sempiternus*), §. 303; he was, is, and will be, §. 298. God exists (*exsistit*).",[]],"baum:850":["If a contingent (*contingens*) eternal being be posited, its eternity differs greatly from the eternity of god, §. 67. (1) Its duration is subject to continuous successions of modifications (*modificationes*), §. 209, 836. (2) Its eternity indeed has no limits of extension, but it cannot on that account yet be called really infinite, §. 259, 849. (3) Its eternity would be time without beginning and end, whence it could be called infinite, but mathematically, not therefore really, §. 248, 849; for a successive being, §. 238, is never in actuality all that it can be as regards its internal determinations, §. 259.",[]],"baum:851":["God is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 823, 824, and infinite (*infinitum*), §. 843. Hence he is a being from himself (*ens a se*) and independent, §. 310. He exists, §. 849, in such a way that he is not the thing caused (*causatum*) of another posited outside himself, but is the cause of his effects in the simple sense, §. 318. All the perfections (*perfectiones*) of god are really infinite, §. 844; hence no perfection of god is the thing caused of something posited outside god, nor can it be, §. 381, 248, 310. But a passion (*passio*) would be a predicate of god that would be the thing caused of something posited outside god, §. 210. Therefore god is wholly impassible (*impassibilis*); he is acted upon by no thing posited outside himself, neither ideally nor really, §. 212; nor does anything posited outside him act upon him, §. 210, and therefore he does not react either, §. 213. Every action of god, in general, is without reaction, ideal as well as real, §. 212.",[]],"baum:852":["Images (*imagines*)@@1@@ are (1) signs of the figure of another. Now god has no figure, §. 848. Therefore images of god in this sense are impossible, §. 347. (2) Things similar to another in a notable degree. Since every being is in some degree similar to god, §. 265, every more perfect being will be an image of god, §. 70, 811, and the more perfect it is, the more similar to god it will be, and therefore the greater an image of god, §. 265, 160.",[]],"baum:853":["The world (*mundus*) is a whole of actual finite things, §. 354. God is not a whole of actual finite things, §. 844. Therefore god is not the world: neither this world nor any world is god. The same is evident from §. 361, 823, from §. 365, 839, from §. 370, 837, and from §. 388, 843.",[]],"baum:854":["This world has an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) outside the world, §. 375, 388, and this cause is a necessary substance, §. 381, 319. Therefore a necessary substance is possible (*possibilis*), §. 333, 69. If a necessary substance is possible, it is actual (*actualis*), §. 109, and sempiternal, §. 302. Therefore a necessary substance exists. God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore god exists.",[]],"baum:855":["God is a being outside the world (*ens extramundanum*), §. 843, 388, and the world is neither an essential (*essentiale*), nor the essence (*essentia*), nor an attribute (*attributum*), nor a mode (*modus*), nor a modification, nor an accident (*accidens*) of god, §. 843. God is not the sole substance, §. 391. Theological Spinozism (*Spinozismus theologicus*) is the opinion that denies god to be a being outside the world, and it is an error.",[]],"baum:856":["As §. 811 proves the existence of god a priori (*a priori*), so §. 854 presents the same, established a posteriori (*a posteriori*), each independently of the propositions which egoism (*egoismus*), §. 392, idealism (*idealismus*), §. 402, and materialism (*materialismus*) deny, §. 395. Therefore they can be employed to convince egoists, idealists and materialists of the existence of god, even as such.",[]],"baum:857":["God is not matter (*materia*), §. 295, 841; therefore he is immaterial (*immaterialis*), §. 422. God is not a body (*corpus*), §. 296. Therefore he is incorporeal (*incorporeus*), §. 422. Therefore he is physically incorruptible (*incorruptibilis physice*), §. 746. The same is evident from §. 837.",[]],"baum:858":["The most perfect world (*mundus perfectissimus*) is the greatest image of god among finite things, §. 436, 852; in the world, among substances, the greatest image is substances, §. 400, 857; among substances, spirits (*spiritus*), §. 402, 531; among spirits, those enjoying the greatest understanding (*intellectus*), §. 637; and among those enjoying the greatest understanding, the happiest, §. 787, the furthest removed from every evil, especially moral evil, §. 788, 813, and the most holy, §. 828.",[]],"baum:859":["The nature of god (*natura dei*) (as naturing, *naturans*; cf. §. 466) is the complex of his internal perfections, §. 430, by which he is the cause of his effects in the simple sense and is impassible, §. 851. Therefore whatever is absolutely possible is physically possible for god, §. 833, and nothing is physically impossible for god that would not be impossible in itself, §. 469, 833; hence nothing is physically necessary for god that would not at the same time be absolutely necessary, and things absolutely contingent are also physically contingent for god, §. 469, 104. So all events of the world are physically contingent for him, not only the preternatural (*praeternaturales*) but also the supernatural (*supernaturales*) ones, §. 474, 475.",[]],"baum:860":["If the naturalist (*naturalista*), in the more general sense, denies all supernatural events in this world, he errs in denying their hypothetical possibility. For since miracles (*miracula*) are possible, §. 834, also by positing a connection with god outside the world, §. 855, 474, they also have extrinsic possibility, §. 16, 859.",[]],"baum:861":["Something homogeneous (*homogeneus*) to the degree of reality in god and in all his perfections is impossible, §. 843, 844, since uniqueness belongs to god and to all his perfections, §. 846, 844. Therefore the degree of reality found in god and in all his perfections cannot be understood from a homogeneous quantity assumed as a unit. Hence we can measure (*metiri*) neither god nor any of his perfections, §. 291. What we cannot measure is immeasurable (*immensum*).@@1@@ Therefore god and all his perfections are immeasurable. We understand this immensity (*immensitas*) also from the following: one who measures must have as many clear perceptions as there are parts, degrees or varieties in what he truly measures. Hence one who truly measured god, or anything internal to god, would have to have really infinite clear perceptions, which do not fall within any finite understanding, §. 844.",[]],"baum:862":["God is conceivable (*conceptibilis*) in himself, §. 632, 809, and for man, §. 804-1000, and can be defined correctly and in many ways, indeed really, §. 816. That is called comprehensible (*comprehensibile*)@@1@@ of which a full cognition (*cognitio*)@@2@@ is possible, that is, one excluding all ignorance, and hence historical, philosophical and mathematical cognition that is complete. Incomprehensible (*incomprehensibile*) to someone@@3@@ is that for the acquisition of full cognition of which his powers do not suffice; and the more it is necessary for him to fall short of full cognition of it, the more incomprehensible it is for him. The greater a thing is, the more difficult or the more impossible is its comprehension for a finite knower, §. 160, 527; hence the greatest is the most incomprehensible to all finite beings, §. 261. Deism (*Deismus*) is the opinion which posits that almost nothing about god is conceivable, except perhaps his existence, and it is an error, although god and everything in him is most incomprehensible to us and to all finite beings, §. 861, 806.",[]],"baum:863":["Distinct cognition (*cognitio distincta*) is a reality, §. 531. In god are all realities, §. 807. Therefore god cognises distinctly. Therefore he has an understanding (*intellectus*) and is an intellectual substance (*substantia intellectualis*), §. 830, a spirit, §. 402.",[]],"baum:864":["In god are all the greatest realities, §. 812. The more things are cognised, and the more distinctly, the greater the distinct cognition is, §. 634. Therefore the most distinct cognition will be the distinct cognition of all things, §. 161. Now a distinct cognition of all things is possible, §. 632. Therefore in god there is a distinct cognition of all things, and this same cognition is the most distinct.",[]],"baum:865":["The understanding (*intellectus*) of God is the highest, §. 863, 812, and immutable, §. 839. Therefore in it there are no antecedent and consequent thoughts, §. 125. It is the highest in that it represents the most and the greatest things, with the most and the clearest marks, in the strongest and most diverse combinations [*in sociis*]. Therefore the understanding of God is the most profound, the most extensive and the purest, §. 637.",[]],"baum:866":["God knows himself (*cognoscit*) as fully as can be, §. 865. Knowledge concerning God is theology (*theologia*) in the wider sense. The theology by which God knows himself is archetypal (*archetypal*, αρχετυποσ), in so far as the theologies of finite beings are to be directed towards likeness to it, §. 346.",[]],"baum:867":["God, representing all things, represents to himself all possible worlds (*mundi possibiles*), §. 864. This is the internal perfection (*perfectio interna*) of God, §. 37, and his essence (*essentia*), §. 816.",[]],"baum:868":["God knows most distinctly all the essences of all finite things, §. 864. In so far, therefore, as the essences of things are represented in the understanding of God, they depend upon it, §. 14, and are eternal in it, §. 849.",[]],"baum:869":["God represents to himself all possible worlds most distinctly, §. 867. Therefore he represents also the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect, §. 442, and this [actual world]. The world, in so far as it is represented sensitively, is sensible@@1@@ (*sensibilis*, visible); in so far as it is known distinctly, it is intelligible@@2@@ (*intelligibilis*). God knows this intelligible world most distinctly. Therefore he knows most distinctly all the monads of this world and all its souls, §. 864. One who is endowed with the most distinct knowledge of the human soul is a searcher of hearts@@3@@ (*scrutator cordium*). Therefore God is a searcher of hearts, §. 740, and sees through the representation of the sensible world in each monad and each soul, §. 400, 741, and indeed much more perfectly than a given monad or a given soul knows itself and its own representation of the world, §. 864.",[]],"baum:870":["In God there is no sensitive knowledge (*cognitio sensitiva*), §. 864, 521. Therefore he has no lower cognitive faculties, §. 520. Nothing is obscure to him, nothing confused; hence he knows no thing more clearly than another, §. 528, and he neither attends nor abstracts, §. 529, nor reflects nor compares, §. 626.",[]],"baum:871":["Since God knows all that is signified (*signata*) most distinctly, he has an intuition of all things, §. 620, 864. He knows also all signs, and all the symbolic knowledge (*cognitio symbolica*) of the souls in the world, §. 864, 869. Yet there is never in him a greater or lesser perception of the signs than of the things signified, §. 870; both are always the greatest, §. 864.",[]],"baum:872":["God represents to himself every nexus (*nexus*) most distinctly, §. 864. Therefore he has the highest reason (*ratio*), §. 640. His highest reason consists in this, that, being the highest understanding and hence immutable, he sees through the greatest nexus of the greatest number of things, without any succession of ratiocinations, §. 865, 645.",[]],"baum:873":["The knowledge of God is of the highest extension and majesty, the most exact, §. 864, and the most orderly, §. 822, without any ignorance and error, narrowness and levity, and with nothing hasty [*tumultuarii*] in it, §. 515. It is the clearest, the most distinct, of all truth, and hence the most certain. In it there is nothing obscure, nothing confused, nothing inadequate, nothing incomplete, nothing impure, nothing superficial, nothing merely probable, doubtful or improbable, and nothing dead, inert and merely speculative, §. 669, 671. He knows most distinctly all certain principles, all that is derived from them (*principiata*), and every nexus of principles and things derived; §. 864, 872. Therefore his knowledge is the highest science (*scientia*). God knows most distinctly all the ignorances and errors of souls, the narrownesses and meagrenesses of their knowledge, whatever in knowledge of that kind is gross and hasty, [all] night, confusion, inadequacy, incompleteness and impurity, all the moral certainties of souls, their probabilities, doubts, improbabilities, opinions, arguments, scruples, and inert and dead speculations, §. 869.",[]],"baum:874":["God knows, §. 873, I) all the determinations of all things, in so far as these are regarded as merely possible. This is the knowledge of simple intelligence@@1@@ (*scientia simplicis intelligentiae*).",[]],"baum:875":["God knows, §. 873, II) all the determinations of actual things, 1) of this world, by free knowledge (*scientia libera*, of vision)@@1@@: α) the past, by divine recollection@@2@@, β) the present, by knowledge of vision@@3@@, γ) the future, by foreknowledge@@4@@. Philosophical Socinianism is the opinion that denies the divine foreknowledge of future contingents, and it is an error.",[]],"baum:876":["God knows all the determinations of actual things 2) of another world than this, by middle knowledge@@1@@ (*scientia media*). For any event of this world another could exist, §. 363, 324. But each would have its own consequences, following without limit through all the states of the world, partly different [from the actual ones], §. 488. Therefore, if even for a single event of this world another had existed, this world would be partly other than it is through all the states successive to that event, indeed also the antecedent ones, §. 357, 278. Therefore whatever could have existed in place of any event in this world, with all its consequences, God knows by middle knowledge, §. 378.",[]],"baum:877":["God is always most distinctly conscious to himself of the states of the present world, §. 875. Therefore he never sleeps, §. 556. The same is evident from §. 870. He does not sense, as we do, §. 870, 544, but is wakeful, §. 552, 826, by eminence, through the knowledge of vision, §. 875.",[]],"baum:878":["God has represented the future thing to himself from eternity, as far as is possible, §. 875, 843. Therefore, when it becomes present in the world, nothing is added to the knowledge of God, §. 161, although the thing itself is changed from an object of foreknowledge into an object of vision, §. 125. God will represent the past thing to himself eternally, as far as is possible, §. 875, 843. Therefore, when the present becomes past, the thing itself will indeed change from an object of vision into an object of recollection, but nothing will be lost from the knowledge of God, §. 161. God beholds from eternity all the successive states of this world, §. 875, whence the internal immutability of the divine knowledge can be conceived, §. 839.",[]],"baum:879":["He who cannot err is infallible@@1@@ (*infallibilis*). Therefore God is infallible, §. 873. Infallibility is least if, for someone, to confuse a single least truth with a falsehood is least impossible. The more and the greater the truths that anyone is unable to confuse with falsehoods, and the greater the impossibility of such confusion, the more infallible he is. When, therefore, we ascribe to God the greatest infallibility, §. 812, we venerate him as absolutely unable to confuse very many and very great truths with any falsehood.",[]],"baum:880":["Certainty (*certitudo*) in its least degree is the least clear knowledge of a single least truth, §. 161. The more clearly, the more numerous and the greater the truths that are known, the greater will the certainty be, §. 160. When, therefore, we ascribe to God the highest subjective certainty, §. 873, 812, we venerate him as knowing the most and the greatest truths most distinctly. The same God is objectively most certain, §. 93, 812, in that his truth is the highest, §. 822, and his existence is demonstrable through his essence and through the existence of any finite thing whatever, §. 856.",[]],"baum:881":["The free knowledge of God is his relative perfection, §. 875, 815. And since it is absolutely necessary that it be most true, §. 879, it presents this world to God as existing, in and through itself, contingently, §. 361; hence that it is necessary only hypothetically is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Therefore the free knowledge of God is an analogue of a mode, §. 827.",[]],"baum:882":["Wisdom of the final nexus (*sapientia nexus finalis*) in general@@1@@ is insight into it; wisdom in the special sense@@2@@ is insight into ends, and prudence@@3@@ insight into means. Hence God is all-wisest, §. 872. When we call him all-wisest, we venerate him as seeing through 1) all ends, 2) all means, 3) all their possible nexuses, §. 343, 4) as to all qualities, 5) all quantities, 6) in the greatest possible nexus, 7) most certainly and most ardently, §. 880, 873.",[]],"baum:883":["God knows all ends, hence both the best and the worst; which of them can be a means to another; hence every possible subordination of all ends, and also every coordination, §. 315; all aims, relative [*secundum quid*] and absolutely [*simpliciter*] ultimate, §. 343, according to all their goodness and degree of goodness, and every possible nexus with means. So it is that no end ever appears to him other than it is, §. 879, while he knows at the same time how each end appears to each and all the souls that know it, §. 869. Therefore God knows best, concerning the best ends, that they are the best, §. 882.",[]],"baum:884":["God knows all means, hence also the best, §. 882, which, when posited, posit the highest perfection, §. 187. Now, when the means are posited, the end is posited, §. 326, 341. Therefore the best means are subordinated to the best end. God knows them in so far as they are subordinated to the best end, and how and to what extent they are subordinated, §. 883.",[]],"baum:885":["A certain means@@1@@ (*remedium certum*) is one that in truth is such and so great as it is known to be. It is opposed to the apparent means, which merely seems [so], and either is not at all or is not so great as it seems, and to the uncertain, whose true goodness or degrees of goodness are not evident. The best means are the most certain, both in so far as they are not apparent and in so far as God is most certain concerning their goodness and degree of goodness, §. 882, nonetheless seeing through all possible doubts of souls concerning them, §. 869, 873.",[]],"baum:886":["The more fruitful a means is, the greater the perfection posited when it is posited, §. 166, 341. Therefore the most fruitful means are the best, and conversely, §. 187. God knows the most fruitful means, §. 884, and how much fruitfulness each has, §. 882, and how much fruitfulness is ascribed to each by given souls, §. 873.",[]],"baum:887":["The best means are complete, §. 886, in relation to the best end, §. 884. Therefore they are not incomplete, §. 81. If they were superfluous, some of them would contribute nothing to the end. Therefore they would not realize the good, §. 341, and yet would not be wholly sterile, §. 23. Hence they would be evils, §. 146. Therefore the best means are adequate to the best end. God knows the means adequate to the best end, §. 884. Means that are certain and adequate are exactly attaining the end@@1@@ (*finem ex asse consequentia*). God knows the means exactly attaining the best end, §. 885.",[]],"baum:888":["The realization of an end through means that exactly attain it, and are the fewest, is the shortest way@@1@@ (*via brevissima*). The use of the best means is always the shortest way. For then the end is realized through means exactly attaining it, §. 887, each of which is most fruitful, §. 886, none superfluous, §. 887. They will therefore be the fewest that can be, §. 161. God knows every shortest way, §. 887.",[]],"baum:889":["Omniscience@@1@@ (*omniscientia*) is the knowledge of all things. God is most omniscient, §. 873. When we call him such, we venerate him as he is exhibited in §. 863-888.",[]],"baum:890":["God intuits (*intuetur*) all the perfections or imperfections of all things most distinctly (*distinctissime*), §. 889, 871. Whatever is in God is the greatest ground (*ratio*), §. 23, 812. Hence God's intuition of perfections or imperfections is supremely lively (*vivus*), §. 669, 873. Therefore pleasure (*complacentia*) and displeasure (*displicentia*) belong to God, §. 655, and in the highest degree, §. 812.",[]],"baum:891":["God's pleasure and displeasure are nothing but most true, §. 880, most distinct, §. 870, and most rational, §. 822. [God] is never totally indifferent, §. 653, nor ever partially [indifferent] toward any thing, §. 654. He has no sensitive pleasure (*voluptas*) or distaste (*taedium*), §. 656, no sensitive desire (*appetitus*) or aversion (*aversatio*), no instincts or flights, §. 677, no affects, §. 678, and no apparent pleasures or distastes, §. 655.",[]],"baum:892":["Since God intuits himself most distinctly as good alone, as the best and most holy, §. 866, 828, he draws from himself the purest pleasure, §. 661. This is God's highest acquiescence (*acquiescentia*) in himself, §. 682. Exemplar theology (*theologia exemplaris*) is most sweet, §. 866. God has mere distaste from no thing, §. 891, 661. His displeasure and all his pleasure are immutable, §. 839. Hence he knows no transitory pleasures and distastes, §. 662, and nothing is troublesome to him, §. 658. Nor does his highest displeasure obscure his highest pleasure in any way, §. 870, nor can anything outside him actuate in him any pleasure or distaste, §. 851.",[]],"baum:893":["In God there is a free knowledge (*scientia libera*) of this world, although the world could also be none, and could be another, §. 881. Yet free knowledge of this world exists in God only by the power (*vis*) of God, §. 851, 197. Therefore God determined his power to actuate the free knowledge not of no world, nor of another, but of this world. Therefore God desires (*appetit*) and has aversion (*aversatur*), §. 663. Now he does not desire or have aversion sensitively, §. 891. Yet his desire and aversion follow cognition, §. 668, 822. Therefore he wills and does not will (*vult et non vult*); he has will (*voluntas*) and nill (*noluntas*), §. 689, in the highest degree, §. 812, that is, as following most perfectly the highest omniscience, §. 668, 889.",[]],"baum:894":["A will or nill that follows the cognition of the intellect in a mathematical manner, that is, that desires in proportion to the degree of goodness distinctly cognized in the things desired, and has aversion in proportion to the degree of imperfection distinctly cognized in the things averted, is proportional to the intellect@@1@@; and if the intellect presents a thing as good or bad as it is, the will or nill is proportional to the objects@@2@@. Therefore proportionality (*proportionalitas*) belongs to the divine will and nill, §. 893, in the highest degree, §. 812, since it follows the most distinct and supremely infallible cognition of the degrees of perfection and imperfection, §. 879, 883.",[]],"baum:895":["God, while he wills, §. 393, acts, §. 210. All God's actions can depend on a sufficient internal principle in the acting God, §. 833, and do depend on it, §. 851. Therefore spontaneity (*spontaneitas*) belongs to the divine actions and to God himself, §. 704, and in the highest degree, §. 872, since he suffices for very many and very great actions, §. 706, 832. God's most spontaneous actions will be either immanent or transeunt, §. 211.",[]],"baum:896":["God is free from absolute internal coaction (*coactio*), §. 710, 702, and from physical coaction, §. 710, 859, and from external coaction, both ideal and real, §. 707, 851, and so also from moral coaction, §. 723, of motives, §. 726, [and] from [coaction] to unwilling actions through violence in a certain respect, §. 714, by allurements, threats, persuasions, dissuasions, and extortions, §. 728.",[]],"baum:897":["All actions are placed in God's power, §. 708, 859. If therefore some action and the action opposed to it, whichever it is, are each possible in themselves, §. 15, that is, if some action is contingent in itself, §. 104, and hence also has an opposed action contingent in itself, §. 104, each is placed in God's power. Therefore all actions contingent in themselves are free for God with respect to execution and indifferent as regards the exercise of the act, §. 708, because with respect to God the physical possibility of each is the highest, §. 844. Hence the actuation of this universe, or of none, or of another, was free for God with respect to execution, §. 835. It was indifferent to him, as regards the exercise of the act, whether [to actuate] the best world or the most imperfect one, and it is and will be so. §. 849.",[]],"baum:898":["Since God determines himself in acting according to his pleasure (*lubitus*), §. 893, 712, and this distinct, §. 893, he has freedom (*libertas*), §. 719, and in the highest degree, §. 812, that is, as actuating the greatest and most numerous actions according to a most distinct pleasure, §. 725. Fatalism (*fatalismus*), the opinion that removes divine freedom, is an error.",[]],"baum:899":["God wills most freely, §. 898. Therefore [he wills] the good, §. 719, 665. God has aversion most freely, §. 898. Therefore [he has aversion to] the bad, §. 719, 665. In God the volition (*volitio*) or love of the good, and the hatred or nolition (*nolitio*) of the bad, are infinite, §. 844, 1) extensively, since he loves every good and hates every bad, §. 898, 889, 2) protensively, since they are eternal, §. 849, and 3) intensively, since they are most proportional, §. 894. Divine volition or nolition, infinite extensively, since there cannot be in God a dead and merely speculative knowledge, §. 873, is directed to the objects 1) of simple intelligence (*simplicis intelligentiae*), the universals, which present notions to us, and whatever is good or bad in them, toward which God cannot be totally indifferent, §. 891, 2) of middle knowledge (*scientiae mediae*), the actual things of another universe, 3) of free knowledge (*scientiae liberae*), §. 874-876. God's will, so far as it desires the objects of free knowledge, or the actual things of this universe, is called consequent@@1@@; so far as it is directed to the universals and the actual things of another universe, it is called antecedent@@2@@, by reduction, §. 826, 695; the former is efficient, §. 671; both are efficacious, the antecedent also, not only because it is serious, but also because it is among the motives of the decree, §. 675.",[]],"baum:900":["A will is inscrutable@@1@@ whose impulsive causes are incomprehensible. Now the impulsive causes of the divine will are his own most distinct pleasure, §. 898, hence an internal perfection of God, §. 37, incomprehensible to us, §. 862. One who conceives some of God's motives in some degree of distinctness does not scrutinize the divine will, §. 862. Hence neither does one who aims at some understanding of the divine motives feign to himself that God's will is scrutable, §. 664. The more numerous the impulsive causes of a certain will, and the less fully they can be cognized, the more inscrutable the will is, §. 160. Hence when God's will is called supremely inscrutable to us, §. 812, we think 1) that very many of his motives are not fully cognized, 2) that any of them is very far from our comprehension, §. 862, 898.",[]],"baum:901":["The conformity of a free action with its ground (*ratio*), and hence with [its] law, §. 83, the moral [law], §. 723, is the rectitude (*rectitudo*) of that action@@1@@. So far, therefore, as all God's most free actions, §. 898, 725, have the highest conformity, §. 176, with the law of the best, §. 822, the moral law, §. 723, 'Let the best be freely joined to the best,' they are most right. The highest rectitude belongs to the divine will, §. 899, [and] moral holiness (*sanctitas*), §. 828, 723.",[]],"baum:902":["God's most free actions are not absolutely necessary, since their opposite is possible in itself. §. 102. All God's actions transeunt into the world, §. 854, 211, have an opposite possible in itself, §. 361. Hence none of them is absolutely necessary. And since the opposite of all God's most free actions can be actuated by God's omnipotence, §. 897, none of them, and thus no action of God upon the world, is physically necessary for him, §. 470, 859, and so none is morally necessary in the sense that through freedom the opposite of any would be rendered physically impossible for God, §. 723. Yet since God's highest freedom always determines itself most rightly, §. 901, while all his actions are morally most holy, they are morally necessary, §. 723, 724.",[]],"baum:903":["Goodness (*bonitas*, benignity)@@1@@ is the determination of the will to do good to another. A benefit (*beneficium*)@@2@@ is an action more useful to another, proceeding from goodness. The least goodness would be the least inclination or disposition of the will to confer one least good on a single least, that is, least worthy, [recipient], §. 161. Therefore the more, the more numerous, the greater the benefits, and the more numerous and worthier the recipients on whom goodness desires to confer them, the greater it is, §. 160. A benefit is a greater good. §. 336, 187.",[]],"baum:904":["God wills benefits to be conferred on others, §. 903, 899. Therefore he is benign, §. 903, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most benign, we venerate him as infinitely loving, and eternally, §. 899, and immutably, §. 839, [as] conferring the most numerous and greatest benefits on the most numerous and worthiest. §. 903.",[]],"baum:905":["Love of human beings is philanthropy (*philanthropia*)@@1@@; willing the proportionate mutual love of the beloved is zeal (*zelotypia*)@@2@@; these, together with compassion, favour, benevolence, and clemency, are universal in God and the greatest, §. 684, 904. And since constant intense love is fidelity (*fidelitas*)@@3@@, God, loving eternally, is infinitely and supremely faithful, §. 904, 812.",[]],"baum:906":["Goodness toward persons or spirits, [when] proportional, is justice (*iustitia*)@@1@@, cf. E. §. 317. The least justice would be the least goodness following the least, least clear, least certain, least lively mathematical cognition, §. 903, 161. Therefore the more intensely it loves (1), the more numerous (2) and greater (3) the benefits, and the more numerous the persons on whom it confers benefits (4) in proportion to the degree of perfection or imperfection cognized in the spirit, and the more clearly (5), the more certainly (6), the more ardently (7) that degree is cognized, the greater the justice, §. 903, 160. God is just, §. 904, 894, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most just, we venerate his highest goodness, §. 904, most proportional, §. 894, and most ready to confer the most numerous and greatest benefits on the most numerous, in accordance with a most distinct and infallible and supremely lively cognition of the degrees of perfection or imperfection to be met with in individual spirits.",[]],"baum:907":["A reward (*praemium*, remuneration)@@1@@ is a contingent good [conferred] on a person on account of a moral good bestowed. Justice in conferring rewards is remunerative (*remuneratoria*)@@2@@, which we venerate in God as the highest, §. 906, 812, since we look up to him as most ready to confer the most proportional rewards for all, even the least, moral goods of all spirits.",[]],"baum:908":["Punishment@@1@@ (*poena*) is a contingent evil (*malum contingens*) of a person on account of moral evil (*malum morale*) brought about. Both punishments and certain rewards (*praemia*) can be sufficiently conceived from the essence of sin and the nature of the one who sins, or from the essence of moral good and the nature of the one who acts well, and these will be natural (*naturalia*). Certain rewards cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another who confers them, and these are arbitrary (*arbitraria*)@@2@@. Certain punishments cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another who punishes, and these are arbitrary (*arbitrariae*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:909":["He in whom there are moral evils is a sinner@@1@@ (*peccator*). He in whom certain moral evils are not is, in respect of them, innocent in the broader sense@@2@@ (*insons*), cf. E. §. 319. A sinner, other things being equal, is not as good as an innocent person, §. 187. Therefore he is also not as much loved by the most proportionate goodness, §. 906. Therefore God wills to confer certain benefits on the innocent which he does not will to confer on the sinner, §. 904. Toward these benefits, which are not to be conferred on the sinner, God is not indifferent, §. 891; hence, in accordance with his most distinct pleasure, he is averse to conferring them on the sinner, §. 898, 669. Therefore he wills the opposite of such benefits, §. 663, 690. The opposites of benefits are evils, §. 81, 903, and they are contingent, §. 146. Therefore God wills certain contingent evils to be inflicted on the sinner on account of moral evils, i.e. punishments, §. 908.",[]],"baum:910":["Justice in inflicting punishments is punitive@@1@@ (*iustitia punitiva*) (vindictive, avenging, vindicating, *nemesis*). Punitive justice belongs to God, §. 909, and in the highest degree, §. 812, since he is most ready to punish most proportionately all the sins of all sinners, §. 906. Philosophical Dippelianism, an opinion that removes the punitive justice of God, is an error.",[]],"baum:911":["The opposite of a natural reward is a natural punishment, the opposite of an arbitrary reward is an arbitrary punishment, §. 908. Therefore the remunerative justice of God dispenses arbitrary rewards, and his vindictive justice imposes arbitrary punishments, §. 907, 909. Even natural punishments are arbitrary in respect of him without whose choice the nature of the sinner would not have a sufficient ground, §. 908. The reasoning is the same for natural rewards.",[]],"baum:912":["The complex of causes placed outside a spirit and concurring toward its prosperity or misery is good and bad fortune@@1@@ (*fortuna*); things actualized through fortune are fortuitous@@2@@ (*fortuita*), and they are contingent, §. 146, 787. Such goods conferred on a spirit on account of moral goods are rewards, §. 907, and, since they cannot be sufficiently known except from the choice of God, §. 854, they are arbitrary, §. 908. Fortuitous evils inflicted on a spirit are misfortunes@@3@@ (*infortunia*). Misfortunes inflicted on a spirit on account of moral evils are punishments, and, since they cannot be sufficiently known except from the choice of God, §. 854, they are arbitrary punishments, §. 908.",[]],"baum:913":["Miraculous (*miraculosa*) and supernatural (*supernaturalia*) rewards and punishments are possible in themselves, §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, for divine omnipotence, §. 833, 834; they are arbitrary, §. 908, 898, yet not the only arbitrary ones, §. 911, 912.",[]],"baum:914":["Whatever in an event (*eventus*) is real and positive is called the remote matter@@1@@ (*materiale remotum*) of the event; the complete determination is called its proximate matter@@2@@ (*materiale proximum*); and whatever in finite things is negative in the same is called its formal@@3@@ (*formale*). Hence evils, and therefore punishments, §. 908, are either regarded materially and formally at once, and then, like all finite things, are partly good, partly evil, taken precisely or formally, §. 264, and are loved by God insofar as they are good, and hated by God infinitely insofar as they are evil, §. 899; or they are regarded only materially, as remote matter, and then are positive and real, and only an object of divine love, §. 899; or only formally, and then they are nothing but negations strictly speaking, or privations, §. 137, and are only an object of divine hatred, §. 899; or [they are regarded] as proximate matter and the formal.",[]],"baum:915":["By punitive justice, §. 910, God wills the remote matter of a punishment, and whatever of reality there is in the proximate matter, but not the formal, §. 914. Now that the same punishments should befall this sinner and not another, but him to whom they are most proportionate, is a reality, §. 36, 909. God therefore wills this, §. 899. Hence the distinction of contingent evils, §. 146, and so of punishments, §. 908, as well as of sins, §. 788, into privative and positive, §. 525, is also to be judged [accordingly].",[]],"baum:916":["Long-suffering@@1@@ (*longanimitas*) (the patience of a judge) is justice that punishes only when the best occasion has been seen. God, infallibly knowing the best opportunities of all punishments, §. 889, 879, will most proportionately also this proximate matter of any punishment, since it is a reality, §. 36, 894, 914. Hence he is supremely long-suffering.",[]],"baum:917":["Impartiality (*impartialitas*) is aversion to deciding from apparent stimuli (*stimuli*). In God not even stimuli, §. 898, much less apparent ones, are possible, §. 889, 12; and since his most holy will is averse both to them and to a decree made from them, §. 902, he is most impartial.",[]],"baum:918":["Impartial justice is equity@@1@@ (*aequitas*). God, who is most just, §. 906, and most impartial, §. 917, is most equitable.",[]],"baum:919":["Sincerity@@1@@ (*sinceritas*) is goodness in signifying one's mind, and this is in God, §. 904, in the highest degree, §. 812, since he is most ready to signify his mind by the most fitting signs, by all those and only so many as the highest wisdom advises, §. 884-888.",[]],"baum:920":["Veracity@@1@@ (*veracitas*) is sincerity in speech. Therefore, since divine wisdom judges it best to signify the divine mind through speech, God is most truthful, §. 919.",[]],"baum:921":["The least duration (*duratio*) of the least nature would be the least life (*vita*). The greater the nature, the greater the duration, and the greater the life, until the greatest duration of the most perfect nature is the greatest life, §. 430, 161. The highest life is to be attributed to God, §. 859, 850.",[]],"baum:922":["Since the greatest life of God is absolutely necessary, §. 921, because it is his essence, §. 816, and existence, §. 823, 780, God is not only immortal, but alone has absolute immortality, §. 781.",[]],"baum:923":["With the highest metaphysical and physical perfection in God, the highest moral perfection is joined, §. 806, 859, through his highest rectitude, §. 901; being most distinctly conscious of these, §. 866, and enjoying thence the highest pleasure, §. 892, he is most blessed (*beatissimus*), §. 787.",[]],"baum:924":["God is happy (*felix*), §. 923, 787, supremely so, §. 812, since 1) all moral corruption and misery is not only absent from him, §. 813, but cannot even be in him, either physically, §. 859, or morally, §. 902; 2) independently and from himself, §. 851; 3) without any change either of the goods themselves or of the intuition of them, he is most happy, §. 839.",[]],"baum:925":["A being more perfect than another is superior to it@@1@@ (*superius*). God is the supreme spirit, §. 889, 796, a supramundane being@@2@@ (*ens supramundanum*), insofar as he has greater perfection than any whole world, even the best, §. 361, 843. And since several most perfect beings posited outside one another are impossible, §. 846, God is the absolutely highest being.",[]],"baum:926":["An efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) actualizes an effect, §. 319, 210. God is the efficient cause of this universe, §. 854. Therefore God actualized this universe either from eternity, i.e. in such a way that this world had no beginning, or in time, i.e. not from eternity, §. 10; in neither case did a part of the world pre-exist the world, §. 371, 394. Therefore in both cases the world was actualized out of nothing, §. 228, and God is he who actualized it out of nothing, §. 854. To actualize something out of nothing is to create@@1@@ (*creare*). Therefore God is the creator of this universe.",[]],"baum:927":["Creation by emanation@@1@@ (*creatio per emanationem*) would be the actualization of the universe out of the essence of God. By such a creation: 1) the world would not be actualized out of nothing, §. 926, for the essence of God is a necessary being, §. 109, 816, which is contrary to §. 926; 2) either the whole essence of God or a part of it could be changed into the universe, §. 370, which is contrary to §. 839; 3) a part of God would be posited outside God, §. 388, and God would be composite, §. 225, which is contrary to §. 838. In still more ways it is evident that creation by emanation is impossible, §. 7.",[]],"baum:928":["All monads (*monades*) of this universe are finite beings, §. 396, hence all are beings from another (*entia ab alio*), §. 308, and none can be actualized except out of nothing, §. 229, 236. Therefore all monads of this universe are created, §. 926. A being which cannot exist except through creation is a creature@@1@@ (*creatura*). Therefore all monads of this universe are creatures, and indeed creatures of God, §. 381, 854. Whatever is substantial in this universe is a creature of God, §. 396.",[]],"baum:929":["Every spirit of this universe, §. 404, all human souls, §. 744, 741, all elements of this world, are creatures of God, §. 928, 423, each actualized by simultaneous creation, §. 238, and instantaneous creation, §. 299, 228. Now the bodies of this world are aggregates of elements, §. 155, 420; hence whatever in bodies is substantial, however many parts they have that subsist through themselves, are creatures of God, §. 928.",[]],"baum:930":["What cannot exist except as the effect of another cannot endure except as the effect of another, §. 299. Hence the world and all its parts cannot endure except as beings from another, §. 928, 375. Therefore a creation that actualizes independent duration in creatures is impossible, and is not an object of divine omnipotence, §. 833. The creation of this universe is possible, §. 926, 57. Therefore no independent duration in it has been actualized through the creation of this universe.",[]],"baum:931":["Whatever does not pertain to the existence of the world and of its substantial parts is not created, §. 926. Now the essences of creatures, and hence also their metaphysical evil, §. 146, do not pertain to their existence, §. 132, 134; hence neither the essences nor the metaphysical evils of creatures are created, unless you wish all the actual determinations of a creature to be called created, contrary to the notion in §. 926.",[]],"baum:932":["That this universe should not exist, that no universe should exist, is possible in itself, §. 370, 361. All things possible in themselves are also possible to God, §. 859. Hence God could, as regards omnipotence, have not created a universe, or created another than this one, upon whose existing this one would not have existed, §. 379, 926. Yet he created this one, being impassible, §. 851, through the highest spontaneity, §. 895, by a creation free in respect of execution, §. 897. Therefore most freely, §. 898.",[]],"baum:933":["God created this world most freely (*liberrime*), §. 932. Therefore he willed (*voluit*) to create it, §. 893; he willed it effectively (*efficienter*), §. 671, and hence also fully (*plene*), because he is infallible, §. 879, and so from complete motives (*motiva completa*), and consequently. Therefore God decreed (*decrevit*) to create this world, §. 695, and indeed efficaciously (*efficaciter*) in every sense, §. 675.",[]],"baum:934":["God decreed the creation of this world, §. 933, by the most proportionate will (*voluntas proportionalissima*), §. 894. Hence he decreed the existence of this world in proportion to the degree of goodness known in it, §. 926. He did not decree the existence of any other world by the most proportionate will, §. 379. Therefore God did not know as great a goodness in the existence of any other world as he knew in the existence of this world, §. 70. Now the knowledge of God is most distinct and most infallible, §. 879. Therefore the existence of this world, preferred and chosen before all the rest, §. 697, is the best (*optima*) that the existence of a world can be, §. 187.",[]],"baum:935":["With the existence of this world posited, the highest perfection (*perfectio*) that can be in a world is posited, §. 187, 934. A world in which the highest perfection that can be in a world is posited is the most perfect, §. 185. Therefore this world is the most perfect of all possible [worlds] (*possibilium*).",[]],"baum:936":["In this world there are the most of the greatest parts and the greatest of the most parts that can be in a world, and as great a consent (*consensus*) as can be in a world, §. 436, 935. Hence this world is the greatest of worlds in extent, in duration and in intensity (*extensive, protensive et intensive*), §. 437; it is really finite, yet in such a way that it can be called infinite in many respects, §. 440; it is the best, §. 443; and in it there are the greatest universal connection (*nexus universalis*), §. 441, order, §. 444, and the greatest harmony that can be, §. 462.",[]],"baum:937":["Suppose a world wholly equal in perfection to this world: it would have to be posited outside this world, otherwise it would be a part of this world, §. 354, 155. The perfection of this world cannot be outside this world, nor could the perfection of what is perfect in a wholly equal degree be outside that thing itself, §. 194. If, therefore, such a world were possible, two perfections wholly equal to each other and outside each other would be possible, which is contrary to §. 272. Therefore the most perfect world is unique (*unicus*), §. 77.",[]],"baum:938":["We can be certain that this world is the best, §. 935, 936. Therefore this doctrine is not a philosophical hypothesis. Those who demand a demonstration of it a posteriori either understand by a demonstration a posteriori one that has an intuitive judgment (*iudicium intuitivum*) in its premises, and they have such a demonstration, §. 933, in whose premises there enters the intuitive judgment: this world exists; or they demand to experience the difference between this world and a worse one, and hence to be placed outside this world, §. 548, a marvellous ecstasy, §. 552.",[]],"baum:939":["Whatever God created, he willed to create, §. 932. Now of contingent and, specifically, moral evils (*mala*) he did not will the formal element at all, §. 914. Therefore he did not will to create it either, §. 926. God is not the creator of any contingent evil, and hence not of any moral evil considered formally.",[]],"baum:940":["An author (*auctor*)@@1@@ is the cause of a free action, and both the action and what is caused by it are effects of the author, or deeds (*facta*)@@2@@. Now God created the world most freely, §. 932. Therefore God is the author of the creation and of this world. God is the author only of those things which he wills, §. 719, 891. Now God wills the formal element of no contingent evil and of no sin, §. 914. Therefore God is the author of no contingent evil and of no sin considered formally. A moral cause in the strict sense (*causa moralis stricte dicta*)@@3@@ is an author through the free determination of another, such as one who entices, threatens, persuades, dissuades or extorts, §. 728. Now a moral cause in the strict sense is an author. Therefore God is not a moral cause in the strict sense of any sin or contingent evil considered formally.",[]],"baum:941":["One who actuates impulsive causes (*causae impulsivae*) toward a moral evil considered formally is a tempter to evil (*tentator ad malum*)@@1@@. If God were to actuate impulsive causes toward moral evil considered formally, he would become its moral cause in the strict sense. Therefore God is never a tempter to evil, §. 940.",[]],"baum:942":["The recognition of a greater perfection in someone is honour (*honor*)@@1@@. A greater honour is glory (*gloria*)@@2@@, cf. §. 684. The glory of God, therefore, is the greater knowledge of his highest perfections. The more persons there are, the more, and therefore the more clearly, the more certainly, the more ardently, the more truly, the more numerous and the greater the perfections of someone that they know, the greater the glory, §. 160. Hence the highest glory of God is the clearest, truest, most certain and most ardent knowledge, in the most persons, of the most numerous and greatest of his perfections, §. 161. The glory of God is a good, §. 866.",[]],"baum:943":["Through this world the highest perfections of God can be known more clearly, more truly, more certainly and more ardently, §. 333, 375. Therefore this world is useful (*utilis*) for the glory of God to those who are able to know it from the world, §. 336. This usefulness was actuated by the creation both of the world itself and of all the spirits in it, §. 926. Therefore the creator of the universe uses the world for the actuation of his glory, which he knows most distinctly to be good, §. 889, 942. Therefore God had an end (*finis*) in creating, §. 341.",[]],"baum:944":["God created the world according to his highest knowledge of the nexus of ends (*nexus finalis*), §. 943, and hence most wisely, §. 882. Hence in creating the best world he knew and actuated all the best ends of that world in the most perfect subordination and coordination, §. 883, their best means, §. 884, the most certain, §. 885, the most fruitful, §. 886, and those wholly in accord with the ends, §. 887, by the shortest way, §. 888, §. 894. Again it is clear that God created no contingent evil considered formally, §. 146, 914.",[]],"baum:945":["The end of God in creating the world was not some perfection internal to himself. For it is impossible that any of these be actuated or augmented through the world, §. 851, and God does not will impossible things, §. 891, 893. Now whatever is, besides God, is the world or a part of the world, §. 846, 354. Therefore the end of God in creating the world was the perfection of creatures, §. 928.",[]],"baum:946":["The end of God in creating the universe was as great a perfection of creatures as is possible in the best world, §. 945, 944. Therefore all the usefulnesses of created things, §. 336, and all their uses, §. 338, are ends of God, §. 944. The science of the divine ends in creatures is teleology (*teleologia*), both physical, of bodies, and pneumatic, exhibiting the ends of spirits.",[]],"baum:947":["The good determination of a spirit from motives of the divine glory is the illustration of the divine glory (the worship of God, *cultus dei*)@@1@@. The glory of God and its illustration are religion (*religio*). Now the glory of God is useful for his worship, §. 336, 712. Glory and worship are useful for religion, §. 336. Therefore the ends of creation were the worship of God and religion, §. 942, 946.",[]],"baum:948":["The prosperity and blessedness, or happiness, of spirits is useful for the glory of God, §. 787, 942, for its illustration and for religion, §. 947, 336. Therefore as great a prosperity and blessedness, or happiness, of spirits as is possible in the best world was an end of creation, §. 946, 942.",[]],"baum:949":["All creatures, whether they are regarded as means or as ends, are useful for the glory of God, and this for the illustration of the divine glory, §. 943, 947; hence all things allied to religion (*confines religionis*) are subordinated to it in creation, §. 315, 947. Therefore religion is the ultimate end of creation, §. 343.",[]],"baum:950":["This world endures, §. 299, but at no moment independently, §. 930. Therefore it cannot endure, even for a single moment, except as the effect (*causatum*) of something posited outside itself, §. 307. Therefore an extramundane power effects its duration at every moment of its duration, §. 210. This power is God, §. 855, 839. Therefore God effects the duration of the universe at every moment of its duration. The actuation of duration is conservation (*conservatio*)@@1@@. Therefore God is the conserver of this universe.",[]],"baum:951":["Conservation is a continuous influx (*influxus*) of God, §. 950, 895, and a real one, §. 212, because by its own existence it cannot be the effect of any finite thing, §. 308. It is the same as creation, §. 926. Hence conservation is not wrongly called continued creation.",[]],"baum:952":["What has not been created is not conserved, §. 950, 926. Therefore neither the essences of things, nor the metaphysical evil of finite things, §. 931, nor the formal element of contingent evil, nor moral evil considered formally is conserved by God, §. 939.",[]],"baum:953":["Whatever cannot exist except through creation cannot endure except through conservation, §. 950, 926. Now all the monads (*monades*) of this world, and whatever is substantial in it, are creatures of God. Therefore all the monads of the world, and whatever is substantial in this world, are conserved by God at every moment of their duration, §. 928.",[]],"baum:954":["Since all efficient causes (*causae efficientes*) besides God, namely all the substances of this world, §. 319, 846, are subordinated to God, §. 928, he is the absolutely first efficient cause and all the rest are second, §. 315, 28. Now all the actions of finite substances are at the same time passions from other finite substances influencing them, §. 451. Therefore God concurs (*concurrit*), as efficient cause, mediately with all the actions of finite substances, §. 314, 320. But since all the passions of finite substances from other finite substances are at the same time their actions, §. 463, not only when they are conceived as chiefly acting but also when they are noted as suffering, God, at the very moment at which they change, concurs immediately as efficient cause with all the actions of finite substances, by actuating through conservation the sufficient reason of this change in them, namely their power, §. 953, §. 320; for to the present existence of a finite substance belongs its action, §. 210, 55.",[]],"baum:955":["The immediate concurrence of God is his presence (*praesentia*), §. 223. Therefore God is most proximately present to all the substances of this world, §. 954. What is most proximately present to all and each of the substantial parts of something is said to be intimately present (*intime praesens*)@@1@@ to that thing itself. Now God is most proximately present to all the substantial parts of bodies in this universe, §. 421. Therefore God is intimately present to all bodies in this universe.",[]],"baum:956":["God is most proximately present to every monad of this world and intimately present to every body, §. 955, and this at every moment and with respect to all the actions of creatures, §. 954. Therefore God is most omnipresent (*omnipraesentissimus*).",[]],"baum:957":["Wherever God is, there he is whole and undivided, §. 842. Therefore he is so also as regards substance (*substantia*) and essence (*essentia*), §. 830, 816. Therefore God is present to all the monads and bodies of this world, as regards substance and essence, §. 955.",[]],"baum:958":["The conservation (*conservatio*) of the powers of this universe, whatever they may be, in the very actuality of those powers, is the physical concurrence of God (*concursus dei physicus*). Because and in so far as it extends to the individual actions of the individual substances, it is called general@@1@@ (universal).",[]],"baum:959":["All concurrence of God is perfectly free, §. 932. Therefore, if he concurred in the formal [element] (*formale*) of any evil action, he would become its author, §. 940. Therefore he concurs in all actions that are physically and morally evil as regards the material [element] (*materiale*), §. 958, but not as regards the formal, §. 940.",[]],"baum:960":["The concurrence of a moral cause, strictly so called, is moral@@1@@. God actuates motives for the worship of himself, §. 947. Therefore he concurs morally in certain actions in this universe, §. 940. The moral concurrence of God, when it is added to the general, is special@@2@@. Therefore God concurs specially in certain actions in this universe.",[]],"baum:961":["God is not a tempter to evil, §. 941. Therefore he does not concur morally in the formal [element] of any morally evil actions, §. 940. Therefore neither does he concur specially in it, §. 960.",[]],"baum:962":["The supernatural concurrence of God (*concursus supernaturalis*), when it is added to the special, is the most special@@1@@ (*specialissimus*). It is possible in itself, §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, and it is actual in this world whenever a certain action in it cannot be actuated equally well through the special concurrence of God, §. 498, 935.",[]],"baum:963":["Government@@1@@ (*gubernatio*) is the action by which several means are actuated in succession toward some further end. Therefore the better the means that are actuated, the more of the best [means], and hence of those that attain the end by the shortest way, §. 888, and the better and the more remote the ends toward which they are actuated, the more perfect is the government, §. 185. The ends which God had in creating, he has and actuates in conserving, §. 951, 944. Therefore, when we revere him as the supreme governor of the universe, we worship him who at every moment actuates the best means, and the greatest number of them, proceeding by the shortest ways and leading to the best ends and finally to the ultimate aim of this universe. When God, in governing, 1) sets a certain limit to the powers (and hence also to the actions, §. 331) of a created thing, he is said to determine it more strictly@@2@@; when 2) he subordinates to his own ends an action of a created thing which was not undertaken by it toward those ends, he is said to direct it more strictly@@3@@.",[]],"baum:964":["The first sin is called a fall@@1@@ (*lapsus*), and the power of falling, liability to fall@@2@@ (*labilitas*). This is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. The opposite of hypothetical liability to fall is confirmation in the good@@3@@.",[]],"baum:965":["Absolute liability to fall belongs to the essence of a finite spirit, §. 40, 964. Hence in such a spirit it is absolutely necessary, §. 106, a metaphysical evil, §. 146. It is not concreated with any spirit, §. 931, although its opposite could not have been concreated with any creature, §. 833. A spirit that falls acts, §. 964, 788. Therefore God concurs in the material [element] of the fall, §. 918, but in the formal neither physically nor morally, §. 909, 911. Therefore he does not become the author of the fall viewed formally, nor a moral cause strictly so called, §. 940.",[]],"baum:966":["The fall is in itself contingent, §. 964, since it is a change of a finite thing, §. 361. Therefore the opposite of the fall is in itself possible, §. 104. Therefore the prevention of the fall is in itself possible, §. 221. Therefore God could have prevented every fall, §. 833.",[]],"baum:967":["Moral prevention@@1@@ (*impeditio moralis*) is the actuation of motives toward the opposite of some change. God has actuated for spirits very many motives toward their own happiness, §. 948. The happiness of a spirit is the opposite of its fall, §. 81, 787. Therefore God prevents every fall morally.",[]],"baum:968":["Prevention which is not moral is called physical. The fall is a sin, §. 964, and therefore a moral action, §. 788, and therefore free as regards execution, §. 719, 722, and hence physically contingent, §. 709. Therefore, besides the freedom of the one who fell and the motives for it, §. 967, there were many other possible things, upon the positing of which the fall that did follow would not have followed, §. 378. These are physical hindrances of the fall, §. 81, 221. Therefore God could also have prevented the fall physically, §. 833.",[]],"baum:969":["God prevents every sin morally, §. 967; he can prevent it physically through omnipotence, §. 968. Yet he does not prevent every sin physically. Non-prevention is permission@@1@@ (*permissio*). Therefore it is either physical, §. 968, or moral, §. 967.",[]],"baum:970":["God permits no fall, §. 967, and no sin, morally, §. 969. Yet he permits certain sins in this world physically. Suppose these also to be physically prevented: then this would not be the world in which they were physically prevented, but another, §. 378, a less good one, §. 937. Therefore God physically permits certain sins in this world, because a world in which they are physically permitted is the best means of religion, §. 949, 935.",[]],"baum:971":["Right (*ius*) (taken as a quality of the person), in the wider sense@@1@@, is a moral faculty; and he who has the right to decide about some thing whatever he pleases is its full lord@@2@@ (*plenus dominus*). Full dominion over persons is full power@@3@@ (*potestas plena*).",[]],"baum:972":["God has a right to his own most upright actions, §. 901, 971. Among them is the decree concerning the world and its individual creatures, §. 934. Therefore God is the full lord of the world and of all the creatures in it. Therefore he has full power over the spirits of this world, §. 971.",[]],"baum:973":["He who has full power over a spirit has the right to bind it by obligation in whatever way he pleases. He who has the right to bind some spirit by obligation in whatever way he pleases has the right to make laws for it. For the author of the obligation which a law declares is said to make the law@@1@@. He who has the right of making laws is a legislator@@2@@. Therefore God is the legislator of all the spirits of this world, §. 971, 972.",[]],"baum:974":["He who alone has supreme might and power over several spirits is their monarch; he who has full power is their despot. Now God alone has supreme might, §. 832, and power, and that full power, §. 971, over all created spirits. Therefore his is the supreme and greatest despotic monarchy, of which all created spirits are subjects, §. 844.",[]],"baum:975":["The providence of God@@1@@ (*providentia dei*) is the action by which he bestows upon every created thing as much of goods as the highest goodness can. Therefore, by conserving, §. 950, by concurring, §. 954, by governing, §. 963, by morally preventing evil, §. 967, by not permitting it physically except what to prevent would detract from his ends, §. 970, and by giving the best laws to his state, §. 973, 974, God exercises providence, §. 903-920: through impulsive causes toward blessedness, the turning of hearts@@2@@ (*verticordia*); through chance events, holy fortune@@3@@ (*fortuna sancta*); through the goods implanted in the nature of each thing, nurturing nature (*alma natura*). Epicureanism, the opinion which abolishes the providence of God, is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:976":["The decree concerning the existence of the world is in God most free, §. 933. Hence, following the most distinct and most accurate knowledge of all the parts of this world and of all good and evil in them, §. 889, it is immutable, §. 839, eternal, §. 849, and irresistible, §. 844, 222. This can be conceived in human fashion thus: Purpose, πρόθεσις: let the complex of the best things compossible outside God exist, §. 822, 901. Foreknowledge, πρόγνωσις: this world is the best complex of things compossible outside God, §. 935. Decree, προορισμός: let this best world exist.",[]],"baum:977":["There do not exist more worlds than this one, nor more finite things than those which are parts of this world, §. 379; nor has God decreed more concerning contingent things than is comprehended in the decree concerning the existence of this world, §. 976. That decree, therefore, in so far as it extends to all the realities of this universe, is called universal; in so far as there are no more [than these], it is called unique (*unicum*), §. 77.",[]],"baum:978":["The particular decrees of God@@1@@ either are parts of the universal and unique decree, §. 977, or they would be primitive@@2@@, that is, either opposed to it or at least not comprehended under the universal. Such decrees are not in God, §. 976.",[]],"baum:979":["All the decrees of God are acts of a will perfectly proportioned to its objects, §. 894, which most perfectly follows the knowledge of the degree of perfection or imperfection in the object, whether in desiring or in turning away, §. 976. The same is also clear thus: suppose that in some decree of God the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object was in no way a motive. Then either God did not know it, which is contrary to §. 875; or he abstracted from it, which is contrary to §. 870; or there was a knowledge of that kind which was most distinct but dead, which is contrary to §. 890.",[]],"baum:980":["A decree of God is called absolute@@1@@ if neither the foreseen perfection nor the imperfection of the object was its motive; on the contrary, it is called hypothetical@@2@@ if it follows the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object. No decrees of God concerning contingent things are absolute; all are hypothetical, §. 979. Theological absolutism is the opinion which posits absolute decrees of God concerning contingent things, and it is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:981":["The decree concerning the eternal happiness of some spirit is predestination (*praedestinatio*) in the stricter sense@@1@@, since in the wider sense@@2@@ it is a decree concerning the future. The decree concerning the eternal unhappiness of some spirit is reprobation@@3@@ (*reprobatio*). Both are hypothetical, §. 980. Predestinatianism, or absolutism in conceiving predestination in the stricter sense, is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:982":["Revelation (*revelatio*), in the wider sense@@1@@, cf. §. 986, 989, is a signification of the divine mind made by God to creatures. Therefore by divine revelation creatures are taught the divine knowledge and volition, never all of it, §. 862, but always as much as has pleased providence, §. 975.",[]],"baum:983":["God is the author of universal nature, §. 940, 466. Therefore whatever a creature, through its own nature, knows of the mind of God from universal nature, it knows through divine revelation, §. 982, which is natural, §. 469. Such theology is natural theology, §. 800.",[]],"baum:984":["All finite things are means for knowing the divine mind, §. 858, 880; all the representative powers of this universe signify their creator, §. 929, as the best and wisest, §. 933. The soul itself is a representative power of its God, §. 741, 744.",[]],"baum:985":["Through the soul itself, through the individual monads to which God is present, and hence also through the individual bodies and senses of the soul, the divine mind is naturally revealed, §. 984: through the uses of individual things, the ends of God, §. 946; through knowledge of the best, his will, §. 894.",[]],"baum:986":["Revelation in the stricter sense (*revelatio strictius dicta*)@@1@@, cf. §. 982, 989, is a supernatural divine revelation made to men through speech, a most special concurrence (*concursus*) toward religion, both possible in itself and actual hypothetically in this world, whenever religion cannot be realized equally well through natural revelation alone, §. 962, 949.",[]],"baum:987":["Suppose that in this world natural revelation only were to be made to certain individuals, e.g. men; suppose that under the same circumstances a revelation to the same individuals, made supernaturally, were richer, nobler, clearer, truer, more certain and more ardent than the merely natural, §. 669. Then a revelation strictly so called can occur most fittingly to the order of this world, §. 484, and it exists in it, §. 986.",[]],"baum:988":["Suppose that some signification of the divine mind in this world is wholly conducive to good religion, §. 947, but cannot be realized naturally, though it can be realized supernaturally; then in this world a revelation strictly so called will take place, §. 496, 987.",[]],"baum:989":["Revelation strictly so called, of things naturally unknowable to creatures, is revelation in the strictest sense (*revelatio strictissime dicta*)@@1@@, cf. §. 982, 986. Therefore whatever knowledge of the divine mind in this world, wholly conducive to religion for certain creatures, is naturally unknowable, is realized in this world through revelation in the strictest sense, §. 988.",[]],"baum:990":["If something is held to be a revelation of God strictly so called, and yet neither promotes the glory of God and religion more, nor supplies a richer, nobler, clearer, truer, more certain or more ardent knowledge of God than the natural does, it is not a revelation of God strictly so called, §. 986, 495.",[]],"baum:991":["In the mind of God there is no contradiction, §. 822. Therefore whatever truly contradicts natural revelation is not a revelation of God strictly so called, §. 983, 986, nor in the strictest sense, §. 989.",[]],"baum:992":["Revelation of God, strictly and in the strictest sense so called, does not contradict the natural, §. 991, and hence not reason taken objectively, so far as it is known by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 646, 984.",[]],"baum:993":["Sacred faith taken objectively (*fides sacra obiective sumpta*)@@1@@ is the sum of things to be believed on revelation strictly so called, just as the faith held in revelation strictly so called is sacred faith taken subjectively@@2@@. Therefore not only things wholly and naturally unknowable to creatures belong to sacred faith, §. 989, 986.",[]],"baum:994":["Sacred faith, taken objectively, so far as it is believed by man through faith taken subjectively, does not contradict reason taken objectively, so far as it is known by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 992, 993.",[]],"baum:995":["Revelation strictly so called signifies what is supremely possible, §. 986, 822. Now what is against reason is impossible, §. 643. Therefore neither revelation of God strictly so called, nor in the strictest sense, §. 989, nor sacred faith taken objectively, is against reason. But what is truly against reason is neither revelation of God strictly so called, nor in the strictest sense, nor sacred faith taken objectively.",[]],"baum:996":["Sacred mysteries (*mysteria sacra*)@@1@@ are things in sacred faith, taken objectively, that lie above the reason of creatures. In every revelation in the strictest sense there are mysteries, §. 989, 644. Therefore they are not repugnant to revelation strictly so called, §. 989, nor are they against reason, §. 643.",[]],"baum:997":["In all his revelation God is supremely sincere, §. 919; hence, since his wisdom judges speech to be the most apt sign for signifying the divine mind to men, and judges it to be realized supernaturally, he is most truthful in revelation strictly so called, §. 920.",[]],"baum:998":["In revelation taken strictly and in the strictest sense God is a witness, §. 986, 946, supremely sincere and most skilful, §. 997, 889, and hence most worthy of belief; his revelation strictly so called gives the highest certainty to faith.",[]],"baum:999":["A naturalist in the stricter sense (cf. §. 493) is one who denies that there is in this world a revelation of God strictly so called. An atheist (theoretical), who denies the existence of God, errs, §. 811, 854. Naturalism in the neutral sense necessarily posits atheism, §. 493.",[]],"baum:1000":["A naturalist in the stricter sense, who denies that revelation strictly so called exists in this world, 1) because it is a supernatural event, §. 986, and hence seems to him impossible, errs, §. 860; 2) because he thinks sacred faith is against reason and hence impossible, errs, §. 995; 3) because he denies providence toward the human race, errs, §. 975, through his own fault, §. 982, 986, ignorant of the most benign God, §. 903, \"whose honour, name and praises shall ever remain.\"",[]]};
